nificant differences in average number of total publications, first-author publications, first-author publications in ophthalmology, or book chapters ( Table 3 ). Table 3. Research output for applicants by gender Total (n=1376) Men (n=937) Women (n=539) p-value Published Research Articles Total Number of Applicants Listing Publications 1107 (80.45%) 473 (50.49%) 451 (83.67%) Average Number of Publications (SD) 3.31 (3.64) 3.20 (3.58) 3.50 (3.74) 0.14 Average Number of First-Author Publications (SD) 1.30 (1.95) 1.27 (1.85) 1.35 (2.09) 0.43 Average Number of Publications in Ophthalmology (SD) 1.87 (3.20) 1.73 (2.96) 2.08 (3.54) 0.05 Average Number of First-Author Publications in Ophthalmology (SD) 0.86 (1.73) 0.80 (1.58) 0.96 (1.93) 0.09 Presentations Total Number of Applicants Listing Presentations (Poster, Oral, Abstracts) 1195 (86.85%) 506 (54.00%) 474 (87.94%) Average Number of Presentations (SD) 4.26 (3.10) 4.09 (3.08) 4.52 (3.12) 0.01 Book Chapters Total Number of Applicants Listing Book Chapters 121 (8.79%) 47 (5.02%) 74 (13.73%) Average Number of Book Chapters (SD) 0.16 (0.85) 0.15 (0.87) 0.17 (0.83) 0.70 Open in a new tab A total of 134 (75.7%) self-identified URiMs listed at least one research publication, compared to 770 (80.5%) of non-URiMs. There were no significant differences in average number of total publications, first-author publications, publications in ophthalmology, first-author publications in ophthalmology, presentations, or book chapters by self-identified URiM status ( Table 4 ). Table 4. Research output for applicants by self-identified underrepresented in medicine status Total (n=1134) Did Not Identify as URiM (n=957) Self-Identified URiM (n=177) p-value Published Research Articles Total Number of Applicants Listing Publications 904 (79.72%) 770 (80.46%) 134 (75.71%) Average number of publications (SD) 3.33 (3.75) 3.39 (3.77) 3.02 (3.62) 0.235 Average Number of First-Author Publications (SD) 1.32 (2.00) 1.36 (2.06) 1.06 (1.65) 0.061 Average Number of Publications in Ophthalmology (SD) 1.86 (3.26) 1.87 (3.25) 1.77 (3.32) 0.696 Average Number of First-Author Publications in Ophthalmology (SD) 0.87 (1.79) 0.91 (1.83) 0.65 (1.53) 0.078 Presentations Total Number of Applicants Listing Presentations (Poster, Oral, Abstracts) 981 (86.51%) 819 (85.58%) 162 (91.53%) Average Number of Presentations (SD) 4.21 (3.12) 4.19 (3.16) 4.29 (2.85) 0.701 Book Chapters Total Number of Applicants Listing Book Chapters 100 (8.82%) 84 (8.78%) 16 (9.04%) Average Number of Book Chapters (SD) 0.17 (0.91) 0.15 (0.83) 0.20 (1.27) 0.612 Open in a new tab URiM = Underrepresented in Medicine We used a multivariate model to examine what factors were associated with having at least one first-author publication in any field. Gender and self-identified URiM status had no effect on the likelihood of having a first-author publication. However, applicants with other advanced degrees (OR: 1.836, 95% CI 1.315–2.564, p<0.001), and applicants who took a research year (OR: 2.682, 95% CI 1.824–3.943, p<0.001) were more likely to have at least one first-author publication in any field. Applicants who attended a U.S. medical school were less likely to have at least one first-author publication in any field (OR: 0.070, 95% CI 0.008–0.583, p=0.014). Other factors such as attending a top 10 medical school, AOA status, and USMLE Step 1 score had no association with having at least one first-author publication in any field ( Table 5 ). Table 5. Multivariate logistic regression of factors associated with listing at least one first-author publication in any field on ophthalmology residency applications Variable Effect Odds Ratio 95% CI p-value Gender Men vs. Women 0.993 0.750 – 1.316 0.961 URiM Status Yes vs. No 0.787 0.517 – 1.198 0.263 U.S. Medical School Yes vs. No 0.070 0.008 – 0.583 0.014 Top 10 Medical School Yes vs. No 0.927 0.594 – 1.446 0.738 AOA Yes vs. No 1.156 0.710 – 1.882 0.560 Not Yet Determined vs. No 1.225 0.864 – 1.736 0.254 Other Advanced Degrees Yes vs. No 1.836 1.315 – 2.564 <0.001 USMLE Step 1 Score 1.011 0.999 – 1.023 0.063 Research Year Yes vs. No 2.682 1.824 – 3.943 <0.001 Open in a new tab Statistical significance indicated in bold URiM = Underrepresented in Medicine AOA = Alpha Omega Alpha USMLE = United States Medical Licensing Examination We next looked to see what factors were associated with having at least one publication in specifically ophthalmology. Self-identified URiMs were less likely to have at least one publication in ophthalmology (OR: 0.650; 95% CI 0.425–0.995, p=0.047), as were applicants from a U.S. medical school (OR: 0.066, 95% CI 0.008–0.534, p=0.011) and applicants from a top 10 medical school (OR: 0.593, 95% CI 0.378–0.931, p=0.023). On the other hand, applicants who took a research year were more likely to have at least one first-author publication (OR: 2.100, 95% CI 1.456–3.027, p<0.001). Gender and other factors such as AOA status, USMLE Step 1 score, and having other advanced degrees had no association with having at least one publication in ophthalmology ( Table 6 ). Table 6. Multivariate logistic regression of factors associated with listing at least one publication in ophthalmology on ophthalmology residency applications Variable Effect Odds Ratio 95% CI p-value Gender Men vs. Women 0.868 0.657 – 1.145 0.320 URiM Status Yes vs. No 0.650 0.425 – 0.995 0.047 U.S. Medical School No vs. Yes 0.066 0.008 – 0.534 0.011 Top 10 Medical School Yes vs. No 0.593 0.378 – 0.931 0.023 AOA Yes vs. No 1.194 0.740 – 1.923 0.468 Not Yet Determined vs. No 1.236 0.873 – 1.749 0.233 Other Advanced Degrees Yes vs. No 0.960 0.693 – 1.330 0.806 USMLE Step 1 Score 1.003 0.993 – 1.013 0.572 Research Year Yes vs. No 2.100 1.456 – 3.027 <0.001 Open in a new tab Statistical significance indicated in bold URiM = Underrepresented in Medicine AOA = Alpha Omega Alpha USMLE = United States Medical Licensing Examination Finally, we examined what factors were associated with having at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology. Self-identified URiMs were less likely to have at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology (OR: 0.570, 95% CI 0.350–0.929, p=0.024), as were applicants from a U.S. medical school (OR: 0.181, 95% CI 0.049–0.666, p=0.010). Applicants who took a research year were more likely to have at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology (OR: 2.209, 95% CI 1.521–3.209, p<0.001). Gender and other factors such as attending a top 10 medical school, AOA status, USMLE Step 1 score, and having other advanced degrees had no association with having at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology ( Table 7 ). Table 7. Multivariate logistic regression of factors associated with listing at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology on ophthalmology residency applications Variable Effect Odds Ratio 95% CI p-value Gender Men vs. Women 0.840 0.623 – 1.133 0.254 URiM Status Yes vs. No 0.570 0.350 – 0.929 0.024 U.S. Medical School Yes vs. No 0.181 0.049 – 0.666 0.010 Top 10 Medical School Yes vs. No 0.690 0.424 – 1.124 0.136 AOA Yes vs. No 1.121 0.666 – 1.889 0.667 Not Yet Determined vs. No 1.172 0.801 – 1.715 0.414 Other Advanced Degrees Yes vs. No 1.027 0.727 – 1.451 0.879 USMLE Step 1 Score 1.008 0.996 – 1.020 0.208 Research Year Yes vs. No 2.209 1.521 – 3.209 <0.001 Open in a new tab Statistical significance indicated in bold URiM = Underrepresented in Medicine AOA = Alpha Omega Alpha USMLE = United States Medical Licensing Examination Discussion Diversity in the workforce continues to remain a problem in medicine, with ophthalmology being no exception. To create a balanced workforce that reflects the demographics of the U.S. population, systematic changes will need to be implemented from all levels. To this end, the recruitment of diverse trainees into ophthalmology residency programs will be an important target to foster change. Because research productivity is a part of the residency application that can be used to evaluate applicants, it is necessary to assess any underlying disparities that may disadvantage minorities. In this study of research listed on ophthalmology residency applications, we found that women had a higher average number of publications in ophthalmology and presentations when compared to men. Additionally, compared to non-URiMs, self-identified URiMs were less likely to have publications in ophthalmology and first-author publications in ophthalmology. An encouraging finding from this study was that the research productivity of women was not inferior to that of men. Our multivariate model showed that there were no significant differences by gender in the likelihood of having first-author publications, publications in ophthalmology, or first-author publications in ophthalmology. In fact, we found that women had significantly higher average publications in ophthalmology and presentations. While these findings could suggest that women need an increased research productivity compared to men in order to achieve similar match rates, women have a higher match rate. According to released 2019 residency match data, women had a 71% match rate compared to 62% in men. 12 While women had more publications in ophthalmology and presentations compared to men, the corresponding higher match rate suggests that women are not held to a higher research productivity standard to attain similar match outcomes. Though there is little data about research output among residency applicants in ophthalmology, several studies have examined the relationship between gender and research productivity in other fields of medicine with mixed findings. One study of dermatology residency applicants found that successful male applicants published more research articles before residency than successful female applicants. 13 On the other hand, other studies in neurosurgery 14 and radiation oncology 15 found that there was no difference by gender in publishing output among residency applicants. Research productivity specifically among ophthalmology residency applicants remains largely unstudied. However, one study among ophthalmology residents found that there was no difference in research productivity by gender. 16 The different findings among these studies could be attributed to the differences between each specific field, or differences in study design or sample size. However, the majority of these studies found that research productivity of women was either equivalent to or greater than that of men, which may signify a shift away from the history of male dominance in academia. Our study supports these findings. Our results are particularly encouraging given the gender inequities historically present in academic ophthalmology. Women in ophthalmology are currently underrepresented in senior academic positions and leadership positions. 17 , 18 Studies have shown that the number of research publications prior to residency is associated with increased research productivity in residency and beyond, 19 and that research productivity is associated with academic rank and promotion in ophthalmology. 17 As such, it is encouraging that we found women to have a similar if not higher research output to men. Similar levels of research productivity among aspiring ophthalmologists may translate into greater representation for women in academic ophthalmology in the future. Our findings suggest that the current efforts to remove barriers for women in scholarly research activity have been effective in generating change. On the other hand, our findings regarding the research output for self-identified URiMs were much more discouraging. Our study showed that self-identified URiMs were less likely to have at least one publication in ophthalmology and at least one first-author publication in ophthalmology. However, there were no significant differences in research output by self-identified URiM status. Additionally, according to the national 2019 ophthalmology residency match data, URiMs had a 60% match rate compared to 66% in non-URiMs, though it is unknown whether this difference is significant. 12 Combined, these findings highlight the persistent disparities that still exist for URiMs, and call for increased efforts that specifically support URiMs. Racial and ethnic disparities in research productivity specifically in ophthalmology remains largely unstudied, though a few similar studies exist in other fields. One study in plastic surgery found that there were no differences in research productivity between URiM versus non-URiM trainees during all stages of training. 20 This study also found that URiM status was not a significant independent predictor of any category of training publications. Another similar study in radiation oncology also found that there were no differences in pre-residency research productivity by URiM status. 21 While the findings of our study highlight a racial disparity among ophthalmology residency applicants, more research in all fields are needed to examine the effects of race in residency and beyond. With the change of USMLE Step 1 to a pass-fail grading system, residency selection committees will need to be intentional in how they use other application metrics like research output to evaluate applicants. Our findings suggest that quantitatively using research output for ophthalmology residency applications could unintentionally disadvantage URiMs. URiMs may have limited access to research opportunities, particularly in ophthalmology, or may lack the mentorship, time, or resources to complete a project to publication. Research opportunities and funding are often concentrated at major academic centers that have an abundance of resources and faculty dedicated to scholarly activity. Particularly in a field like ophthalmology where smaller institutions may not even have an ophthalmology program, URiMs may face even larger barriers. As such, URiMs may be disadvantaged by evaluations that focus more on quantity of research publications, as opposed to a more holistic view that evaluates an applicant’s growth and learning from research experiences. This study was limited as it was a retrospective study that examined the results at a single institution. In particular, because of the heavy research emphasis of our institution’s program, the research output of our applicant pool may not be representative of that of other programs, limiting the generalizability of our study. However, there were a total of 649, 677, and 677 applicants who participated in the ophthalmology match in 2019, 2020, and 2021 respectively, indicating that our sample captured the majority of applicants each cycle (~70%). Additionally, not all applicants self-reported URiM status on their application, which was a further limitation of the study, though these applicants were excluded from our multivariate model. However, we were able to capture a large sample of URiMs (177 applicants) over three cycles of applications, and the overall percentage of self-identified URiMs in our applicant pool (12.86%) does reflect the percentage of URiM students enrolled in U.S. medical schools (13.6%), suggesting that URiM candidates were not deterred from applying to our program. It is of note that, while 12.86% of applicants to the Wilmer Eye Institute self-identified as URiM, 7.7% of ophthalmology residents nationwide are URiMs. It is unclear why the number of self-identified URiM applicants may be higher, or whether this is significant. It is possible that our residency program, geographical location, or community may attract more self-identified URiM applicants. In conclusion, our findings suggest that while progress has been made in addressing gender inequities in ophthalmology research, current efforts for URiMs are far from adequate. Focused efforts that continue to target women and especially URiMs will be necessary to help address these inequities. Many institutions have research fellowships specifically for women and URiMs, and these fellowships have been successful in improving research productivity among participants. The expansion and widespread implementation of these types of programs would likely aid in the efforts to address the current inequities. Additionally, the creation of more institutional and individual grant initiatives could also encourage minorities to become more involved in research. Finally, it will be crucial for institutions to set up robust mentorship programs for minorities. Studies have shown that individuals from underrepresented minority groups often receive less mentoring support, 22 , 23 and having at least one mentor has been found to increase scholarly productivity. 24 Because research productivity is strongly associated with academic career advancement, supporting research productivity among URiMs and women would help to increase their representation on all levels. As we continue to move toward a balanced workforce, ophthalmology residency programs should be cognizant of disparities in research productivity of minorities. We highly encourage a holistic review of applicants that assesses academic rigor, motivation, passion, personality, and other skills. Working toward a diverse ophthalmology workforce is a necessary priority that will be part of the solution to addressing health disparities affecting the U.S. Given the prevalent disparities in eye care, efforts are needed to increase the level of diversity among ophthalmology residents, trainees, physicians, and leadership. Increased implementation of mentorship opportunities and programs specifically targeting the recruitment of minorities will be crucial to reduce disparities in ophthalmology. A more diverse profession requires the effort of everyone in ophthalmology including its physicians, leaders, medical institutions, private practices, and mentors. Acknowledgments and Financial Disclosure This study was supported by the National Eye Institute, Bethesda, Maryland (Wilmer Biostatistics Core Grant P30EY01765). None of the following authors has any conflicts of interest to disclose. Footnotes Meeting Presentations: Women in Ophthalmology 2022 Summer Symposium, Monterey, CA Association of University Professors of Ophthalmology 2023 Annual Meeting, San Diego, CA Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References 1. Komaromy M, Grumbach K, Drake M, et al. The role of black and hispanic physicians in providing health care for underserved populations. N Engl J Med. 1996;334(20):1305–1310. doi: 10.1056/NEJM199605163342006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Moy E, Bartman BA. Physician race and care of minority and medically indigent patients. JAMA. 1995;273(19):1515–1520. doi: 10.1001/jama.1995.03520430051038. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Simon AE, Marsteller JA, Lin SX. Physician-patient race concordance from the physician perspective. J Natl Med Assoc. 2013;105(2):150–156. doi: 10.1016/s0027-9684(15)30110-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Reede JY . A recurring theme: The need for minority physicians. Health Aff (Millwood). 2003;22(4):91–93. doi: 10.1377/hlthaff.22.4.91. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Association of American Medical Colleges. Diversity in medicine: Facts and figures 2019. 2019. 6. Xierali IM, Nivet MA, Wilson MR. Current and future status of diversity in ophthalmologist workforce. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2016;134(9):1016–1023. doi: 10.1001/jamaophthalmol.2016.2257. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Lin F, Oh SK, Gordon LK, Pineles SL, Rosenberg JB, Tsui I. Gender-based differences in letters of recommendation written for ophthalmology residency applicants. BMC Med Educ. 2019;19(1):476–6. doi: 10.1186/s12909-019-1910-6. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Bradford PS, Akyeampong D, Fleming MA2, Dacus AR, Chhabra AB, DeGeorge BRJ. Racial and gender discrimination in hand surgery letters of recommendation. J Hand Surg Am. 2021;46(11):998–1005.e2. doi: 10.1016/j.jhsa.2021.07.009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Polanco-Santana JC, Storino A, Souza-Mota L, Gangadharan SP, Kent TS. Ethnic/racial bias in medical school performance evaluation of general surgery residency applicants. J Surg Educ. 2021;78(5):1524–1534. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2021.02.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Boatright D, O’Connor PG, E Miller J. Racial privilege and medical student awards: Addressing racial disparities in alpha omega alpha honor society membership. J Gen Intern Med. 2020;35(11):3348–3351. doi: 10.1007/s11606-020-06161-x. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Venincasa MJ, Steren B, Young BK, et al. Ophthalmology residency match in the covid-19 era: Applicant and program director perceptions of the 2020–2021 application cycle. Semin Ophthalmol. 2022;37(1):36–41. doi: 10.1080/08820538.2021.1906917. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Association of University Professors of Ophthalmology. Gender and ethnicity data for ophthalmology residency 2019. 2019. 13. Zheng DX, Kalra MK, Narang J, et al. Gender trends in research output among matched dermatology residency applicants. J Am Acad Dermatol. 2022;86(5):e213–e214. doi: 10.1016/j.jaad.2021.09.073. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Sheppard JP, Lagman C, Nguyen T, et al. Analysis of academic publishing output among 1634 successful applicants in the 2011–2018 neurosurgery residency match. J Neurol Sci. 2021;420:117186. doi: 10.1016/j.jns.2020.117186. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. McClelland S3, Mu