explore the integration of generative AI, specifically large language models (LLMs), in ophthalmology education and practice, addressing their applications, benefits, challenges, and future directions. Design: A literature review and analysis of current AI applications and educational programs in ophthalmology. Methods: Analysis of published studies, reviews, articles, websites, and institutional reports on AI use in ophthalmology. Examination of educational programs incorporating AI, including curriculum frameworks, training methodologies, and evaluations of AI performance on medical examinations and clinical case studies. Results: Generative AI, particularly LLMs, shows potential to improve diagnostic accuracy and patient care in ophthalmology. Applications include aiding in patient, physician, and medical students’ education. However, challenges such as AI hallucinations, biases, lack of interpretability, and outdated training data limit clinical deployment. Studies revealed varying levels of accuracy of LLMs on ophthalmology board exam questions, underscoring the need for more reliable AI integration. Several educational programs nationwide provide AI and data science training relevant to clinical medicine and ophthalmology. Conclusions: Generative AI and LLMs offer promising advancements in ophthalmology education and practice. Addressing challenges through comprehensive curricula that include fundamental AI principles, ethical guidelines, and updated, unbiased training data is crucial. Future directions include developing clinically relevant evaluation metrics, implementing hybrid models with human oversight, leveraging image-rich data, and benchmarking AI performance against ophthalmologists. Robust policies on data privacy, security, and transparency are essential for fostering a safe and ethical environment for AI applications in ophthalmology. Keywords: Generative AI, Large Language Models (LLMs), Ophthalmology Education, Artificial Intelligence (AI) Background: overview of generative AI Artificial intelligence (AI) has been receiving increasing attention in medicine, and a diverse range of applications of AI in ophthalmology has been reported in the literature, including the use of AI in an assistive capacity as clinical diagnostic aids (e.g., diagnosis and classification of corneal ulcers from photographs, detection of diabetic retinopathy and differentiation of optic neuropathies from color fundus photos) as well as in therapeutic monitoring and quantification of disease severity (e.g., estimation of glaucoma disease progression from visual field data). 1 Within the realm of AI, generative AI has generated great excitement over the past several years; the two most widely used generative AI models are generative adversarial networks (which can create new data including images) and large language models (LLMs) (which can generate human-like narrative text). In this review, we will focus on LLMs given their prominence in current discussions centered on generative AI. LLMs are a type of generative AI that is capable of generating novel sequences of words after being trained on text data. 2 They leverage deep learning neural networks and can be used for various natural language processing tasks, including comprehending and generating human-like text in response to free-text queries. 3 One popular LLM is ChatGPT (Generative Pre-trained Transformer); the most current version is GPT-4, which has the new capability of accepting both image and text inputs. 4 Moreover, developments in LLMs comprise a rapidly evolving area. Recently, some LLMs are now able to browse the internet in real-time, which is especially important as up-to-date knowledge is critical for providing quality medical care. 5 Furthermore, in November 2023, GPTs became customizable, where users can now instruct GPTs to answer questions using certain methods as well as to use information from predetermined sources to reduce the generation of false information. 6 LLMs have a wide range of applications in medicine ( Fig. 1 ), including clinical applications such as administrative tasks (e.g., writing discharge summaries, medical notes, patient clinical letters). 3 , 7 Other clinical applications include generating differential diagnoses and answering patient messages. 3 , 7 – 10 Furthermore, LLMs have applications in research, including summarization and synthesis of articles as well as initial writing of articles. 11 Moreover, in the research space, LLMs have been used to analyze protein structure data that can be represented in text form. 12 In ophthalmology in particular, some applications have included diagnosing glaucoma based on clinical case reports, 13 responding to questions about myopia, 14 and constructing ophthalmic discharge summaries and operative notes. 15 Other potential applications of LLMs in ophthalmology include enhancing the patient experience by remotely triaging patients and responding to a range of questions—from common symptoms like blurred vision and eye redness to specific eye diseases such as cataracts and glaucoma, as well as streamlining medical record documentation by generating notes from recorded patient encounters. 16 Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Applications of Generative AI in Medicine. Besides clinical applications, there is also increasing interest regarding how LLMs might be leveraged for educational applications. However, this is a relatively less well-studied area. Education is critical for providing a foundation for the future ophthalmology workforce, especially given the anticipation that AI will play an increasing role in clinical practice and society at large. Use of LLMs in medical education is a relatively nascent field, and findings regarding both successes and challenges will be informative for future curricular development. In this review article, we will focus specifically on the applications of LLMs in ophthalmology education, including physician/clinical education, patient education, as well as the design of curricula and existing AI/data science educational programs in the United States. Physician/clinician education In the realm of education, there has been recent growth in demonstration projects showcasing the applications of LLMs in physician/clinician education, spanning from undergraduate medical education to graduate medical education and post-residency/fellowship ( Fig. 2 ). First, studies have shown that generative AI can score highly on standardized medical examinations. 17 Without specialized training, ChatGPT-3 performed at or near the passing threshold for the three written United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) exams, 2 where the Step 1 exam is typically taken by medical students after their pre-clinical didactic curriculum is completed, the Step 2 Clinical Knowledge (CK) exam is usually taken by medical students after completing core clinical rotations, and the Step 3 exam is generally taken by physicians during their first year of postgraduate medical education. Furthermore, ChatGPT was able to generate answer explanations with significant insights, which has the potential to augment human learning by offering knowledge pearls and modeling deductive reasoning to test takers. 2 Moreover, ChatGPT-3.5 has even been applied to the premedical education level as one study evaluated the chatbot on the Medical College Admission Test (MCAT) and found that it performed at or above the median performance of student test takers, while also generating insightful explanations. 18 This highlights two potential applications of ChatGPT and other LLMs in education: (1) Low-cost access to personalized explanations of exam questions for students from all socioeconomic backgrounds; (2) Ability to generate additional test questions, allowing for their use as a “Socratic tutor.” 3 , 18 Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Studies about LLMs’ use for educational applications across the training continuum. For ophthalmology in particular, LLMs such as ChatGPT have been tested on question banks used to prepare for the Ophthalmic Knowledge Assessment Program (OKAP), an in-service exam taken by ophthalmology residents every year, and Written Qualifying Exam (WQE), commonly known as the “written boards” for qualification for board certification by the American Board of Ophthalmology. One study found that ChatGPT-3.5 correctly answered 46 % of questions from the OphthoQuestions practice question bank, a commonly used resource consisting of practice questions of similar style and content to the OKAP and WQE. 19 Another study found that ChatGPT-3.5 Plus had a 59.4 % accuracy on questions from the Basic and Clinical Science Course (BCSC) Self-Assessment Program and a 49.2 % accuracy on the OphthoQuestions question bank (compared with an average of 74 % and 63 % for humans). 20 The study’s authors also found that the GPT’s strongest subjects were fundamentals, general medicine, and cornea, which they attributed to the greater amount of training data available for these topics. In contrast, the weakest subjects were neuro-ophthalmology, oculoplastics, and clinical optics. 20 Not only has ChatGPT been studied, one study also tested Bing Chat, another LLM which incorporates a more recent internet search. 21 They tested the LLMs on the BCSC Self-Assessment Program and found that they performed more poorly on image interpretation compared to single-step reasoning questions. 21 Furthermore, the authors documented AI hallucination (the generation of false information) and non-logical reasoning and found that this occurred 18 % of the time for ChatGPT-4.0 and 25.6 % of the time for Bing Chat. 21 These incidences of hallucination and non-logical reasoning are misleading to humans and can impact clinical decision-making. For example, the authors found that ChatGPT mistakenly chose trabeculectomy instead of an aqueous shunt as the treatment for medically uncontrolled neovascular glaucoma. 21 AI hallucinations and nonlogical reasoning can also impact diagnostic evaluation, such as in the case of ChatGPT recommending CT or MRI imaging for loose and stretchy eyelids rather than a sleep study to evaluate for floppy eyelid syndrome and obstructive sleep apnea. 21 Overall, compared to general medical standardized exams, LLMs currently do not perform as well on ophthalmology-specific standardized exams; accuracy levels are subpar compared to human performance and mediocre at best, which could be due to the lack of domain-specific training data. Generative AI has also been investigated in the context of maintenance of certification for practicing physicians. Although to our knowledge there have not been generative AI studies for the American Board of Ophthalmology’s Continuing Certification program, there have been studies in other specialties. One study evaluated the performance of AI chatbots in the American Sleep Medicine Certification Board Exams and found that GPT-4 passed five out of ten exam categories from self-assessment questions developed by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine. 22 Another study tested GPT-4 on the American Shoulder and Elbow Surgeons Maintenance of Certification; although human significantly outperformed GPT-4, GPT-4 was still able to answer 60.2 % of the questions correctly (compared to an average of 75.3 % for humans). 23 This raises concern regarding the integrity of online assessments, especially the maintenance of certification exams, which are often administered in a self-paced, remote format. 24 The goal of maintenance of certification exams is to reinforce concepts for practicing physicians. Their open-book nature encourages reflective learning, but the use of ChatGPT or other LLMs could undermine the assessments’ validity, 25 and have implications for the return to fully in-person examinations. Besides standardized medical examinations, in terms of diagnostic capabilities, previous studies have demonstrated the ability of LLMs to have some capacity to solve clinical case vignettes. One study used GPT-4 to diagnose New England Journal of Medicine (NEJM) case challenges and found that GPT-4 correctly diagnosed 57 % of cases (compared to 36 % of cases diagnosed correctly by the journal readers), highlighting the potential of generative AI as a diagnostic aid. 26 However, one limitation was that the GPT-4 version used in the study could not analyze images, so an image description was provided instead. 26 Since the publication of that study, GPT-4 with Vision (GPT-4 V) has been released, which allows for the analysis of images and texts together. Furthermore, the study only tested GPT-4’s capability to answer multiple-choice questions. 26 In contrast, another study evaluated the reasoning skill of GPT-4 in open-ended clinical ophthalmology cases, in which the authors tested GPT-4 on 422 Journal of the American Medical Association Ophthalmology Clinical Challenges. 27 Milad et al. prompted the model to open-endedly determine the diagnosis, as well as to identify the next step, which was a multiple choice question. 27 GPT-4 was accurate on average 48 % of the time for diagnosis and 63 % of the time for the next step question, which underperformed compared to senior residents. 27 Kanjee et al. also tested GPT-4 on NEJM clinicopathologic conferences without multiple-choice options. 28 They found that the model’s top diagnosis agreed with the final diagnosis for 39 % of the cases, while the differential included the final diagnosis in 64 % of cases. 28 However, this study did not delve into the models’ potential biases, blind spots, or line of reasoning, 28 which was addressed by Jin et al. who evaluated GPT-4 V’s rationales when solving NEJM Image Challenges, including ophthalmology cases. 29 They found that although GPT-4 V had similar multi-choice accuracy compared to physicians (81.6 % vs 77.8 %), the model had flawed rationale in 35.5 % of the cases that were answered correctly. 29 Furthermore, physicians were still superior with open-book tools, especially with hard questions, and GPT-4 V had the most difficulty with image comprehension questions. 29 Other studies have also shown GPT’s underperformance on image analysis, including Mihalache et al. who investigated ChatGPT-4’s performance in processing ophthalmic imaging data by using 136 ophthalmic cases with 448 images, including various imaging modalities (e.g., OCT scans, fundus images, retinal nerve fiber layer OCT analyses, Humphrey visual fields, fundus autofluorescence) from OCTCases. 30 They found that the chatbot answered 70 % of the multiple choice questions correctly, but the chatbot performed better on questions that did not directly require image interpretation. 30 In addition to its ability to take medical exams and answer clinical case vignettes, LLMs could have an impact by offering personalized learning experiences for medical trainees. One benefit of LLMs is their ability to sustain real-time conversations, which could allow for personalized dialogues regarding medical cases that can be tailored to each student’s unique learning style, strengths, and needs. 31 Furthermore, LLMs have been proposed to enhance problem-based learning in medical education. 32 In problem-based learning, students analyze a case by identifying learning objectives in areas of growth, performing individualized self-directed learning, then reconvening as a group to discuss the learning objectives. 33 LLMs could aid in this process by developing cases (e.g., chief complaints, patient history, test results, etc.), providing feedback on students’ learning objectives and potential solutions to case problems, as well as generating socratic lines of questioning to further student learning. 32 This form of questioning could potentially augment or even replace the current role of standardized patients, which are relatively costly and difficult to scale. In ophthalmology in particular, one use case of personalized learning is a custom GPT “EyeTeacher,” an active learning tool that enhances the process of learning clinical guidelines. 5 Sevgi et al. provided clinical guidelines on diabetic retinopathy and angle closure glaucoma management to “EyeTeacher” and instructed the GPT-4 to be an interactive, active learning tool and to develop multiple-choice questions from the uploaded guidelines and provide explanations for the answers. 5 The GPT-4 would also ask the user for their level of experience, and users were able to direct their own learning by asking for different levels of question difficulty and selecting particular topics to focus on, similar to the experience of working with a personal tutor. 5 This metacognitive approach to learning could also be applied to other scenarios such as users uploading their own notes and asking the GPT to generate questions from the notes to test their knowledge. 5 One could also envision the use of this tool by educators to assist in the development of curricular materials and/or assessments. However, custom GPTs still have limitations, including the potential to generate erroneous content; furthermore, students may need to be aware of their own weaknesses in order to probe the GPT to focus on particular topics. 5 Furthermore, AI can potentially enhance physician and clinician capabilities by analyzing patient data, such as past medical history, to provide personalized recommendations. This enables healthcare professionals to offer more informed and tailored advice, ultimately improving patient care. 34 Generative AI synthesizes complex patient histories to support comprehensive decision-making, which is especially crucial for managing chronic and complex cases. By consolidating, integrating, and analyzing patient histories efficiently, AI can potentially aid clinicians in making timely, accurate decisions. This has a dual benefit in both clinician and patient education and underscores the transformative potential of AI in healthcare, benefiting professionals and patients alike. Overall, foreseeable applications for enhancing education in ophthalmology include offering answer explanations to standardized medical examinations, generating questions for active learning, incorporating clinical guidelines, simulating real-word cases as well as providing personalized recommendations and supporting comprehensive decision-making for improving patient care. However, there are persistent concerns about issues such as hallucinations resulting in inaccurate answers or explanations, academic integrity, and challenges with image comprehension. More research is needed to establish the strengths, limitations, and utility of these novel tools in educational settings, particularly for specialized domains such as ophthalmology education. Patient education In addition to AI’s potential in enhancing ophthalmology education among physicians, use of generative AI can also help with patient education. Patients have already been relying on the internet to find medical information about their conditions and have traditionally used free websites like Wikipedia, UpToDate and WebMD. 35 However, unlike these static sites, generative AI technology like ChatGPT has the potential to offer more dynamic responses using the most current information while tailoring its responses to specific queries by the patients. 36 There have been several studies on using ChatGPT for answering frequently asked questions by patients regarding different conditions or procedures. 36 – 39 One study on ChatGPT’s responses to 100 commonly asked questions regarding hypertension and its secondary complications found that ChatGPT answered appropriately in 92.5 % of cases with a reproducibility score of 93 %, exhibiting remarkable accuracy and potential for use. 37 Another study on the appropriateness and readability of ChatGPT-4’s responses for surgical treatment of retinal disease found that although responses were consistently appropriate, answers were difficult to read for the average lay person, and a college degree would be required to understand the material. 38 An extension of this application of AI would be using it as a virtual triage assistant where patients input symptoms and are informed about possible diagnoses and guidance on the urgency of seeking care. 40 AI is still in the early phases of its full utilization for medical diagnosis. However, increasing data is emerging to support the application of AI in diagnosis. 41 Recent studies in ophthalmology and other medical fields including internal medicine and emergency medicine have shown that AI virtual triage assistants are significantly superior to online symptom checkers and are able to provide patients with triage information that is comparable in accuracy and safety to that of human doctors. 40 , 42 – 44 AI has the potential to revolutionize patient care, but several challenges must be addressed to fully realize this potential. For an AI-based healthcare system to be successful, it is essential to build a relationship founded on trust between patients and the AI. 41 Research on whether people prefer AI over human healthcare practitioners has shown mixed results, depending on the context, type of AI system, and participants’ characteristics. Some surveys indicate that people believe AI has the potential to improve their healthcare and are generally willing to use AI for health-related purposes. 45 However, other studies reveal that people still prefer human healthcare interactions over AI, especially for complex or sensitive issues. 46 These findings highlight the need to carefully integrate AI into healthcare, ensuring it enhances rather than diminishes the patient-physician relationship. Another example of AI potentially improving patient outcomes by being a part of the patient care team and supporting patients is AI usage for Motivational Interviewing (MI). Patient outcomes in ophthalmology significantly depend on adherence and patient participation, especially in chronic conditions with complex treatment regimens like glaucoma. One method that has been shown to improve medication adherence is MI. MI helps patients express and resolve their feelings about a behavior and promotes the discovery of their intrinsic motivation. 47 However, MI faces implementation challenges in busy clinics due to time constraints and insufficient billing structures. Recently, LLMs have been proposed as a novel solution, offering chatbot-driven MI to enhance patient adherence in diseases like glaucoma. 48 Another way that AI can help in empowering patients is by analyzing patient data such as past medical history and providing personalized recommendations, potentially enabling them to better comprehend their health status and make well-informed decisions regarding their care. 49 An example of such AI use is using AI-powered remote monitoring systems, which track patients’ vital signs and other health data to detect potential risks, including the early onset of chronic diseases or the probability of developing particular conditions. 50 Li et al.’s review of applications of AI on diabetes education and self-management explores AI’s potential applications in lifestyle education, glucose monitoring, insulin dosing, and tracking diabetes complications. AI-powered virtual assistants can assist patients by reminding them to take their medications, scheduling doctor appointments, and tracking vital signs. 41 In addition to using AI tools for improving patient’s health literacy and supporting their informed decision-making process, physicians and health systems can use generative AI for creating patient education materials, for example in the forms of discharge or after-visit summaries and post-operative instructions. 51 , 52 Studies have revealed that the current quality of written discharge information is often inadequate, with up to 88 % of discharge instructions being incomprehensible to the patients they are intended for. 53 Early studies have shown increased patient satisfaction when using AI software to translate medical notes into plain-language notes. 54 Generative AI can also be used in creating more interactive patient education materials, for example by providing post-operative instructions in the form of a video. 55 In a study by Adeboye et al. on the use of AI avatar-generated videos in post-operative management of patients undergoing breast surgery, 79 % of patients stated preference for the video content and 86 % of patients had a better understanding of post-operative wound management after watching the videos. 55 With the use of AI, these videos can be seamlessly generated in multiple languages, potentially providing more equitable healthcare services. Regarding interpretation, AI can also play a pivotal role in language translation and providing equitable care as a real-time, automated translator improving accessibility of interpretation. 56 While AI translation tools can provide quick and accessible language services, they often fall short in complex or sensitive healthcare scenarios and can miss context-specific subtleties such as non-verbal cues and cultural contexts. 57 Studies have highlighted that non-English-speaking patients are at a higher risk of receiving suboptimal care when ad-hoc interpreters or AI-based tools are used instead of professional interpreters. 58 , 59 Overall, generative AI can be effective in direct patient education by increasing patient’s health literacy through helping patients understand their diagnosis and treatment options, answering patient’s questions, and aid in triaging patients. On a deeper level, AI can potentially be used to monitor a patient’s symptoms, track their adherence, and provide encouragement and feedback throughout a patient’s treatment plan. Physicians and healthcare systems can also take advantage of AI tools to provide more interactive and easily understandable resources for patient education. Fig. 3 summarizes potential applications of AI in patient education. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Applications of Generative AI in Patient Education. However, it should be noted that real-world deployment of these tools remains limited. Most of the applications referenced have been developed in the realm of research investigation and limited to feasibility evaluations, but few have been utilized in real-world implementation settings. Implementing AI in healthcare involves significant technical and logistical challenges. Furthermore, people’s trust and acceptance of AI can vary based on factors such as age, gender, education level, cultural background, and prior experience with technology. 60 , 61 Additional concerns about patient data sharing and safety, the impact on the patient-provider therapeutic alliance and patient trust, and the possibility of algorithmic bias are some of the challenges faced in the real-world deployment of AI tools. 62 Curricular design relating to AI Given the application of generative AI on physician/clinician and patient education in ophthalmology, as well as AI’s widespread use in medicine in general, it is vital to have a structured and validated curricular framework that can be utilized to teach the next generation of clinicians about the applications and limitations of AI. Curricular frameworks serve as educational roadmaps, guiding the development of syllabi by detailing objectives, outcomes, content, assessment methods, and teaching methodologies. 63 , 64 For example, a curricular framework on quality improvement and patient safety training was created for graduate medical education during residency training. 65 Among different medical specialties, radiology and pathology have made the most progress in developing curricular frameworks for integrating AI into residency training. 66 – 68 The American College of Radiology (ACR) developed a free, vendor-neutral framework that serves as a platform for democratizing AI for all individuals and institutions. Among its core capabilities, ACR Connect provides educational resources, tools for dataset annotation, model building and evaluation, and an interface for collaboration and federated learning across institutions, all without requiring data to be moved off hospital premises. 69 ACR AI-LAB ™ offers radiologists tools designed to help them learn the basics of AI and participate directly in the creation, validation and use of healthcare AI. 70 In pathology, to address the need for informatics training in residency programs, Association of Pathology Chair, Association for Pathology Informatics and the College of American Pathologists collaboratively developed and released the Pathology Informatics Essentials for Residents (PIER). 71 PIER offers a thorough and structured approach for educating pathology residents with essential informatics knowledge and skills. It features an adaptable resource guide and toolkit tailored to seamlessly integrate informatics training into residency programs, regardless of their varying needs, sizes, settings, and resources. The program is linked to Milestone SBP7 of the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) milestones, which covers competencies related to clinical informatics in anatomic and clinical pathology. 68 , 71 Similar to the radiology curriculum components, the PIER program covers foundational AI algorithms, integrating secondary lessons like basic mathematics, and enhancing practical skills through hands-on exercises and real-world applications. Topics included are informatics in pathology practice, information systems fundamentals, fundamentals of databases, introduction to data standards, and data availability and security. Instructional methods involve didactic sessions, concurrent journal clubs discussing relevant literature, simplified study guides, and pre- and post-lecture questionnaires to assess confidence and understanding. 68 , 71 Several national and federal regulatory bodies have acknowledged the need for integrating AI into medical education curricula. 72 An AI curriculum for medical schools, residencies, and fellowships should provide information to help trainees to develop the ability to appropriately utilize AI clinically, understand how to interpret AI results, and explain AI results to patients and other healthcare providers. 72 , 73 As suggested by Valikodath et al., a core AI curriculum in ophthalmology should include recognizing major AI studies and discoveries, understanding AI’s limitations, and exploring its clinical applications. It should start with basic mathematics and statistics, followed by the fundamentals of AI, machine learning, and deep learning. 72 The course should teach methods to evaluate AI studies using resources like “An Ophthalmologist’s Guide to Deciphering Studies in Artificial Intelligence” 74 and “A Clinician’s Guide to Artificial Intelligence: How to Critically Appraise Machine Learning Studies”. 75 Once the foundation is set, key articles and studies in ophthalmology subspecialties should be introduced, highlighting AI’s strengths and limitations. The curriculum should also cover the use of AI in clinical practice, telemedicine, and virtual visits. For those seeking deeper knowledge, a specialized curriculum with hands-on activities using automated deep learning (DL) platforms should be considered, as shown by Faes et al., demonstrating that physicians can use DL algorithms for medical imaging tasks without prior coding experience. 76 These automated DL platforms can offer a practical, hands-on AI experience, further supporting the democratization of AI and empowering a broader range of individuals without extensive programming or coding expertise to participate in AI development and application. 77 Additionally, a core or specialized AI curriculum should incorporate key principles such as ethical implications, medicolegal aspects, health disparities, and the humanization of medicine. This curriculum can be progressively implemented from medical school through fellowship, with each stage focusing on different aspects. 72 Given the significant changes required for designing and implementing such a curriculum, several barriers are faced in this process. These include the low priority often assigned to AI instruction, the limited number of faculty and trainees with adequate math and computer science backgrounds, insufficient funding, and already full schedules leaving little time to add more content. 78 Integrating AI into medical education requires support from major organizations and collaboration among programs for uniform curriculum adoption. Partnerships with entities like the American Academy of Ophthalmology and multidisciplinary collaboration across departments such as bioinformatics, bioengineering, computer science, and statistics are crucial for designing and implementing a comprehensive AI curriculum. 72 Table 1 summarizes proposed components of a curriculum for teaching AI in medical education. Table 1. Components of an AI curriculum for Medical Education. Components of an AI Curriculum for Medical Education Core Objectives: To develop the ability to utilize AI clinically To understand and interpret AI results Key Principles: Ethical implications Medicolegal aspects Health disparities Humanization of medicine Suggested Curriculum Structure: Basic mathematics and statistics Fundamentals of AI, machine learning, and deep learning Methods to evaluate AI studies Open in a new tab Currently there are no accreditation requirements related to covering fundamental concepts or familiarization with AI in graduate medical education. 63 However, the ACGME has issued a revised set of program requirements for graduate medical education in ophthalmology that encompasses seven themes, one of which focuses on education about emerging technologies. The list of emerging technologies included: “telemedicine, electronic health records, and the use of data standards”, “benefits, limitations, and ethical implications of artificial intelligence”, and “application of biostatistics/informatics, and implementation of science and cybersecurity”. 79 , 80 The revision however notes that finishing ophthalmology residency will not be enough for mastering these competencies and must only serve as a foundation for training ophthalmologists of the future. It is noteworthy that a recent survey by Stanford Medicine, published as the Stanford Medicine 2020 Health Trends Reports, revealed that 44 % (230 out of 523) of physicians and 23 % (48 out of 210) of medical students and residents reported that their education had not adequately prepared them for new technologies in healthcare. 81 Several ophthalmologists, however, are double-boarded in both ophthalmology and clinical informatics, thanks to the established clinical fellowship program in clinical informatics (CI). 82 – 84 Clinical Informatics Fellowships, approved by the aforementioned ACGME in 2014, span two years and are open to physicians from any medical specialty, providing broad exposure to clinical informatics across various departments. Upon completion, fellows are eligible for the Clinical Informatics Boards. Starting in 2026, board certification in clinical informatics will require the completion of an ACGME-accredited fellowship. Additionally, there are elective rotations and training programs available for residents and medical students in clinical informatics. To our knowledge and based on a search of the current literature as of June 2024, there are currently no medical schools with formal required courses on AI in healthcare. Many, however, do offer optional electives or modules. We will discuss examples of these elective courses in the next section of the review on AI and data science education programs. The American Medical Association (AMA) in the United States, the National Health Service in the United Kingdom, and the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons in Canada were the first national medical associations to have recommended policies for integrating AI into their respective medical educational institutions. 63 The AMA has put out several reports on the landscape view of AI in today’s healthcare system. 85 In 2018, the AMA adopted its first policy on augmented intelligence, emphasizing the need for research into integrating augmented intelligence into medical education. 86 The policy outlines several important paths to support this integration, including the development of AI education modules for medical students and physicians, the inclusion of AI-related content in accrediting and licensing standards, and the encouragement of specialty-specific educational modules. 87 AMA has also proposed ChangeMedEd initiative where they feature modules on AI in healthcare and use of generative AI in medical education training. 88 The Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC) has also been involved in supporting the integration of AI into medical education through various initiatives. They have an ongoing webinar series, “AI in Academic Medicine,” which explores critical concepts, the current landscape, and practical strategies for AI integration. 89 AI has the potential to enhance the efficiency, quality, and personalization of training in ophthalmology education. By leveraging machine learning algorithms and large datasets, AI can provide real-time feedback and personalized learning experiences tailored to the needs of individual trainees. AI-driven platforms can simulate complex clinical scenarios, allowing trainees to practice and refine their skills in a risk-free environment. Furthermore, AI can analyze performance data to identify areas for improvement and recommend targeted educational resources, thus optimizing the learning process. These advancements may improve the quality of training and facilitate ophthalmologists’ preparedness to meet the evolving demands of patient care. 72 , 90 , 91 Overall, despite national initiatives by various organizations to incorporate AI into medical education, there remains a lack of well-defined curricular frameworks and minimum teaching requirements on AI integration in medical education. Examples of existing AI and data science educational programs In this section, we will review examples of AI and data science educational programs relevant to clinical medicine and ophthalmology. To maintain a manageable scope, we are focusing on programs located in the United States. While we will provide a few examples of institution-specific initiatives, our review will mostly focus on national initiatives. This is not an exhaustive or comprehensive list but serves to illustrate the range of educational programs that have been developed in response to the growing recognition of the need for training in this domain. Stanford Medicine and the Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai are examples of institutions that are integrating AI technologies into their medical training and research programs. Mount Sinai’s Center for Ophthalmic Artificial Intelligence and Human Health focuses on leveraging AI for advanced vision care, training the next generation of eye care professionals to help bring AI and its rapidly growing family of technologies and capabilities into the ophthalmic mainstream. 92 Additionally, clinical and research fellowships are expected to be offered in the future. These programs highlight the growing emphasis on AI in medical education, aiming to equip healthcare professionals with cutting-edge tools to improve patient outcomes. Louis Pasquale, a Co-Director of the Mount Sinai’s Center for Ophthalmic Artificial Intelligence and Human Health believes that “having a fellowship in AI will be important to attracting the next generation of leaders, particularly the computer scientists”. 93 Another example of a structured AI/data science educational program is offered by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) Common Fund’s Bridge to Artificial Intelligence (Bridge2AI) Initiative. The mission of Bridge2AI is to propel biomedical research forward by setting the stage for widespread adoption of artificial intelligence that tackles complex biomedical challenges beyond human intuition. 94 Through this initiative, four data generation projects were funded nationwide, each with associated training programs. One of the NIH data generation projects is called the Artificial Intelligence Ready and Equitable Atlas for Diabetes Insights (AI-READI). 95 This project aims to create and share a flagship ethically-sourced dataset on type 2 diabetes, optimized for future AI/ML analysis. The goal is to provide critical insights, particularly into the salutogenic pathways from diabetes to health. The long-term objective is to develop a foundational, unbiased dataset to map T2DM development and reversal towards health. This project is particularly relevant to ophthalmology given the inclusion of retinal imaging data with several imaging modalities using instruments from different manufacturers, and the project leadership includes several ophthalmologists. 96 . AI-READI has instituted a yearlong research internship program designed to offer immersive training for individuals aiming to work at the intersection of biomedicine and AI. 97 This comprehensive program begins with a two-week data science and programming bootcamp, followed by a yearlong curriculum featuring didactic lectures and mentored research. Interns are guided by AI-READI investigators and engage in diverse aspects of the project, such as creating datasheets to describe AI-READI data, mapping project data to common data models, exploring standards for OCT imaging metadata, conducting data quality assessments, and analyzing equity and ethical issues. Future iterations of the program will involve interns in the direct use of data and the development of subsequent AI algorithms. The inaugural cohort for the 2023–2024 academic year includes 10 interns, with 70 % women, 30 % Black, and 50 % from underrepresented backgrounds as defined by NIH criteria. The program is committed to ongoing outreach and recruitment efforts and aims to share its successful strategies to benefit similar initiatives. 98 The American Medical Informatics Association (AMIA) is a community committed to the vision of a world where informatics transforms people’s care. Over the last 35 years, the use of informatics has grown exponentially to improve health and to make better healthcare decisions. Today, informatics is the key to accelerating the current goals of healthcare reform. AMIA’s 10 × 10 program utilizes curricular content from certified informatics training programs and other AMIA educational initiatives. Topics range from introductory health informatics courses to more specialized courses focusing on nursing informatics and clinical decision support. These courses are ideal for those looking to advance their healthcare profession with informatics training. 99 , 100 The American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) has introduced new content on artificial intelligence (AI) in ophthalmology in the Basic and Clinical Science Course (BCSC) textbooks addressed to residents specializing in ophthalmology. 101 These updates include information on the latest AI applications, such as diagnostic tools and treatment planning, and ethical considerations surrounding AI use in clinical practice. The new textbook chapter on Digital Ophthalmology also covers electronic health records, databases (e.g., the Intelligent Research In Sight [IRIS] Registry), AI, and teleophthalmology topics. 102 The AAO has also established official policies on the use of AI, 103 which are regularly discussed in forums and educational sessions. These resources ensure that ophthalmologists can access current knowledge and guidelines on integrating AI into their practice, enhancing patient care and maintaining ethical standards. The National Library of Medicine (NLM) supports biomedical informatics and data science research training at 18 U.S. institutions through its T15 program. This program offers graduate and postdoctoral education in various informatics areas, including healthcare, clinical, public health, and consumer health informatics. Trainees gain skills in biomedical data concepts and methods as well as develop skills needed to lead independent future research. Efforts are made to recruit individuals from diverse and underrepresented backgrounds. Prospective trainees can contact T15 program institutions directly for details on eligibility, selection, and support. 104 Several ophthalmologists have undergone NLM training to better understand how informatics integrates with ophthalmic practice and research, with one of the most notable examples being Michael Chiang, MD, the current Director of the National Eye Institute. 105 Examples of medical schools offering elective AI courses for medical students include the University of Florida School of Medicine which offers an optional longitudinal Research and Discovery Pathways Program that focuses on AI in medicine. 106 They offer a series of lectures and seminars covering the distinctions between data, information, and knowledge, the design of data structures, and the history and paradigms of AI. They also include “learning Python for AI applications”, coding skills, and methodologies like machine learning. Additionally, a capstone project is part of the program requirements. 106 The University of British Columbia has also been offering a 5-week/10-hour “Introduction to Medical AI” workshop targeted towards medical students since 2019. 107 Their lectures are designed for those with beginner-level technical proficiency and first-year undergraduate mathematics knowledge. Their curriculum covers biostatistics and machine learning for medical data and AI devices, including data collection, model development, regulatory approval, and advanced topics like neural networks. Practical AI development in clinical settings is emphasized, with case studies and project feedback reinforcing key concepts. 108 In an end-of-course survey of their pilot study of 25 students, overall learning experience was rated as 4.5 out of 5 with students agreeing that the course significantly enhanced their understanding of ML concepts. 109 Harvard Medical School also offers an open-enrollment, two-month online program titled ‘AI in Healthcare: From Strategies to Implementation’, designed to equip healthcare leaders with strategies for designing and implementing AI-driven solutions. 81 , 110 There are other self-paced open-to-public online courses focused on the integration of AI in healthcare offered by academic institutions like Stanford, Duke, and the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign. 111 – 113 Issues and key concerns regarding LLM/ generative AI use Here, we will review some key issues and concerns related to the use of generative AI. These are important to include in any educational curriculum concerning generative AI to ensure clinicians are well-informed of their implications. Hallucinations/clinical inaccuracies Despite their promising potential, existing LLM applications face several challenges that hinder their readiness for clinical deployment. One of the identified limitations of large language models (LLMs) is known as ‘hallucination” or “AI misinformation”. 114 Instances of “hallucinations” have been widely reported, where inaccurate information is invented (as it is not represented in the training dataset) and espoused lucidly; an alternative term such as ‘fact fabrication’ is preferred to avoid inappropriate anthropomorphism. 3 , 115 , 116 In a systematic review by Wong and colleagues, the use of large language models (LLMs) in ophthalmology was analyzed. The review found that a significant number of articles (27 out of 31, or 87 %) expressed concerns about the potential for LLMs to generate AI misinformation. 117 This misinformation is typically identified only when an ophthalmology specialist evaluates the output based on their clinical knowledge and experience. 117 These clinical inaccuracies raise concerns that users will be misled and suffer avoidable harm. Until these applications are properly engineered and validated in appropriate settings, they cannot be recommended by clinicians. 11 Inconsistent accuracy and a lack of uncertainty indicators necessitate caution with deployment. 118 Curricula should emphasize the importance of critically verifying the outputs of generative AI models rather than accepting them unquestioningly. Several authors have noted that ChatGPT tends to cite works that do not actually exist. 116 , 119 – 122 Six studies have systematically investigated the problem, 123 – 128 and among them, 51 % of the 732 citations (bibliographic references cited at the end of each paper) were fabricated. 122 Therefore, it is crucial to verify the sources generated by ChatGPT using PubMed and other reliable resources. Lack of recency Training datasets are insufficient to ensure that generated information is accurate and useful. One reason for this is a lack of recency: GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 (ChatGPT’s backend LLMs) were trained mostly using text generated up till September 2021. 4 As research and innovation continuously advance in various fields, including medicine, the absence of up-to-date content can exacerbate inaccuracies. This issue becomes particularly problematic when language evolves rapidly, such as when researchers introduce new terminology or alter the usage of existing words to describe recent discoveries and methodologies. 3 Two new medications in ophthalmology recently approved in 2023, namely pegcetacoplan (Syfovre) 129 and avacincaptad pegol (Izervay), 130 which are dedicated to slowing down the progression of advanced dry age-related macular degeneration, also referred to as geographic atrophy, would not be suggested as an output if treatment of dry AMD was queried using LLMs. If similar events exceed the training dataset’s cutoff date, models will inevitably produce poor-quality responses to related queries. Therefore, consulting healthcare professionals remains essential in the context of patient education. Consequently, curricula should emphasize the importance of verifying AI-generated content against up-to-date, reliable sources. Lack of interpretability Another issue is the lack of interpretability which results from the “black box” nature of LLMs that renders the decision-making process opaque. 131 Generated responses often lack supporting citations or information sources unless explicitly requested. This issue is exacerbated by the tendency to produce fabricated and inaccurate yet plausible-sounding responses, which undermines their credibility. In the healthcare domain, this can be particularly harmful, as patients may be misled by inaccurate medical advice. Explainable AI initiatives aim to enhance interpretability, yet research in LLMs is still in its early stages. Current techniques in machine learning often fall short of fostering genuine trust, as they are insufficient to ensure complete transparency and reliability. 3 Enhancing interpretability can be achieved using Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting, which encourages chatbots to explain their reasoning processes along with providing final answers. For example, a CoT prompt might ask a chatbot to outline a differential diagnosis for this patient’s symptoms using step-by-step reasoning like an expert ophthalmologist. 132 Human expert annotations of these CoT explanations in medical question-answering tasks have shown that most responses exhibit sound reasoning, knowledge recall, and comprehension of the question and context. 11 , 133 Teaching best practices for crafting clear and precise prompts is crucial. This includes understanding how to frame questions to elicit the most accurate and relevant outputs from AI models. Effective prompts should be specific, unambiguous, and contextually relevant. For instance, in a medical context, specifying symptoms, patient history, and desired information can lead to more accurate responses. 134 , 135 In the context of medical professionals and health care in general, prompt engineering could encompass decision support, for instance. Medical professionals can use prompt engineering to optimize AI systems to aid in decision-making processes, such as diagnosis, treatment selection, or risk assessment. In training and education, prompts can be engineered to facilitate the education of medical professionals, including ongoing training in the latest treatments and procedures. 136 In research scenarios, prompts can be crafted to assist in tasks such as literature reviews, data analysis, and generating hypotheses. 6.4. Ethical/safety concerns/biases/discrimination Another limitation of LLMs is their tendency to mirror biases present in the data they are trained on. Unstructured data, such as fundus photographs, can encode factors like age, sex, and race, potentially leading LLMs to draw conclusions based on inappropriate assumptions. 137 This can perpetuate biases and inaccuracies, reinforcing socioeconomic stereotypes and negative generalizations about minorities in ethnicity, religion, and gender. 138 Therefore, curricula should teach methods for detecting biases in AI models and use case studies to illustrate how biases can manifest in AI outputs. Curriculum creators should incorporate ethical guidelines for AI development and deployment, including transparency, accountability, and fairness, and discuss the role of ethical AI in building trust and ensuring equitable outcomes. 139 Moreover, it is crucial to discuss in such curricula the importance of diverse and representative training datasets and to regularly update the AI/ML models with new data to maintain relevance and accuracy. An example would be introducing continuous learning frameworks that allow models to adapt to new information over time. 140 Additionally, LLM applications pose risks to patient safety when misused to spread misinformation or extract confidential patient information. Any identifiable healthcare information must be protected and used in compliance with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA). 141 This regulation was established before electronic health records (EHRs) became prevalent. 141 , 142 As of December 2023, ChatGPT is not HIPAA-compliant and cannot handle protected health information, whereas Med-PaLM 2, an LLM by Google, does support HIPAA compliance. 143 AI algorithms can infer, predict, and identify patients from digital health data. 144 Despite HIPAA compliance, patient privacy remains a concern in the AI ecosystem due to extensive electronic information sharing. One solution is to use LLMs within health system firewalls instead of public APIs, which was employed in a recent real-world implementation pilot of using GPT to respond to patient portal messages in EHRs. However, this requires significant IT infrastructure and investment, which may not be feasible in lower-resourced settings, potentially increasing inequity. Trainees, physicians, and healthcare workers should be educated about LLM and generative AI policies, including the importance of not sharing clinical data with LLM interfaces. They should also be taught the importance of protecting sensitive data, such as HIPAA-protected clinical information, and best practices for anonymizing data and ensuring compliance with privacy regulations. Lastly, ethical concerns have arisen with the advent of generative AI models capable of producing responses indistinguishable from human-written text. 145 – 147 Using a model trained on biased data (for example, unverified content from books and the internet) risks perpetuating those biases. In clinical contexts, LLMs’ cognitive assistance might accelerate research at the cost of safety standards and ethical considerations. Despite efforts to reduce risks, ChatGPT and similar models remain vulnerable to adversarial prompts that can bypass built-in rules. 148 Prominent figures in tech and academia have raised concerns, calling for a pause in development. However, innovation is likely to continue as developers strive to ensure the safety of their releases. 4 There are also ethical issues around generative AI related to scientific writing. Scientific journals promptly decided against accrediting ChatGPT as an author, arguing that the technology lacks the accountability necessary for authorship. They suggested it should be treated as a methodological tool that assists humans with their work, rather than as a co-author. 149 – 151 In relation to the issues discussed above, there are several policies around generative AI in scientific writing. Authors may use artificial intelligence (e.g., ChatGPT) in scientific writing solely to improve readability and language of the work, not to replace essential research and reporting tasks. AI must be used with human oversight in order to avoid incorrect, incomplete or biased output. As mentioned above, AI cannot be listed as an author or co-author. Moreover, the use of AI in the manuscript development must be disclosed to promote transparency and trust. 152 – 154 Authors are accountable for the content of their work and each author is responsible for ensuring the accuracy and integrity of the work and approving the final version. Additionally, authors must ensure that their work is original and does not infringe on third-party rights. The American Academy of Ophthalmology requires that authors request permission to use any copyrighted Academy materials (including, but not limited to, anything published in the Ophthalmology family of journals) as source materials for training AI systems such as large language models. Not only are these materials protected by U.S and international copyright laws and the Academy’s own contractual provisions, AI can present concerns regarding data privacy, the accuracy of content online (including liability for erroneous information), patient safety, and discrimination. 155 Curricula should cover academic integrity policies and guidelines related to the use of generative AI in academic work. This includes discussions on plagiarism, authorship, and the responsible use of AI tools in research and writing. When working with generative AI models in healthcare or other sensitive domains, curricula should emphasize the importance of data privacy and security. This includes guidelines for handling protected health information (PHI) and other sensitive data, as well as best practices for secure data storage and transmission. By incorporating these key issues into curricula, educators can better prepare trainees and researchers to navigate the challenges and opportunities presented by generative AI while promoting responsible and ethical practices. Conclusions Generative AI and LLMs offer promising opportunities to revolutionize medical education, particularly in ophthalmology. Several institutions and nationwide initiatives are pioneering the integration of AI technologies into their training programs, enhancing diagnostic accuracy and patient care. However, various challenges need to be addressed to fully harness the potential of these technologies. Key issues include the generation of clinical inaccuracies or “hallucinations”, the use of outdated training data, and the lack of interpretability in AI models. Ethical concerns, such as biases and data privacy, also pose significant challenges. Therefore, to fully leverage these technologies in ophthalmology education, these gaps and issues must be addressed through comprehensive curricular design. To begin with, structured curricula should encompass fundamental concepts of AI, machine learning, and deep learning, progressing to advanced applications in clinical settings. Emphasis should be placed on ethical implications, legal aspects, and potential biases to ensure responsible and equitable use of AI technologies. Furthermore, incorporating up-to-date training data is crucial for avoiding inaccuracies, necessitating the verification of AI-generated content against current medical research and guidelines. To enhance interpretability, curricula should include explainable AI techniques, Chain-of-Thought prompting, and train students on crafting precise and contextually relevant prompts. Addressing biases in AI models is also essential, with methods for detecting and mitigating biases integrated into the curriculum. Future directions for AI integration in medical education should focus on developing evaluation metrics that are clinically relevant to healthcare and ophthalmology to accurately assess AI model performance. Implementing hybrid models that combine LLM-based evaluations with human oversight will ensure the reliability and accuracy of AI outputs. Additionally, leveraging image-rich ophthalmic data alongside unstructured text data will enhance the comprehensiveness and precision of AI models. Benchmarking AI performance against ophthalmologists and prior DL computer vision models will help establish high standards and improve accuracy. Moreover, it is essential to establish robust policies to govern the use of AI in medical education and clinical practice. These policies should include guidelines for data privacy and security, ensuring compliance with regulations such as HIPAA. Policies should also mandate the transparent disclosure of AI use in research and clinical decision-making processes to maintain trust and accountability. Additionally, there should be standards for continuous monitoring and updating of AI systems to reflect the latest medical knowledge and to address any emerging biases or inaccuracies. By integrating these policies, the medical community can foster a safe and ethical environment for AI applications, ensuring that advancements in AI contribute positively to patient care and medical education. By addressing these areas, educators can better prepare healthcare professionals to use AI technologies effectively and responsibly, ultimately improving patient outcomes and advancing medical knowledge. Ensuring the integration of these elements into medical curricula will foster a new generation of healthcare providers proficient in utilizing AI for enhanced clinical practice. Table 2. Examples of AI and data science educational Institution-Specific Programs in the United States. Institutions and Programs Key Features and Focus Areas Target Audience Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai AI for advanced vision care, - Training in telemedicine, AI-facilitated care, and virtual ophthalmology Eye care professionals, computer scientists Bridge2AI Consortium (NIH Common Funds) Immersive training at the intersection of biomedicine and AI AI-READI project for diabetes insights Individuals aiming to work in biomedicine and AI ACGME-accredited Clinical Informatics Fellowships ACGME-accredited Clinical Informatics Fellowships - decision analysis, predictive analytics, change management, data structures, leadership ACGME Clinical Informatics fellowships for all the medical specializations American Medical Informatics Association (AMIA) 10 × 10 program on health informatics - Specialized courses on nursing informatics, clinical decision support Healthcare professionals American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) New AI content in BCSC textbooks - Policies and guidelines on AI use in ophthalmology Ophthalmologists, ophthalmology residents National Library of Medicine (NLM) T15 Program Training in biomedical informatics and data science - Focus on diverse and underrepresented backgrounds Graduate and postdoctoral trainees American College of Radiology (ACR) AI-LAB ™ Tools for AI learning and model creation - Federated learning across institutions Radiologists Harvard Medical School, Stanford, Duke, University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign Online courses “AI in healthcare” designed to equip healthcare leaders with strategies for designing and implementing AI-driven solutions Healthcare professionals Open in a new tab Table 3. Key Issues and Solutions for LLM/Generative AI Use. Issue Key Concerns Proposed solution Hallucinations/Clinical Inaccuracies LLMs can generate “hallucinations” or AI misinformation. 87 % of articles in a review expressed concerns about misinformation. 51 % of cited references in studies were fabricated. Critically verify AI outputs. Use PubMed and other reliable sources to check generated references. Educate on the importance of source verification. Lack of Recency Training datasets are not up-to-date, leading to inaccuracies. New medications and discoveries are not included in older datasets. Regularly update AI training datasets. Consult healthcare professionals for the latest information. Verify AI-generated content against current, reliable sources. Lack of Interpretability AI models operate as “black boxes,” making decision processes opaque. Responses often lack citations unless requested. Potential for misleading patients. Implement Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting for transparency. Train users in crafting precise, contextually relevant prompts (prompt engineering) Ethical/Safety Concerns/Biases LLMs may perpetuate biases from training data, leading to socioeconomic and demographic inaccuracies. Risk of patient safety breaches and HIPAA violations. Teach methods for detecting AI biases. Incorporate ethical guidelines and case studies in curricula. Use LLMs within health system firewalls for data security. Generative AI in Scientific Writing Ethical concerns about AI authorship and accountability. AI can only improve readability, not replace research tasks. Disclosure of AI use is necessary for transparency. Follow academic integrity policies. Ensure human oversight of AI-generated content. Require permission for using copyrighted materials in AI training. Open in a new tab Funding sources This study was supported by the National Institutes of Health (Grants Keywords: OT2OD032644 [A.H., S.L.B..], DP5OD29610 and P30EY022589 [S.L.B..]) Bethesda, MD,