 396(63.5%) 144(23.1%) 84(13.5%) The ability to treat Viral and bacterial conjunctivitis 468(75.0%) 92(14.7%) 64(10.3%) Blepharitis and stye 342(54.8%) 178(28.5%) 104(16.7%) Trichiasis 287(46.0%) 212(34.0%) 125(20.0%) Corneal abrasion 345(55.3%) 179(28.7%) 100(16.0%) Conjunctival and Corneal foreign body 380(60.9%) 154(24.7%) 90(14.4%) The ability to take comprehensive ophthalmic history 453(72.6%) 100(16.0%) 71(11.4%) Clinical ophthalmic Skills The ability to measure Visual acuity using the Snellen chart 453(72.6%) 124(19.9%) 47(7.5%) The ability to assess pupillary reflexes including using the Swinging flashlight test to detect RAPD 478(76.6%) 89(14.3%) 57(9.1%) The ability to examine the anterior segment with torch light or direct ophthalmoscope 361(57.9%) 170(27.2%) 93(14.9%) The ability to do ophthalmoscopy using a direct ophthalmoscope 354(56.7%) 185(29.6%) 85(13.6%) The ability to assess visual field by confrontation 388(62.2%) 161(25.8%) 75(12.0%) The ability to assess ocular alignment and motility 405(64.9%) 138(22.1%) 81(13.0%) Minor surgery skills such as the ability to remove misdirected lashes and corneal/conjunctival foreign bodies 173(27.7%) 364(58.3%) 87(13.9%) Open in a new tab However, when we questioned the students about how ophthalmology is used in other spheres of life, we found that the proportion of students who were competent in this area was low. When we asked about the visual requirements for committing suicide in the armed forces or for getting a driver’s license, nearly 40% of the students were incompetent, and less than half of them were able to answer. When asked what they knew about the social-psychological effects of blindness or losing an eye, 32.7% of them were not able to answer, 16.8% of them were unsure and only half were able to know the effect of this condition on patients. In a specialized area, more than three-quarters of the student sample as a whole was able to diagnose ptosis, ectropion, entropion, chalazion, and herpes simplex periocular infection. About two-fifths of students were unable to diagnose trichiasis, and even an added 11.5% of them were unsure. Over a quarter of the participants were unable to diagnose either stye or decryocystitis, and nearly 15% were unsure about both conditions. Diagnoses for orbital cellulitis, preseptale cellulitis, lid laceration, corneal abrasion, corneal foreign body, conjunctival foreign body, subconjunctival hemorrhage, and hyphema were made by the majority of students (Table 2 ). It is important to highlight that 13.3% of the sample as a whole were unsure and 20.8% were unable to diagnose an intraocular foreign body. For corneal perforation, 11.5% of students were unsure of the diagnosis. Furthermore, when 11.7% of the participants were uncertain, nearly 18% of them were unable to diagnose acute angle closure glaucoma. As usual, conjunctivitis was caused by bacteria, viruses, and allergies, which the majority of students were able to efficiently diagnose. Pterygium, a fleshy protrusion of the conjunctiva, is essentially a thin, transparent membrane covering the surface of the eye, about 10% of students were unsure, and 15.5% of them were incompetent to make the diagnosis (Table 2 ). Approximately 16% of the student sample were also unable to diagnose retinal vascular diseases in patients with diabetic and hypertensive retinopathy. Besides that, the majority of the sample could identify strabismus, papilledema, and cataracts. We discovered that a particular kind of vision impairment, known as amblyopia, typically affects one eye but is less frequently seen in both. Of the students, 17.5% were incompetent and 15.7% were unsure of their capacity to diagnose. Leukocoria and neonatal nasolacrimal duct obstruction were diagnoses that nearly 14% of students were doubtful of. We noticed that a sizable portion of the participants could identify the majority of eye conditions. The rates of ability to treat were lower compared to the high rates of diagnosis. Nearly 17% of students were unsure of their capacity to treat blepharitis, stye, and corneal abrasions, and more than a quarter of students were incompetent in these areas. Trichiasis can be treated by fewer than half of the participants. 60.9% were competent in treating foreign bodies in the cornea and conjunctiva. However, there were some exceptions in terms of the proportion of the sample that could treat bacterial and viral conjunctivitis, as well as the ability to perform a comprehensive ophthalmic assessment, which increased to three-quarters (Table 2 ). Moreover, we were interested in their clinical ophthalmic abilities. We discovered that a large portion of students were able to measure their visual acuity using the Snellen chart and evaluate their pupillary reflexes, including detecting RAPD in approximately three-quarters of the students using the swinging flash test. But even so, they scored poorly on the skills to achieve ophthalmoscopy with a direct ophthalmoscope and examine the anterior segment with a torch light or direct ophthalmoscope (nearly 30% were incompetent, and nearly 14% were unsure about these skills). Approximately two-fifths of the participants lacked the capacity to evaluate visual fields by confrontation, as well as ocular alignment and mortality. It was necessary to observe that over half of the participants appeared to lack minor surgical skills, and nearly 14% of the students were unsure, including the ability to remove misdirected lashes and corneal or conjectural foreign bodies (Table 2 ).Lastly, we want to highlight that a significant portion of the participants were able to diagnose orbital cellulitis, ectropion, ptosis, corneal foreign bodies, and conjunctival foreign bodies. It’s also encouraging to know that three-quarters of them could treat bacterial and viral conjunctivitis. Furthermore, a significant proportion of the students exhibited the clinical competencies to evaluate pupillary reflexes, including the capacity to identify RAPD using the swinging flash light test and estimate visual acuity using the Snellen chart (Table 2 ). Statistical analyses revealed no significant association between gender and ophthalmology knowledge levels (p-value = 0.149). However, a statistically significant relationship was identified between university affiliation and knowledge level ( p = 0.04). Specifically, students from Yarmouk University and Jordanian University exhibited a higher prevalence of “excellent” knowledge levels compared to students from other universities. This suggests that the university attended may play a role in shaping students’ knowledge and understanding of ophthalmology. Furthermore, a positive correlation was observed between the cumulative averages and ophthalmology knowledge grades ( p < 0.001). Students with higher cumulative averages tended to achieve better grades in the ophthalmology course. Year of medical school also emerged as a significant factor influencing students’ ophthalmology knowledge ( p = 0.014). No statistically significant difference was found in knowledge grades between students with eye problems or a first-degree relative with eye problems ( p = 0.121 and 0.795 respectively). A statistically significant difference in knowledge grade was identified between students who were satisfied and dissatisfied with their ophthalmology rotation ( p < 0.001). Students who expressed satisfaction with their rotation had a higher prevalence of excellent and good knowledge grades. No statistically significant difference in knowledge grade was found between students who planned to apply to ophthalmology residency and those who intended to apply to other specialties ( p = 0.299). This is shown in (Table 3 ). Our study underscores the significant influence of several factors on ophthalmic science knowledge among medical students: university affiliation, cumulative academic performance and the year in which students compete their ophthalmology rotation. Additionally, overall satisfaction with the ophthalmology rotation was found to be a significant predictor of ophthalmic knowledge and diagnostic proficiency among medical students (Table 3 ). Table 3. Variables that affect the basic ophthalmic sciences knowledge among medical students Grade of Knowledge Excellent Good Poor Total P-Value Gender Male 78(12.5%) 112(17.9%) 61(9.8%) 251(40.2%) 0.149 Female 142(22.8%) 141(22.6%) 90(14.4%) 373(59.8%) University Al Balqa Applied University 7(1.1%) 13(2.1%) 8(1.3%) 28(4.5%) 0.004 Hashemite University 7(1.1%) 15(2.4%) 15(2.4%) 37(5.9%) Jordan University of science & technology 27(4.3%) 31(5.0%) 27(4.3%) 85(13.6%) Jordanian University 39 (6.3%) 50(8.0%) 29(4.6%) 118(18.9%) Mu’tah University 6(1.0%) 17(2.7%) 13(2.1%) 36(5.8%) Yarmouk University 134 (21.5%) 127(20.4%) 59(9.5%) 320(51.3%) Cumulative Average Below 70% 10(1.6%) 17(2.7) 19(3.0%) 46(7.4%) 0.000 70-79.9% 63(10.1%) 90(14.4%) 70(11.2%) 223(35.7%) 80.0-89.9% 115(18.4%) 112(17.9%) 47(7.5%) 274(43.9%) Above 90.0% 32(5.1%) 34(5.4%) 15(2.4%) 81(13.0%) Year of ophthalmology rotation 5th year 202(32.4%) 232(37.2%) 126(20.2%) 560(89.7%) 0.014 6th year 18(2.9%) 21(3.4%) 25(4.0%) 64(10.3%) Do you have an eye problem? No 88(14.1%) 124(19.9%) 72(11.5%) 284(45.5%) 0.121 Yes 132(21.2%) 129(20.7%) 79(12.7%) 340(54.5%) Does any of your first-degree relatives have eye problems? No 71(11.4%) 81(13.0%) 53(8.5%) 205(32.9%) 0.795 Yes 149(23.9%) 172(27.6%) 98(15.7%) 419(67.1%) Are you overall satisfied with your Ophthalmology rotation No 77(12.3%) 105(16.8%) 91(14.6%) 273(43.8%) 0.000 Yes 143(22.9%) 148(23.7%) 60(9.6%) 351(56.3%) what will be your first-choice specialty in residency application? Ophthalmology 46 (7.4%) 46(7.4%) 22(3.5%) 114(18.3%) 0.299 others 174(27.9%) 207(33.2%) 129(20.7%) 510(81.7%) Open in a new tab Statistical analyses revealed no significant gender-based disparity in diagnostic ability ( p = 0.133). However, a statistically significant difference emerged in diagnostic ability based on university affiliation ( p < 0.001). Notably, students from Al Balqa Applied University exhibited a higher prevalence of poor diagnostic ability, while those from Yarmouk University demonstrated a greater likelihood of excellent diagnostic ability. Furthermore, a significant relationship was observed between cumulative averages and diagnostic ability ( p < 0.001). Students with lower cumulative averages were more prone to poor diagnostic ability, whereas those with higher averages tended to possess excellent diagnostic skills. No statistically significant difference in diagnostic ability was found between fifth and sixth-year medical students ( p = 0.076). Similarly, no significant differences were detected based on the presence or absence of eye problems ( p = 0.728) or a family history of eye problems ( p = 0.580). However, a statistically significant difference in diagnostic ability was identified between students who were satisfied and dissatisfied with their ophthalmology rotation ( p < 0.001). Students who expressed satisfaction with their rotation demonstrated higher levels of diagnostic proficiency. Finally, no significant difference in diagnostic ability was observed between students who planned to pursue ophthalmology residency and those who intended to apply to other specialties ( p = 0.072). The study findings indicate that the majority of medical students possess excellent diagnostic skills in ophthalmology following completion of the ophthalmology science course. Notably, students from specific universities, those with higher cumulative averages, and those who express satisfaction with their ophthalmology rotation tend to exhibit superior diagnostic abilities. While gender, presence of eye problems, family history of eye problems, and first choice of residency specialty do not appear to significantly diagnostic ability, there is a trend towards students who plan to apply to ophthalmology residency demonstrating better diagnostic skills. Other details and characteristics are shown in (Table 4 ). It is noteworthy that our study revealed significant variations in diagnostic proficiency for ophthalmic diseases based on the university attendance and the overall satisfaction with the ophthalmology rotation. Furthermore, a positive correlation was observed between cumulative academic performance and diagnostic proficiency in ophthalmology, indicating that students with higher cumulative averages demonstrated superior diagnostic abilities (Table 4 ). Table 4. Variables that affect the ability of medical students to diagnose several ophthalmic diseases The ability to diagnose Excellent Good Poor Total P-Value Gender Male 125(20.0%) 79(12.7%) 47(7.5%) 251(40.2%) 0.133 Female 211(33.8%) 91(14.6%) 71(11.4%) 373(59.8%) University Al Balqa Applied University 11(1.8%) 9(1.4%) 8(1.3%) 28(4.5%) 0.000 Hashemite University 11(1.8%) 16(2.6%) 10(1.6%) 37(5.9%) Jordan University of science & technology 40(6.4%) 29(4.6%) 16(2.6%) 85(13.6%) Jordanian University 56(9.0%) 30(4.8%) 32(5.1%) 118(18.9%) Mu’tah University 14(2.2%) 12(1.9%) 10(1.6%) 36(5.8%) Yarmouk University 204(32.7%) 74(11.9%) 42(6.7%) 320(51.3%) Cumulative Average Below 70% 13(2.1%) 15(2.4%) 18(2.9%) 46(7.4%) 0.000 70-79.9% 105(16.8%) 67(10.7%) 51(8.2%) 223(35.7%) 80.0-89.9% 170(27.2%) 66(10.6%) 38(6.1%) 274(43.9%) Above 90.0% 48(7.7%) 22(3.5%) 11(1.8%) 81(13.0%) Year of ophthalmology rotation 5th year 309(49.5%) 151(24.2%) 100(16.0%) 560(89.7%) 0.076 6th year 27(4.3%) 19(3.0%) 18(2.9%) 64(10.3%) Do you have an eye problem? No 148(23.7%) 80(12.8%) 56(9.0%) 284(45.5%) 0.728 Yes 188(30.1%) 90(14.4%) 62(9.9%) 340(54.5%) Does any of your first-degree relatives have eye problems? No 113(18.1%) 58(9.3%) 34(5.4%) 205(32.9%) 0.580 Yes 223(35.7%) 112(17.9%) 84(13.5%) 419(67.1%) Are you overall satisfied with your Ophthalmology rotation No 116(18.6%) 84(13.5%) 73(11.7%) 273(43.8%) 0.000 Yes 220(35.3%) 86(13.8%) 45(7.2%) 351(56.3%) what will be your first-choice specialty in residency application? Ophthalmology 71(11.4%) 29(4.6%) 14(2.2%) 114(18.3%) 0.072 others 265(42.5%) 141(22.6%) 104(16.7%) 510(81.7%) Open in a new tab We investigated the correlations between the ability of students to treat and their year of rotation in ophthalmology, gender, cumulative average, and university. We discovered that the university factor had an impact on students’ treatment abilities ( p = 0.043). Yarmouk University accounted for nearly half of the study’s participants; of them, 34.5% had good treatment abilities and 16.8% had poor ones. Students from Jordanian Universities then make up 18.9% of the sample, and 10.4% of them were well-trained. An 85 students were from science and technology universitiy, 40 student of them had poor ability to treat, while 45 students had a good ability; Hashemite and Mut’ah Universities roughly contributed the same percent for the agood ability to treat in students, with 3.2% (20) of students at Hashemite University and 3.4%(21) of students at Mut’ah University. Additionally, we observed that the cumulative average affected the students’ treatment capacity. The commutative average of 70-79.9% constitutes 35.7% of the sample size, and 20.7% of them had a good ability to treat. Of the sample size, which is 43.9%, with a communicative average of 80-89.9%, 29.2% exhibited good treatment capacity (P-value = 0.000). Of the sample size, 13% had communication scores above 90%, and 8.2% of them had strong treatment ability. On the other hand, we found that there is no relationship between gender and treatment capacity ( P = 0.633). Furthermore, the year of the ophthalmology rotation had no discernible impact on the ability to treat ( P = 0.276). 89.7% of the sample consisted primarily of students who completed the ophthalmology rotation during their fifth year of study. In addition, there is no effect neither if the student had a problem in their eye, nor if their first-degree relative had an eye problem and their ability to treat (respectively, P = 0.071, P = 0.581). There is no effect on the student’s ability to treat and what will their first choice specialty in residency application ( P = 0.108). Finally, the student’s general satisfaction with their ophthalmology rotation affected their treatment capacity ( P = 0.000) (Table 5 ). Finally, we would like to draw attention to the large percentage of students who had strong cumulative averages, which were significantly correlated with their capacity for diagnosis. Furthermore, it is noteworthy that 75% of the respondents expressed satisfaction with their ophthalmology consultation, which appears to be satisfactory (Table 5 ). Table 5. Variables that affect the ability of medical students to treat several ophthalmic diseases The ability to treat Good Poor Total P-Value Gender Male 150(24.0%) 101(16.2%) 251(40.2%) 0.633 Female 230(36.9%) 143(22.9%) 373(59.8%) University Al Balqa Applied University 14(2.2%) 14(2.2%) 28(4.5%) 0.043 Hashemite University 20(3.2%) 17(2.7%) 37(5.9%) Jordan University of science & technology 45(7.2%) 40(6.4%) 85(13.6%) Jordanian University 65(10.4%) 53(8.5%) 118(18.9%) Mu’tah University 21(3.4%) 15(2.4%) 36(5.8%) Yarmouk University 215(34.5%) 105(16.8%) 320(51.3%) Cumulative Average Below 70% 18(2.9%) 28(4.5%) 46(7.4%) 0.003 70-79.9% 129(20.7%) 94(15.1%) 223(35.7%) 80.0-89.9% 182(29.2%) 92(14.7%) 274(43.9%) Above 90.0% 51(8.2%) 30(4.8%) 81(13.0%) Year of ophthalmology rotation 5th year 337(54.0%) 223(35.7%) 560(89.7%) 0.276 6th year 43(6.9%) 21(3.4%) 64(10.3%) Do you have an eye problem? No 162(26.0%) 122(19.6%) 284(45.5%) 0.071 Yes 218(34.9%) 122(19.6%) 340(54.5%) Does any of your first-degree relatives have eye problems? No 128(20.5%) 77(12.3%) 205(32.9%) 0.581 Yes 252(40.4%) 167(26.8%) 419(67.1%) Are you overall satisfied with your Ophthalmology rotation No 119(19.1%) 154 (24.7%) 273 (43.8%) 0.000 Yes 261(41.8%) 90(14.4%) 351(56.3%) what will be your first-choice specialty in residency application? Ophthalmology 77(12.3%) 37(5.9%) 114(18.3%) 0.108 others 303(48.6%) 207(33.2%) 510(81.7%) Open in a new tab We also search into the correlation coefficients between the year of ophthalmic rotation, gender, university, cumulative average, and clinical ophthalmic skills. We discovered that clinical ophthalmic skills were significantly affected by the university ( P = 0.011). Yarmouk University students’ clinical ophthalmic skills were rated as follows: 17.9% had excellent skills, 22.1% had good skills, and 11.2% had poor skills. 3.8% of Jordan University of Science and Technology students possessed exceptional abilities. Furthermore, there is a significant correlation ( P = 0.011) between a student’s clinical ophthalmic skills and whether or not their first-degree relative has vision issues. Furthermore, there is a correlation ( P = 0.000) between a student’s clinical ophthalmic skills and whether or not they were satisfied with their ophthalmology rotation. Additionally, there is a correlation ( P = 0.001) between the student’s ophthalmic skills and the specialty that they will have as their first choice in your application. However, neither gender nor cumulative averages have a significant impact on the clinical advantage skills ( P = 0.515, P = 0.061, respectively). Furthermore, even if the student has eye issues, the ophthalmology rotation they completed at their university has no bearing on their clinical ophthalmic skills or their clinical upside skills ( P = 0.708) (Table 6 ). It is important to note that there was a detectable difference in diagnostic proficiency according to university affiliation. Furthermore, there is a tendency for applicants to ophthalmology residencies to exhibit stronger diagnostic abilities. (Table 6 ). Table 6. Variables that affect the clinical ophthalmic skills among medical students Clinical ophthalmic Skills Excellent Good Poor Total P-Value Gender Male 78(12.5%) 114(18.3%) 59(9.5%) 251(40.2%) 0.515 Female 111(17.8%) 159(25.5%) 103(16.5%) 373(59.8%) University Al Balqa Applied University 7(1.1%) 15(2.4%) 6(1.0%) 28(4.5%) 0.011 Hashemite University 9(1.4%) 11(1.8%) 17(2.7%) 37(5.9%) Jordan University of science & technology 24(3.8%) 44(7.1%) 17(2.7%) 85(13.6%) Jordanian University 26(4.2%) 50(8.0%) 42(6.7%) 118(18.9%) Mu’tah University 11(1.8%) 15(2.4%) 10(1.6%) 36(5.8%) Yarmouk University 112(17.9%) 138(22.1%) 70(11.2%) 320(51.3%) Cumulative Average Below 70% 10(1.6%) 19(3.0%) 17(2.7%) 46(7.4%) 0.061 70-79.9% 70(11.2%) 85(13.6%) 68(10.9%) 223(35.7%) 80.0-89.9% 88(14.1%) 126(20.2%) 60(9.6%) 274(43.9%) Above 90.0% 21(3.4%) 43(6.9%) 17(2.7%) 81(13.0%) Year of ophthalmology rotation 5th year 168(26.9%) 251(40.2%) 141(22.6%) 560(89.7%) 0.900 6th year 21(3.4%) 22(3.5%) 21(3.4%) 64(10.3%) Do you have an eye problem? No 82(13.1%) 129(20.7%) 73(11.7%) 284(45.5%) 0.708 Yes 107(17.1%) 144(23.1%) 89(14.3%) 340(54.5%) Does any of your first-degree relatives have eye problems? No 78(12.5%) 82(13.1%) 45(7.2%) 205(32.9%) 0.011 Yes 111(17.8%) 191(70.0%) 117(72.2%) 419(67.1%) Are you overall satisfied with your Ophthalmology rotation No 49(7.9%) 118(18.9%) 106(17.0%) 273(43.8%) 0.000 Yes 140(22.4%) 155(24.8%) 56(9.0%) 351(56.3%) what will be your first-choice specialty in residency application? Ophthalmology 51(8.2%) 39(6.3%) 24(3.85) 114(18.3%) 0.001 others 138(22.1%) 234(37.5%) 138(22.1%) 510(81.7%) Open in a new tab Discussion This cross-sectional observational study attempted to evaluate the quality of ophthalmology education at the undergraduate level and the level of comfort that students have in managing eye diseases. The majority of research participants had a good degree of competence in identifying common ocular issues. 86.1% of respondents said they were comfortable enough to diagnose cataracts, which is good because cataracts are the primary avoidable cause of blindness in developing nations. Our survey revealed a high proportion of students’ knowledge in ophthalmology; in contrast, another study that contacted 56 hospitals and 20 medical schools in Australia revealed medical students’ mean confidence in ophthalmology-specific knowledge and skill areas was low [ 13 ]. However, even with this adequate degree of familiarity with handling common eye issues, the majority of research participants’ comfort level in identifying stye and dacryocystitis was determined to be insufficient. Our study agreed with the prospective survey questionnaire study [ 8 ], that there was no statistically significant variation ( p > 0.05) observed in the percentage of confidence in one’s capacity to detect eye illnesses or conduct clinical skills between genders. Another study contradicted this and that there was a noticeable gender difference ( P < 0.05) [ 14 ]. The knowledge scores and the student’s age and academic year showed a significant correlation ( P = 0.014), referring to another cross-sectional survey, it was noted that the knowledge scores were highly linked with the age of the students and their academic year; the knowledge score rose by 0.25 for every extra year of study ( P < 0.001) [ 15 ]. We found that students’ treatment abilities were impacted by the university factor ( p = 0.043). Approximately 50% of the study participants were from Yarmouk University, and 34.5% of them had good therapeutic abilities, but knowledge was less among Al Balqa Applied University students. 72.4% of the students in our study had the ability to diagnose retinal vascular diseases like diabetic and hypertensive retinopathy which was a good percentage, unlike other cross-sectional studies involving 150 participants low awareness of diabetes-related eye illnesses among study participants was found, and increased understanding of diabetic retinopathy was connected with higher socioeconomic class [ 16 ]. Over three-quarters of the student population as a whole diagnosed chalazion, ptosis, entropion, ectropion, and herpes simplex periocular infection in a particular area. the percentage of the sample that was capable of treating both viral and bacterial conjunctivitis and full ocular history, is 75% of the sample. Approximately two-fifths of participants were unable to assess visual fields based on confrontation, ocular alignment, and mortality. Overall, our participants expressed satisfaction with their ophthalmology rotation. There was a significant difference between the students’ ability to treat and their cumulative average ( P = 0.003). Furthermore, there was no significant difference in the students’ clinical ocular abilities or treatment capacity based on the year they completed the ophthalmology rotation. It was discovered that those with first-degree relatives with eye issues performed better on their clinical ophthalmology skills. Lastly, there was no discernible difference in diagnostic proficiency between students who intended to apply to other specialties and those who wanted to pursue ophthalmology residency ( p = 0.072). According to a study, students who wanted to become ophthalmologists showed early engagement in relevant academic activities and a lower level of anxiety about intellectual and practical problems compared to their peers. By dispelling myths and generating interest in this field, formal ophthalmology rotations are a critical step in recruiting future ophthalmologists [ 17 ]. After completing the ophthalmic science course, most medical students have outstanding diagnostic skills in ophthalmology, according to the study’s findings. Notably, better diagnostic skills are typically demonstrated by students from particular colleges, those with higher cumulative averages, and those who report satisfaction with their ophthalmology rotation. Finally, there was no discernible difference in the diagnostic skills of students who intended to apply to other specialties versus those who wanted to pursue a residency in ophthalmology. After completing the ophthalmic science course, most medical students have outstanding diagnostic skills in ophthalmology, according to the study’s findings. Notably, better diagnostic skills are typically demonstrated by students from particular colleges, those with higher cumulative averages, and those who report satisfaction with their ophthalmology rotation. There is a tendency for students who intend to apply to an ophthalmology residency to show higher diagnostic skills, even while factors like gender, the existence of eye issues, a family history of eye problems, and first choice of residency specialty do not seem to significantly affect diagnostic ability. Numerous research has been conducted to determine what should be taught in undergraduate ophthalmology courses [ 18 , 19 ]. Over the years, other national and international curriculum guidelines have also been developed [ 11 , 12 ]. Increasing the amount of training hours would help us concentrate more on imparting the knowledge and skills that undergraduate medical students require to be effective in the field of ophthalmology. According to a study, students’ knowledge and abilities in the field of ophthalmology can be improved by concentrating on getting enough training hours spread out over several weeks. For the purpose of gaining both theoretical and practical information, medical students at International Medical University were provided with sufficient hours of ophthalmology education and training during their two-week posting. Their capacity to carry out clinical tasks and confidently detect common eye conditions served as proof of this. According to the self-reported assessment survey, the training was successful [ 8 ]. Conclusion Our study underscores the critical need for enhanced and standardized ophthalmology education within undergraduate medical curricula. While a significant proportion of medical students demonstrated proficiency in diagnosing common ocular conditions, there remains a notable gap in their ability to manage more complex cases and perform certain clinical skills. Variability in knowledge and skills based on university affiliation and satisfaction with ophthalmology rotations highlights the importance of a cohesive educational strategy. Furthermore, the positive correlation between higher cumulative averages and superior diagnostic and treatment abilities suggests that academic excellence, in general, can translate to better ophthalmological competencies. The data indicate that increasing the emphasis on practical, hands-on ophthalmology training and expanding the curriculum to address identified deficiencies could significantly improve the readiness of future primary care physicians to manage eye health effectively. Implementing these educational enhancements will be pivotal in addressing the rising prevalence of eye diseases and ensuring comprehensive primary eye care in diverse healthcare settings. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (13.6KB, docx) Acknowledgements Not applicable. Author contributions Rami Al-Omari, Khayry Al-Shami, Almu’atasim Khamees, Saja Karaja, Danya Radaideah, Omamah Shlool, Nedalaldeen Alnajadat, Amira Otoum, and Manar Al-Shami: wrote the main manuscript text and prepared tables. All authors reviewed the manuscript and agree with its submission. Funding None. Data availability The datasets used and/or analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The Ethics Committee at Yarmouk University considered that the research does not require ethical approval as it is an cross-sectional research based on a questionnaire. In addition, one of the professors of our university is with us in this research. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Methods The survey used in this cross-sectional study was developed for this study. Informed consent Informed consent was obtained from all individual participants included in the study. Participants were provided with detailed information about the study’s purpose, procedures, potential risks, and benefits, and they could ask questions before giving their written consent. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Divya K, et al. Needs assessment of ophthalmology education for undergraduate medical students–A study from a medical college in South India. Educ Health. 2017;30(3):223–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Abuallut I et al. Assessment of Ophthalmology Teaching and its impact on the choice of future specialties among medical students of Jazan University. Cureus, 2023. 15(11). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 3. Fan JC, Sherwin T, McGhee CN. Teaching of ophthalmology in undergraduate curricula: a survey of Australasian and Asian medical schools. Clin Exp Ophthalmol. 2007;35(4):310–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Al-Najmi YA et al. Medical schools’ ophthalmology course: an appraisal by ophthalmology residents. Int J Gen Med, 2021: pp. 8365–72. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 5. Ibrahim MKM, Wolvaardt JE. Outcomes of a short course in improving eye healthcare knowledge and skills among final-year medical students. Med Sci Educ. 2022;32(4):847–53. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Succar T, et al. Advancing ophthalmology medical student education: international insights and strategies for enhanced teaching. Surv Ophthalmol. 2020;65(2):263–71. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hill S, Dennick R, Amoaku W. Present and future of the undergraduate ophthalmology curriculum: a survey of UK medical schools. Int J Med Educ. 2017;8:389. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Reddy S, Moe S. Assessment of skills and Knowledge in Clinical Ophthalmology: a Survey among Undergraduate Medical students in Malaysia. Br J Med Med Res. 2017;19(7):1–10. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Alselaimy RM, ALBalawi HB. Undergraduate ophthalmology teaching in Saudi Arabia: assessment, analysis, and comparisons. Advances in Medical Education and Practice, 2021: pp. 1457–1464. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 10. Byers WGM. The place of ophthalmology in the undergraduate medical curriculum. Trans Am Ophthalmol Soc. 1922;20:215. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Goldberg MF, Lee AG, Tso MO. Principles and guidelines of a curriculum for education of the ophthalmic specialist: presented by International Task Force on Resident and specialist education in Ophthalmology-On behalf of the International Council of Ophthalmology (ICO). Klin Monatsbl Augenheilkd. 2006;223(SUPPL 4):S3–48. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Parrish RK, Tso MO, (ICO). Principles and guidelines of a curriculum for ophthalmic education of medical students: Presented by International Task Force on Ophthalmic Education of Medical Students-On behalf of the International Council of Ophthalmology. Klinische Monatsblatter fur Augenheilkunde, 2006. 223(SUPPL. 5): pp. S3-S19. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 13. Zhang HH, et al. Sharpening the focus on ophthalmology teaching: perceptions of medical students and junior medical officers. Clin Exp Ophthalmol. 2018;46(9):984–93. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Eze BI, et al. Assessing the knowledge and skills in clinical ophthalmology of medical interns: Survey results from Enugu, South-Eastern Nigeria. Middle East Afr J Ophthalmol. 2012;19(1):135–40. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Abusamak M, et al. Knowledge, attitudes, practices and barriers of medical research among undergraduate medical students in Jordan: a cross-sectional survey. BMC Med Educ. 2024;24(1):23. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Eshwar MD et al. Knowledge, awareness, and perception of common eye diseases and eye donation among people seeking healthcare in a tertiary hospital in Telangana, South India. Cureus, 2022. 14(11). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 17. Madani FM, et al. Perceptions and career expectation in ophthalmology among Saudi undergraduate medical students. Middle East Afr J Ophthalmol. 2018;25(3–4):142–9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Crombie A. Ophthalmology in the undergraduate curriculum. Trans Ophthalmological Soc United Kingd. 1976;96(1):33–4. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Spivey BE. A technique to determine curriculum content. Acad Med. 1971;46(4):269–74. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1 (13.6KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The datasets used and/or analysed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',8,'2025-10-14 10:04:14','2025-10-14 10:04:14');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2803,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6724451','Why ophthalmology? Analysis of the motivating factors influencing the choice of ophthalmology as a career among different generations in Brazil.','Gameiro GR, Darcie ALF, Hazaki D, Gameiro GR, Carricondo PC.','2019','immediatist desiring comites cep boomer boomer altruism impatience immediatism craves gratification altruism motivator immediatism gameiro darcie alf hazaki gameiro carricondo leagues jarecky strodel griffen cendan kolcic polasek mihalj gombac kraljevic kraljevic croat burack irby carline ambrozy ellsbury stritter selves naskashima motivacoes percepcoes escolha carreira oftalmologia oftalmol brandao barral netto e0133585 uemoto kawamoto kusunoki kohara ronen igakkai zasshi marschall karimuddin jarjoura rutecki creed searle parsa aghazadeh nejatisafa amini mostafazadeh freshmen morio otaki jarecky strodel grayson tardiff seiferth enoch chibnall slavin elam millennial twenge dilullo kriebel demystifying millennial pingleton millennial s0008 schendel baerlocher i07 abdulrahman makki shaaban shamsi venkatramana sulaiman bron katona attractions arruda s1807 wagoner bridwell kleinert romotzky knepper wasilewski baton e0188114','Abstract OBJECTIVES: The increasing demand for medical specialties with flexible working hours has been associated with the important role of quality of life as a determining factor when choosing a career in medicine, which might change the motivations for pursuing a career in ophthalmology. We aim to identify the main determinants of ophthalmology as a career choice as well as the reasons that motivated previous generations to follow this path. METHODS: Responses to self-administered online questionnaires were analyzed. RESULTS: A total of 225 responses were analyzed, including those of baby boomers (21), generation X (48), generation Y (131) and generation Z (25). Although the main reasons for choosing ophthalmology as a career are the same for all the generations in this study (flexible working hours, self-satisfaction from helping people improve their vision and the possibility of performing surgical procedures), some reasons for this career choice are more important to the younger generations (short-term results and short procedures), and some are more important to the older generations (the influence of an ophthalmologist in the family). CONCLUSION: The main reasons for choosing ophthalmology as a career are essentially the same over time. The differences in secondary motivations could be explained by generational differences. Keywords: Ophthalmology, Career Choice, Career Satisfaction, Motivation, Choice Behavior INTRODUCTION In recent years, the search for professional areas with flexible working hours by new graduate physicians has attracted the attention of scholars worldwide, showing a shift in the profile of new physicians. A growing demand for positions in ophthalmology, dermatology, otorhinolaryngology, psychiatry and radiology has been reported 1 - 4 . The main motivations for choosing a medical specialty include the intellectual challenge, market perspectives and, recently, quality of life 5 - 9 . The high importance given to work-life balance when choosing a medical career has been noted as being responsible for the increasing demand for certain specialties over other, more traditional areas such as internal medicine, pediatrics and general surgery 5 - 8 , 10 - 18 . Changes in motivation can be explained by generational differences, as members of each generation have their own attitudes, preferences and key characteristics. In spite of better dealing with differences and working better in a group, the current generation of medical students and residents, known as generation Y or millennials, is often described as being immediatist, desiring change, being individualistic and advocating for quality of life and leisure time 19 - 23 . Their particular traits could influence their main motivations in their career choices. Many studies have investigated the current reasons for choosing different medical specialties; however, only a few have focused on understanding the selection of less traditional specialties and the differences among generations. This study aims to characterize the main current determinants of the choice of ophthalmology as a specialty as well as the reasons that influenced previous generations to pursue this career. METHODS Participants The sample was composed of Brazilian medical students participating in ophthalmology interest groups, ophthalmology residents in institutions accredited by the Brazilian Council of Ophthalmology and ophthalmologists in private practice. One thousand two hundred fifty-three subjects were contacted by email to answer a self-administered online questionnaire. Acceptance of the electronic consent form was necessary before proceeding to the questionnaire itself. The participants were divided into generational groups according to their birth year (baby boomers: 1946-1964; generation X: 1965-1980; generation Y/millennials: 1981-1994; and generation Z: 1995-). Instruments The instrument used in this study was an online self-administered questionnaire with objective questions regarding demographic information, reasons for choosing ophthalmology as a specialty, the presence of ophthalmologists in the family and the time of selection of the specialty. The questionnaire was sent via email by the Brazilian Council of Ophthalmology and the Brazilian Association of Ophthalmology Interest Groups. A sample of the administered questionnaire is shown in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Questionnaire. Data Analysis Data analysis was performed with the aid of the computer program SPSS v.24.0 (IBM, Armonk, New York) using the appropriate tests for each variable, which are displayed after each table. Ethical Approval Ethical approval for this study was obtained from the Research Ethics Committees (Comitês de Ética em Pesquisa - CEP) under protocol 2.622.328/2018. RESULTS The response rate was 17.95%. A total of 225 responses were analyzed, which consisted of the responses from the baby boomers (n=21), generation X (n=48), generation Y (n=131) and generation Z (n=25) groups, as shown in Table 1 . The participants included 114 ophthalmologists, 51 ophthalmology residents and 60 medical students ( Table 1 ). There were no significant differences in the gender composition of the groups ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Demographic analysis of the participants. Generation Frequency Percentage Baby boomer 21 9.3 Generation X 48 21.3 Generation Y 131 58.2 Generation Z 25 11.1 Total 225 100 Level of training Frequency Percentage Ophthalmologist 114 50.7 Ophthalmology resident 51 22.7 Medical student 60 26.7 Total 225 100 Gender Frequency Percentage Male 121 53.8 Female 104 46.2 Total 225 100 Region Frequency Percentage North 9 4.0 Midwest 19 8.4 Northeast 27 12.0 Southeast 145 64.4 South 25 11.1 Total 225 100 Ophthalmologist in the family Frequency Percentage No 159 70.7 Yes 66 29.3 Total 225 100 Open in a new tab The majority of the participants were from the Southeast Region (64%), followed by the Northeast Region (12%) and South Region (11%), as shown in Table 1 . When asked about having an ophthalmologist in the family, 70.7% of the participants in our study answered negatively ( Table 1 ). The main motivations behind each generation''s choice of a career in ophthalmology as well as the significant differences among them are shown in Table 2 . Table 2. Main reasons for choosing a career in ophthalmology. Reason Baby boomer Generation X Generation Y Generation Z All p -value Surgical procedures 52% 65% 70% 64% 67% 0.415 a Flexible working hours 48% 48% 58% 48% 54% 0.519 a Personal satisfaction from helping people improve their vision 52% 40% 37% 44% 40% 0.598 a Compensation 24% 23% 38% 28% 32% 0.180 a Short-term results 14% 17% 34% 20% 27% 0.049 * a Influence of another person (mentor, professor, role model) 43% 29% 22% 20% 25% 0.182 a Technological innovations 29% 31% 24% 16% 25% 0.502 a Little contact with emergency situations 24% 19% 27% 20% 24% 0.622 a Short procedures 5% 6% 34% 24% 24% 0.000 * a Exposure to the area during medical school 19% 38% 19% 24% 24% 0.076 a Interface with other areas of medicine 29% 15% 16% 48% 20% 0.002 * a Personal experience of an ophthalmic condition 14% 31% 16% 16% 19% 0.144 b Influence of an ophthalmologist in the family 33% 19% 12% 36% 18% 0.008 * b Characteristics of the residency program 0% 6% 24% 16% 17% 0.002 * b More individualized work 19% 15% 16% 12% 16% 0.944 b Extracurricular experiences in the area 5% 21% 13% 20% 15% 0.260 b Academic career 0% 8% 10% 28% 11% 0.022 * b Intellectual challenge 5% 10% 6% 4% 7% 0.749 b Altruism 0% 4% 3% 8% 4% 0.479 b Social recognition 5% 0% 3% 0% 2% 0.483 b Open in a new tab a -chi-square, b - Fisher’s Exact test, * - statistically significant. The majority of the participants chose to follow ophthalmology as a career after being exposed to the area during medical school (77.7%), except for those who had a relative who was an ophthalmologist, who chose the career earlier, as seen in Table 3 . Table 3. Time of career choice. Chi-square = 0.003 No ophthalmologist in the family Ophthalmologist in the family Total Before being exposed to ophthalmology 27 23 50 After being exposed to ophthalmology 132 43 175 Total 159 66 225 Open in a new tab DISCUSSION Old but still gold In our study, we tried to identify whether younger generations aiming for a career in ophthalmology are influenced by different reasons than older generations in making their career choices, following the current trend of deciding on lifestyle-friendly residency programs 2 - 3 . According to previous studies 9 , 24 - 27 , the main reasons for choosing ophthalmology as a career are the possibility of performing surgical procedures, flexibility, earning potential and intellectual stimulation. The strongest determining factors of career choices for all generation groups in this study were in accordance with the previous results in the literature, and there were no significant differences among the groups, showing that the main reasons for choosing ophthalmology may be essentially consistent over time ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Percentage of the participants in each generation that indicated the possibility of surgical procedures and flexible working hours as important reasons for choosing a residency in ophthalmology. No significant differences were found among the groups ( p -values of 0.415 and 0.519, respectively). All things come to those who… want? Although the main reasons for choosing a career in ophthalmology were the same for all groups, some reasons were significantly more important to the generation Y and generation Z physicians. This motivational trend can be explained by differences among generations. Known for their impatience and immediatism, generation Y craves instant gratification, which may reflect the motivations that influence their career choices. In fact, direct access (no requirement to first complete an internal medicine residency) to ophthalmology residency programs and short-term results were significantly ( p =0.002 and p =0.047) more important to the younger generations than to the older generations. Additionally, the short length of ophthalmic surgical procedures was statistically more likely to be identified by the generation Y and Z participants than by the participants from previous generations ( p =0.000) ( Figure 3 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Percentage of the participants in each generation that chose short-term results, short procedures and the characteristics of the residency program (direct access) as important reasons for choosing ophthalmology. Generations Y and Z valued these reasons significantly more than the previous generations ( p -values of 0.049, less than 0.0001 and 0.002, respectively). The apple does not fall far from the tree The influence of an ophthalmologist in the family was noted as a determinant for the choice of ophthalmology by 33.3% of the baby boomers, 18.8% of the generation X participants, 12.2% of the millennials and 36% of the generation Z participants, with statistically significant differences among the groups ( p -value of 0.008) ( Figure 4 ). Figure 4. Open in a new tab Percentage of the participants in each generation that indicated the influence of an ophthalmologist in the family as an important factor for choosing a career in ophthalmology. Generation Z participants and baby boomers were the most impacted by this circumstance, with 36% and 33% of the participants answering positively, respectively ( p -value of 0.008). Although having an ophthalmologist relative was not one of the most cited reasons, 34.8% of the participants who had an ophthalmologist in the family chose a career in ophthalmology before being exposed to the area during medical school; this finding differed from that for the other participants without an ophthalmologist in the family, as 83% of these participants had decided on a career in ophthalmology during the last years of their graduate studies ( p -value of 0.003) ( Table 3 ). Last and least The least cited reasons among all generation groups for choosing ophthalmology as a career were altruism, the intellectual challenge and social recognition. This finding is inconsistent with the main determinants of the choice of medicine as a career that have been traditionally described in the literature 28 - 31 . Whether future ophthalmologists will choose to attend medical school for different reasons remains to be investigated. Pursuing an academic career was an important motivator for 28% of the generation Z participants and for 11% of the respondents overall ( p =0.022). This result might arise from a selection bias since, at the time of the study, most of the participants in the generation Z group were involved in academic research activities linked to their participation in ophthalmology interest groups, which are organizations run by medical students interested in ophthalmology who organize and participate in extracurricular activities related to the field. CONCLUSIONS The main reasons for choosing ophthalmology as a career differ from the determinants for deciding to be a physician. Whether a change in motivation occurs during medical school for those who decide on the ophthalmology career path remains to be investigated. The differences in the motivations for deciding to become an ophthalmologist could be explained by generational differences. The immediatism of members of younger generations may impact their career choice determinants so that not only quality of life but also short-term results are highly valued. However, the main reasons for choosing ophthalmology are essentially consistent over time. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Gameiro GR, Darcie ALF and Hazaki D contributed to the conception and design of the manuscript, literature review, manuscript drafting, critical revision and final approval of the version to be published. Gameiro RG contributed to the drafting of the final version of the manuscript, critical revision and final approval of the version to be published. Carricondo PC contributed to the conception and design of the manuscript, critical revision of the manuscript and final approval of the version to be published. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thank the Brazilian Council of Ophthalmology and the Brazilian Association of Ophthalmology Leagues for sending our questionnaire via email and thus helping us with this research. Footnotes No potential conflict of interest was reported. REFERENCES 1. Gough HG. Specialty preferences of physicians and medical students. J Med Educ. 1975;50((6)):581–8. doi: 10.1097/00001888-197506000-00003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Schwartz RW, Jarecky RK, Strodel WE, Haley JV, Young B, Griffen WO., Jr Controllable lifestyle: a new factor in career choice by medical students. Acad Med. 1989;64((10)):606–9. doi: 10.1097/00001888-198910000-00012. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lind DS, Cendan JC. Two decades of student career choice at the University of Florida: increasingly a lifestyle decision. Am Surg. 2003;69((1)):53–5. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kolcić I, Polasek O, Mihalj H, Gombac E, Kraljević V, Kraljević I, et al. Research involvement, specialty choice, and emigration preferences of final year medical students in Croatia. Croat Med J. 2005;46((1)):88–95. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Lieu TA, Schroeder SA, Altman DF. Specialty choices at one medical school: recent trends and analysis of predictive factors. Acad Med. 1989;64((10)):622–9. doi: 10.1097/00001888-198910000-00015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Burack JH, Irby DM, Carline JD, Ambrozy DM, Ellsbury KE, Stritter FT. A study of medical students’ specialty-choice pathways: trying on possible selves. Acad Med. 1997;72((6)):534–41. doi: 10.1097/00001888-199706000-00021. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Alves MR, Naskashima AF. Motivações e percepções de médicos residentes em relação è escolha da carreira em Oftalmologia. Rev Bras Oftalmol. 2003;62(|(11)):776–81. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Correia Lima de Souza L, Mendonça VR, Garcia GB, Brandão EC, Barral-Netto M. Medical Specialty Choice and Related Factors of Brazilian Medical Students and Recent Doctors. PLoS One. 2015;10((7)):e0133585. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0133585. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Uemoto A, Kawamoto R, Abe M, Kusunoki T, Kohara K, Miki T. The differences in speciality preferences and career determinant factors between first- and fifth-year medical school students. Nihon Ronen Igakkai Zasshi. 2015;52((1)):48–54. doi: 10.3143/geriatrics.52.48. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Marschall JG, Karimuddin AA. Decline in popularity of general surgery as a career choice in North America: review of postgraduate residency trainin