ed Educ. 2013;47((2)):173–81. doi: 10.1111/medu.12083. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Borges NJ, Manuel RS, Elam CL, Jones BJ. Comparing millennial and generation X medical students at one medical school. Acad Med. 2006;81((6)):571–6. doi: 10.1097/01.ACM.0000225222.38078.47. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Olson ME. The “Millennials”: first year in practice. Nurs Outlook. 2009;57((1)):10–7. doi: 10.1016/j.outlook.2008.06.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Twenge JM. Generational changes and their impact in the classroom: teaching Generation Me. Med Educ. 2009;43((5)):398–405. doi: 10.1111/j.1365-2923.2009.03310.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. DiLullo C, McGee P, Kriebel RM. Demystifying the Millennial student: a reassessment in measures of character and engagement in professional education. Anat Sci Educ. 2011;4((4)):214–26. doi: 10.1002/ase.240. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Pingleton SK. Millennial health care: change you can believe in. Chest. 2012;142((1)):22–9. doi: 10.1378/chest.11-3034. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Noble J. Factors influencing career choice in ophthalmology. Can J Ophthalmol. 2006;41((5)):596–9. doi: 10.1016/S0008-4182(06)80029-3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Noble J, Schendel S, Daniel S, Baerlocher MO. Motivations and future trends: a survey of Canadian ophthalmology residents. Can J Ophthalmol. 2007;42((6)):821–5. doi: 10.3129/i07-156. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Abdulrahman M, Makki M, Shaaban S, Al Shamsi M, Venkatramana M, Sulaiman N, et al. Specialty preferences and motivating factors: A national survey on medical students from five uae medical schools. Educ Health. 2016;29((3)):231–43. doi: 10.4103/1357-6283.204225. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Lambert TW, Goldacre MJ, Bron AJ. Career choices for ophthalmology made by newly qualified doctors in the United Kingdom, 1974-2005. BMC Ophthalmol. 2008;8:3. doi: 10.1186/1471-2415-8-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. McManus IC, Livingston G, Katona C. The attractions of medicine: the generic motivations of medical school applicants in relation to demography, personality and achievement. BMC Med Educ. 2006;6:11. doi: 10.1186/1472-6920-6-11. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Millan LR, Azevedo RS, Rossi E, De Marco OL, Millan MP, de Arruda PC. What is behind a student’s choice for becoming a doctor? Clinics. 2005;60((2)):143–50. doi: 10.1590/S1807-59322005000200011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Wagoner NE, Bridwell SD. High school students’ motivations for a career as a physician. Acad Med. 1989;64((6)):325–7. doi: 10.1097/00001888-198906000-00009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 31. Kleinert R, Fuchs C, Romotzky V, Knepper L, Wasilewski ML, Schröder W, et al. Generation Y and surgical residency - Passing the baton or the end of the world as we know it? Results from a survey among medical students in Germany. PLoS One. 2017;12((11)):e0188114. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0188114. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-14 10:04:27','2025-10-14 10:04:27');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2804,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10570920','Ophthalmology Faculty Diversity Trends in the US.','Ali AA, Chauhan MZ, Doty M, Bui T, Phillips PH, Sallam AB.','2023','fairless woreta aggregations intradepartmental anaconda fairless woreta xierali tanya emami naeini rabb venable blueprints compel cultivates talented parsa hassanein elam e89 e113 fairless nwanyanwu woreta zebardast e127 e136 fountain parke briceno omoruyi montez e2021055472 datacenter aecf bernet zebardast xierali nivet xierali nivet orji sebro e0207274 hrm scholarworks dspace fulton_2021_engaging differences_postprint dotson nuru jeter adetayo tanya jcjo terrin breeze emami naeini lieng watane darani esplen bjpsych dougan dromgold sermen sathy panter s40594 vanka dlo meneses mckenney elkbuli aguwa srikumaran canner padovani claudio croteau chonka mmg rabb venable e298 e303 mmg frierson sedlacek dickins 0b013e3182769513 elhusseiny oke adomfeh vanderveen ogla elhusseiny rook boatright genao floyd','Key Points Question What are the current trends in ophthalmology faculty diversity in the US, and how have they evolved over the past 20 years? Findings This cross-sectional study of 56 438 ophthalmology faculty members found a substantial increase in ophthalmology faculty members and an increase in representation of women across several faculty ranks. However, the representation of individuals underrepresented in medicine (URiM) has remained largely unchanged. Meaning Findings suggest that, given the increasing resistance to diversity, equity, and inclusion efforts in numerous states, it is crucial to prioritize the recruitment and retention of URiM faculty to ensure a diverse ophthalmic workforce capable of delivering culturally sensitive care and fostering the recruitment of URiM students to the field of ophthalmology. Abstract Importance Enhancing the diversity of ophthalmologists can potentially contribute to diminishing disparities in eye care. Objectives To investigate longitudinal trends in the representation of individuals underrepresented in medicine (URiM) and women among ophthalmology faculty compared with other specialties and to assess disparities between ophthalmology faculty demographic characteristics and the US population. Design, Setting, and Participants In this cross-sectional study, a comprehensive analysis of Association of American Medical Colleges Faculty Roster data between 2000 and 2021 was conducted, using data from the US Medical School Faculty report on 56 438 ophthalmology faculty members. Main Outcomes and Measures The data set was used to differentiate ophthalmology faculty members by gender, rank, and designation as a URiM individual. Outcome measures included changes in URiM and non-URiM faculty proportions, faculty rank, gender, department chair positions, and the US population between 2000 and 2021. Results A total of 56 438 ophthalmology faculty members (37 511 men [66.5%]) were included in the study. The number of ophthalmology faculty increased from 1820 in 2000 to 3151 in 2021. Across all years, URiM female faculty represented 3.1% of ophthalmologists (1733 of 56 438), while URiM men accounted for 3.5% (1983 of 56 438). Overall, non-URiM men constituted 63.0% of ophthalmologists (35 528 of 56 438), and non-URiM women accounted for 30.5% (17 194 of 56 438). The proportion of URiM male faculty remained stable from 2000 to 2021 (63 of 1820 [3.5%] vs 104 of 3151 [3.3%]), with a small increase in URiM women faculty at junior faculty positions (2000, 40 of 1820 [2.2%]; 2021, 129 of 3151 [4.1%]; difference, 1.9% [95% CI, 0.9%-2.9%]). Proportions of non-URiM men decreased from 71.2% (1295 of 1820) in 2000 to 55.3% (1743 of 3151) in 2021 (difference, 15.8% [95% CI, 13.1%-18.6%]) and proportions of non-URiM women increased from 23.2% (422 of 1820) in 2000 to 37.3% (1175 of 3151) in 2021 (difference, 14.1% [95% CI, 11.5%-16.7%]). In terms of faculty rank, there were increases in representation of women from 2000 to 2021 at assistant professor (from 11.2% [203 of 1819] to 19.7% [622 of 3165]; difference, 8.5% [95% CI, 6.5%-10.5%]), associate professor (from 4.6% [83 of 1819] to 8.6% [271 of 3165]; difference, 4.0% [95% CI, 2.6%-5.4%]), and professor levels (from 2.8% [51 of 1819] to 7.1% [223 of 3165]; difference, 4.3% [95% CI, 3.9%-6.1%]). URiM representation remained stable at most ranks. Compared with other specialties, ophthalmology had among the lowest percentage change in URiM faculty. The expansion of ophthalmology’s URiM faculty representation was less than one-third that of the diverse US population. Conclusion and Relevance These findings highlight substantial growth in ophthalmology faculty and an increase in women’s representation across several faculty ranks. However, URiM representation has remained largely unchanged, despite an increase at the associate professor level. This finding emphasizes the need for continued efforts to enhance diversity in academic ophthalmology. This cross-sectional study investigates longitudinal trends in the representation of individuals underrepresented in medicine and women among ophthalmology faculty compared with other specialties and assesses disparities between ophthalmology faculty demographic characteristics and the US population. Introduction The correlation between increased diversity in the physician workforce and improved health care quality has been consistently demonstrated in contemporary research, emphasizing the necessity of a diverse workforce. 1 , 2 In 2021, a study by Fairless et al 3 brought to light the absence of individuals from underrepresented racial and ethnic minority groups among ophthalmology faculty compared with other specialties, providing a single-year analysis of the 2019 Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC) Faculty Roster. The findings were not just an echo of a familiar narrative of underrepresentation but rather an urgent call to action, serving as a catalyst for further investigation into diversity in the medical profession. This concern was further magnified in a review of 2021 American Academy of Ophthalmology data by Woreta et al 4 that showed persistent racial and ethnic and gender disparities within the ophthalmology workforce. Moreover, other studies have raised the issue of health disparities in ophthalmology, corroborating the need for diversified representation in the field. 2 , 5 , 6 Based on these insights, 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 we undertook a thorough analysis of the AAMC Faculty Roster’s data from 2000 to 2021, focusing on ophthalmology faculty. 7 Our primary objective was to gain insights into the progression of diversity over time, specifically focusing on underrepresented in medicine (URiM) and gender groups within the field of ophthalmology in comparison with their counterparts. By adopting a longitudinal perspective, we aimed to enhance the understanding of the complex dynamics of representation over the course of 2 decades and evaluate the effectiveness of diversity initiatives implemented during this period. Methods In this retrospective cross-sectional study, we used data from the AAMC Faculty Roster, specifically Tables 19, 16, and C from the annual US Medical School Faculty report from 2000 to 2021. 7 , 8 The AAMC data set provides information on various characteristics of faculty, such as gender, race, and ethnicity trends, including department chairs. In addition, the data set includes information on faculty members belonging to 17 specialty classifications within the clinical sciences. Table 16 provides information on reported gender, race and ethnicity, and specialty department. Table 19 provides additional information on faculty rank. Table C provides information on department chairs by specialty, gender, and race and ethnicity constructs. The total number of faculty across all years (2000-2021) varied slightly between Tables 16 and 19 (56 438 vs 56 457). The AAMC Faculty Roster has collected comprehensive data on accredited US medical schools since 1966, with annual updates every January. We used the definition of URiM as established by the AAMC prior to June 26, 2003. Per the current definition, URiM is not defined by fixed aggregations but rather includes “racial and ethnic populations that are underrepresented in the medical profession relative to their numbers in the general population.” 9 Therefore, we identified individuals belonging to the following self-identified racial and ethnic constructs as URiM: American Indian; Alaska Native; Black or African American, Hispanic, Latino, or of Spanish origin; Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander; and multiple race–Hispanic. 10 Non-URiM individuals were those who identify as Asian, White, multiple race–non-Hispanic, or other (because race and ethnicity was self-reported, any race reported that did not fall in the previous categories was in the “other” category). The AAMC classified the category of “multiple race–Hispanic” as individuals who have reported themselves as Hispanic and also belonging to 1 or more additional race or ethnicity categories. Conversely, the “multiple race–non-Hispanic” category includes individuals who have reported themselves as belonging to more than 1 race or ethnicity category, except for the Hispanic category. Any faculty missing race and ethnicity and gender data were excluded. We also used data for the US population from the revised US Census Bureau population estimates for the same time period (2000-2021) to compare the percentage increase in URiM faculty with the percentage increase in the same minority groups in the US population. 11 Because of the deidentified nature of the data, the institutional review board of the University of Arkansas for Medical Sciences determined that this study is not human participant research and does not require institutional review board approval or participant consent. The study adhered to the tenets of the Declaration of Helsinki 12 and followed the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) reporting guideline. To assess trends in URiM representation over the years examined, we compared faculty trends with the Cochran-Armitage tests by URiM status, rank, chair status, and self-identified gender from 2000 to 2021. We also evaluated the association between the proportions of women faculty and URIM faculty across varying specialties from 2000 and 2022 using an independent-sample t test. This data set lacks a comprehensive representation of gender identities beyond the binary constructs of woman and man. Intradepartmental ophthalmology comparisons were made using the years 2000 to 2021, while interdepartmental comparisons were made using the years 2000 and 2022, which represented the latest data available in the data sets across specialties. We conducted statistical analyses using Python software, version 3.9.7 (Anaconda Inc), Excel software, version 16.72 (Microsoft Corp), and Stata Release, version 17.0 (StataCorp LP). All P values were 2-sided, and results were deemed statistically significant at P < .05. Adjustments were not made for multiple comparisons because this was an exploratory analysis. Results Between 2000 and 2021 there were a total of 56 438 ophthalmology faculty members (37 511 men [66.5%] and 18 927 women [33.5%]) in Table 16 of the AAMC Faculty Roster. The number of ophthalmology faculty members increased from 1820 in 2000 to 3151 in 2021 ( P < .001) ( Figure 1 A). From 2000 to 2021, URiM female faculty and URiM male faculty were among the smallest groups represented, with 1733 (3.1%) and 1983 (3.5%) URiM female and male ophthalmologists, respectively. Comparatively, there was a total of 35 528 non-URiM male faculty (63.0%) and 17 194 non-URIM female faculty (30.5%). We found that the proportion of URiM male faculty remained stable from 3.5% (63 of 1820) in 2000 to 3.3% (104 of 3151) in 2021 ( P = .99), with a small increase in the proportion of URiM female faculty members from 2.2% (40 of 1820) in 2000 to 4.1% (129 of 3151) in 2021 (difference, 1.9% [95% CI, 0.9%-2.9%]; P < .001). There was a decrease in the proportion of non-URiM male faculty from 71.2% (1295 of 1820) in 2000 to 55.3% (1743 of 3151) in 2021 (difference, 15.8% [95% CI, 13.1%-18.6%]; P < .001), with an increase in non-URiM female faculty from 23.2% (422 of 1820) in 2000 to 37.3% (1175 of 3151) in 2021 (difference, 14.1% [95% CI, 11.5%-16.7%]; P < .001) ( Figure 1 B). Figure 1. Number and Proportion of Ophthalmology Department Faculty Members by Gender and Underrepresented in Medicine (URiM) Status, 2000-2021. Open in a new tab A, Comprehensive count of faculty members in the field of ophthalmology in the US, spanning the years 2000 to 2021. B, Distribution of ophthalmic department faculty members, categorized by their URiM status and gender. P < .05 indicates a statistical significance from Cochran-Armitage tests. Regarding the changes over the years by faculty rank in Table 19 of the AAMC Faculty Roster, we found that there was no change in the proportion of URiM faculty at the instructor level (from 0.6% [11 of 1819]) in 2000 to 0.7% [22 of 3165] in 2021; difference, 0.1% [95% CI, −0.4% to 0.6%]; P = .98). There was also no change in the proportion of URiM faculty at the assistant professor level (from 2.6% [47 of 1819] in 2000 to 3.3% [104 of 3165] in 2021; difference, 0.7% [95% CI, −0.3% to 1.7%]; P = .97) and professor level (from 1.0% [19 of 1819] in 2000 to 1.3% [40 of 3165] in 2021; difference, 0.2% [95% CI, −0.4% to 0.8%]; P ≥ .99). At the level of associate professor, there was a small increase in the proportion of URiM faculty from 1.0% (18 of 1819) in 2000 to 2.0% (62 of 3165) in 2021 (difference, 1.0% [95% CI, 0.3%-1.6%]; P < .001) ( Figure 2 A). We found that this increase at the associate professor level was primarily associated with increases in URiM female faculty from 33.3% (6 of 18) in 2000 to 46.8% (29 of 62) in 2021 ( P < .001). For the rank of instructor, the proportion of female faculty members remained stable from 5.4% (98 of 1819) in 2000 to 4.5% (143 of 3165) in 2021 (difference, 0.9% [95% CI, 0.1%-2.4%]; P = .89). For the rank of assistant professor, the proportion of female faculty members increased from 11.2% (203 of 1819) in 2000 to 19.7% (622 of 3165) in 2021 (difference, 8.5% [95% CI, 6.5%-10.5%]; P < .001). There was also an increase in the proportion of female faculty members at the level of associate professor (from 4.6% [83 of 1819] in 2000 to 8.6% [271 of 3165] in 2021; difference, 4.0% [95% CI, 2.6%-5.4%]; P < .001) and professor (from 2.8% [51 of 1819] in 2000 to 7.1% [223 of 3165] in 2021; difference, 4.3% [95% CI, 3.9%-6.1%]; P < .001) ( Figure 2 B). At the level of department chair, we found that there was a decrease in the proportion of non-URiM men (from 92.3% [96 of 104] in 2000 to 76.9% [83 of 108] in 2021; difference, 15.5% [95% CI, 5.9%-24.9%]; P < .001), with an increase in the proportion of non-URiM women from 1.0% (1 of 104) in 2000 to 15.7% (17 of 108) in 2021 (difference, 14.8% [95% CI, 7.7%-21.9%]; P < .001). We found that there was no change in the proportion of URiM men (from 5.8% [6 of 104] in 2000 to 5.6% [6 of 108] in 2021); difference, 0.2% [95% CI, −6.0% to 6.4%]; P ≥ .99) and URiM women (from 1.0% [1 of 104] in 2000 to 1.9% [2 of 108] in 2021; difference, 0.9% [95% CI, −2.3% to 4.1%]; P = .86) at the level of department chair ( Figure 2 C). Figure 2. Trends in the Representation of Female and Underrepresented in Medicine (URiM) Faculty Members in the Ophthalmology Department by Academic Rank and the Proportion of URiM Ophthalmic Department Chairs, 2000-2021. Open in a new tab A, Representation of URiM ophthalmology faculty members by academic ranks. B, Proportion of women faculty members in the ophthalmology department by different academic ranks. C, Breakdown of the proportion of ophthalmic department chairs by their URiM status and gender. P < .05 indicates a statistical significance from Cochran-Armitage tests. We examined the change in the proportion of URiM and female faculty members in ophthalmology departments compared with other specialties between the years 2000 and 2022 using the data in Table 16 of the AAMC Faculty Roster. The overall percentage of URiM ophthalmology faculty members increased from 5.7% (103 of 1820) in 2000 to 7.5% (230 of 3133) in 2022 (difference, 1.8% [95% CI, 0.4%-3.2%]; P = .01), which was among the lowest percentage changes compared with other specialties ( Figure 3 A). The only specialties with lower percentage changes in URiM faculty members were emergency medicine (1.3%; P = .17), radiology (1.5%; P < .001), and physical medicine and rehabilitation (1.7%; P = .15). The specialties with the greatest proportional increase in URiM faculty members were public health and preventive medicine (7.1%; P < .001), obstetrics and gynecology (4.6%; P < .001), and neurology (4.1%; P < .001). The overall percentage of female ophthalmology faculty members increased from 25.4% (462 of 1820) in 2000 to 41.8% (1309 of 3133) in 2022 (difference, 16.4% [95% CI, 14.0%-19.0%]; P < .001). This change represented the sixth largest increase, with obstetrics and gynecology (27.6%; P < .001), psychiatry (18.7%; P < .001), dermatology (18.4%; P < .001), pediatrics (17.6%; P < .001), and neurology (16.4%; P < .001) having larger percentage changes ( Figure 3 B). Specialties with the lowest percentage changes in female faculty members were radiology (6.3%; P < .001), physical medicine and rehabilitation (7.4%; P < .001), and anesthesiology (8.6%; P < .001). Figure 3. Change in Proportion of Underrepresented in Medicine (URiM) and Female Faculty Members in Clinical Departments, 2000-2022. Open in a new tab A, Change in the proportion of URiM faculty members across clinical departments from 2000 to 2022. B, Change in the proportion of female faculty members across clinical departments. The data include all the departments listed by the Association of American Medical Colleges Faculty Roster. Last, we examined the increase in ophthalmology URiM faculty members from 2000 to 2021, comparing it with the increase in the corresponding minority groups aged 18 years or older in the US population. Although these minority groups saw an increase in their US population share from 23.1% in 2000 to 30.1% in 2021, the increase in URiM ophthalmology faculty members was only one-fourth (0.25-fold) of this observed increase in the broader US minority population ( Figure 4 ). Figure 4. Comparative Increase in Underrepresented in Medicine (URiM) Faculty Members vs US Population Minority Groups, 2000-2021. Open in a new tab A, Percentage increase in URiM faculty members and minority groups in the US population from 2000 to 2021. B, Percentage change between URiM faculty and minority population across years. Discussion This study provides a longitudinal analysis of diversity trends in ophthalmology faculty membership over the past 20 years, with particular attention to the representation of URiM groups and women. We found that, between 2000 and 2021, the number of ophthalmology faculty members increased substantially, with the proportion of URiM female faculty members experiencing a small (<2%) increase, while the proportion of URiM male faculty members remained steady. Over the same period, the proportion of non-URiM male faculty members decreased, with a simultaneous increase in non-URiM female faculty members from 23.2% to 37.3%. We found that the increase in URiM faculty members in ophthalmology was only one-fourth of the increase observed in the overall US minority population, indicating a widening disparity between 2000 and 2021. Our findings reinforce earlier cross-sectional studies by Fairless et al 3 and Woreta et al, 4 which highlighted the lack of representation of URiM individuals within the field of ophthalmology. Building on these studies, our analysis adds a dimension by tracing the longitudinal trends in racial and ethnic diversity within ophthalmology faculties from 2000 to 2021. This temporal perspective not only underscores the extent of the diversity challenge but also provides a context to evaluate the pace and effectiveness of initiatives to increase URiM representation in ophthalmology over the years. In addition, our findings underscore the need for effective strategies to enhance diversity in ophthalmology, aligning with the current discourse on the importance of diversity for health care quality and equity. 13 , 14 , 15 In terms of gender disparity, our findings indicate a steady increase in female representation across all faculty ranks, in line with national trends in other specialties. 10 , 16 , 17 , 18 Despite this, there remains a gap in the representation of URiM women in ophthalmology, who continue to form the smallest percentage of ophthalmologists. This disparity, mirrored in academic medicine broadly, 1 requires dedicated efforts to address intersectional underrepresentation. We found a minimal increase in URiM representation among faculty ranks despite an increase in the demographic proportion of these groups within the US population. This lack of proportionate growth suggests a divergence between the evolving demographic profile of the country and the diversity within academic ophthalmology. This trend aligns with the findings of Xierali et al, 17 who identified a similar trend in dermatology. In our study, at different faculty ranks, there was a considerable increase in female representation over the 20-year period, especially at the professor level, with URiM faculty representation largely unchanged, except for a small increase at the associate professor level. On examining the data for department chairs, the representation of URiM men and women remains low, with only marginal changes over the years. This finding suggests a need for deliberate measures to promote diversity in leadership roles, particularly for URiM groups, in alignment with evidence correlating diverse leadership with improved organizational performance. 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 Furthermore, there may be differences in demographic data between academic and nonacademic ophthalmologists. First, academic institutions typically employ faculty members based on specific criteria, such as achievements, research experience, and teaching abilities. A comprehensive study conducted by Tanya et al 23 revealed a significant positive correlation between faculty appointment and the H-index (which evaluates the quality and quantity of academic work, which is dependent on the citations of a researcher’s work as well as the scientific age of the researcher), further emphasizing the importance of academic achievements. However, despite this correlation, another study examining academic rank and promotion highlighted a concerning disparity: female physicians encountered diminished chances of attaining the position of professor, even after accounting for variables such as publications and research productivity. 24 Second, an investigation conducted by Emami-Naeini et al 25 brought to light a gender pay gap among academic ophthalmologists, with female practitioners earning a mean of $50 300 less than their male counterparts. In addition, research by Patel et al 26 shed light on the lack of female representation in ophthalmology, particularly in terms of the percentage of women receiving private industry funding and the monetary value of the funding they receive. Further research is necessary to provide a comprehensive understanding of the disparities between academic and nonacademic ophthalmologists regarding underrepresentation, salary discrepancies, and positions held. When we compared ophthalmology with other specialties, we observed a relatively lower increase in URiM representation. This trend suggests that the strides made in other specialties to enhance diversity are yet to be mirrored in ophthalmology. Conversely, the increase in female representation in ophthalmology compares favorably with other specialties, highlighting progress made in this regard. Notwithstanding, the representation of URiM faculty in ophthalmology remains relatively low compared with the changes observed in the US population demographics. This finding suggests that the diversity efforts within academic ophthalmology have not kept pace with the demographic evolution of the country, suggesting a need for more effective strategies to address this discrepancy. In light of these findings, it becomes imperative to implement strategies aimed at fostering a more inclusive environment within ophthalmology. Evidence indicates that mentorship programs can play a pivotal role in enhancing diversity and fostering a more inclusive environment. 27 , 28 , 29 In addition, recruitment and retention strategies designed specifically to attract and support URiM individuals and women would likely prove beneficial. Moreover, the creation of more inclusive curricula, training programs, and departmental cultures would be key in attracting and retaining diverse talent. 30 The percentage of URiM match applicants and those matching into ophthalmology also showed substantial increases from 2016 to 2019. 31 Since 2000, the Rabb–Venable Excellence in Ophthalmology Program has implemented interventions to recruit URiM trainees into ophthalmology. A study conducted by Knight et al 32 in 2021 found that since 2000, the program has had 245 participants. Within this group, 73 were current medical students, 42 were ophthalmology residents or fellows, and 114 were practicing physicians. The match rate for eligible participants (2008-2020) was 75%, with only 6% selecting other specialties. Of those who completed their ophthalmology training, 31.5% were recruited to academic positions. In addition, newer programs, such as the Minority Ophthalmology Mentoring Program, which was established in 2018, have also made progress in enhancing URiM students’ exposure to the field of ophthalmology and their success in residency applications. 33 Through the program, participants are matched with ophthalmologist mentors, provided with educational resources for medical board examination preparation, and presented with various networking and research prospects. Both programs should serve as blueprints for future URiM mentorship initiatives. The implementation of such initiatives has the potential to further enhance diversity within the ophthalmology workforce, consequently fostering increased diversity in leadership roles, engagements, and publications. However, for such programs to be successful in the long term, academic ophthalmology departments should actively recruit diverse faculty members who can serve as role models and mentors for URiM trainees. Diverse faculty representation can enhance inclusivity and create a supportive environment that encourages URiM students to pursue academic careers. Limitations This study has several limitations. Our analysis does not inherently provide a definitive explanation for the underlying causes behind these trends. The dynamics associated with diversity in academia are complex and multifaceted. However, we can speculate how social determinants of health might be associated with the observed diversity trends. 34 Social determinants of health encompass various factors, such as educational attainment, income, social support, and physical environment, that can affect an individual’s health, opportunities, and trajectory in the field of medicine. 35 , 36 , 37 , 38 Furthermore, our study is intrinsically retrospective and relies on self-reported data. Although the AAMC Faculty Roster data are generally robust and widely used in health care disparities research, potential inaccuracies may exist due to the voluntary nature of reporting race and gender. The data collected from the AAMC Faculty Roster did not include a nonbinary gender option, nor did they specify whether this population of individuals was excluded from the data altogether. In addition, faculty members may have changed positions or institutions during the study period, which could influence the exact data on URiM and gender distribution. Thus, while the AAMC Faculty Roster may offer a reasonable estimate of the proportion of faculty members across key variables, it may not accurately reflect the actual number of faculty members. The scope of our study is confined to ophthalmology faculty in the US, which restricts the generalizability of the findings to international settings. Furthermore, our study focused primarily on gender and race and ethnicity. There are various other dimensions of diversity, including but not limited to socioeconomic background, sexual orientation, age, and disability status, that were not included in this study but are nonetheless essential to creating a fully inclusive medical faculty. Also, we report race constructs in the aggregate, which may obscure subtle differences between different racial groups. Despite these limitations, our study lays the groundwork for future research aimed at better understanding and promoting diversity within the field of ophthalmology and the broader landscape of academic medicine. Conclusions Our cross-sectional study underscores the need for a sustained focus on improving diversity within academic ophthalmology, particularly concerning representation of URiM individuals across all ranks. The benefits of a diverse medical workforce are well documented and should compel us to address this gap. 13 Research has shown that diversity in health care enhances cultural competency, fosters trust, resolves health disparities, and cultivates the future supply of talented individuals from diverse backgrounds. 39 , 40 By putting forth these findings, we hope to contribute to ongoing efforts toward fostering a more inclusive, diverse, and, consequently, more effective and equitable field of ophthalmology. Supplement. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (14.7KB, pdf) References 1. Yu PT, Parsa PV, Hassanein O, Rogers SO, Chang DC. Minorities struggle to advance in academic medicine: a 12-y review of diversity at the highest levels of America’s teaching institutions. J Surg Res. 2013;182(2):212-218. doi: 10.1016/j.jss.2012.06.049 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Elam AR, Tseng VL, Rodriguez TM, Mike EV, Warren AK, Coleman AL; American Academy of Ophthalmology Taskforce on Disparities in Eye Care . Disparities in vision health and eye care. Ophthalmology. 2022;129(10):e89-e113. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2022.07.010 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Fairless EA, Nwanyanwu KH, Forster SH, Teng CC. Ophthalmology departments remain among the least diverse clinical departments at United States medical schools. Ophthalmology. 2021;128(8):1129-1134. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2021.01.006 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Woreta FA, Gordon LK, Knight OJ, Randolph JD, Zebardast N, Pérez-González CE. Enhancing diversity in the ophthalmology workforce. Ophthalmology. 2022;129(10):e127-e136. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2022.06.033 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Fountain TR, Lee P, Parke DW II. Why ophthalmologists should care about disparities in vision health. Ophthalmology. 2022;129(10):1075-1076. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2022.06.036 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Capó H, Briceño CA. Impact of health literacy on eye health disparities. Ophthalmology. 2022;129(10):1083-1084. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2022.06.031 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Report: Faculty Roster: US Medical School Faculty. American Association of Medical Colleges (AAMC). Accessed September 3, 2023. https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/faculty-institutions/report/faculty-roster-us-medical-school-faculty 8. Faculty Roster: US Medical School Faculty. American Association of Medical Colleges (AAMC). Accessed September 3, 2023. https://www.aamc.org/data-