coronavirus outbreak: an experience from Hong Kong. Graefes Arch Clin Exp Ophthalmol 2020. 10.1007/s00417-020-04641-8 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ]',239,'2025-10-14 10:06:28','2025-10-14 10:06:28');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2814,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10472264','Sex and Racial and Ethnic Diversity Among Ophthalmology Subspecialty Fellowship Applicants.','Ali M, Menard M, Zafar S, Williams BK Jr, Knight OJ, Woreta FA.','2023','injustices indigent keratoconus categorial supspecialties fifths aguwa choinski rabb venable rabb venable buckle diversify diversify capped hoarding prematch filipino hmong aguwa srikumaran woreta marrast zallman woolhandler bartman indigent takeshita loren e2024583 schillinger warton dunlap jaramillo koppolu bruzoni s40615 elam e89 e113 aguwa aguwa onor aguwa srikumaran fairless nwanyanwu colby ortman demo p25 choinski lipsitz indes klawe refract jcrs ahluwalia adelman margo bounds bakri mahr erie ramulu akpek deobhakta klawe padovani claudio croteau chonka mmg rabb venable e298 e303 shaner sesi woreta zebardast e127 e136 brodin retinatoday asrs sfmatch 15f162acdff34991a280901f5b6b1f94 lagessa usnews','This cohort study examines match rates for females and underrepresented in medicine (URiM) applicants for ophthalmology subspecialty fellowships in 2021 and identifies factors associated with matching. Key Points Question What were the match rates for female applicants and underrepresented in medicine (URiM) applicants for ophthalmology subspecialty fellowships in 2021? Findings Among 537 applicants, the match rates for female and male applicants were similar but the match rate for URiM applicants was lower than that for non-URiM applicants. Females had the lowest representation in retina and glaucoma fellowships, and URiM applicants had the lowest representation in retina and cornea fellowships. Meaning Findings of this study suggest that sex and racial or ethnic workforce differences in ophthalmology subspecialty fellowships exist, and their identification may help programs and subspecialty societies improve diversity. Abstract Importance Physician-patient concordance in sex and race is associated with improved patient outcomes. Studies have explored diversity among ophthalmology residents and faculty, but to our knowledge, not among ophthalmology fellows. Objective To assess diversity by sex and race and ethnicity among fellowship applicants in ophthalmology subspecialties and compare match rates by applicants’ sex and underrepresented in medicine (URiM) status. Design, Setting, and Participants This cohort study examined ophthalmology subspecialty fellowship data from the 2021 San Francisco Match. Main Outcomes and Measures Applicant characteristics were stratified by sex and URiM status and compared using χ 2 , Mann-Whitney U , and median tests. For applicants who matched, the percentages of female and URiM applicants were compared among the ophthalmic subspecialties. A multivariable logistic regression model was used to assess the association of applicant characteristics with their match outcomes. Results Included in the sample were 537 candidates who applied for an ophthalmology fellowship using the 2021 San Francisco Match; 224 applicants (42.6%) were female, and 60 applicants (12.9%) had URiM status. Females and males had similar match rates (70.5% [n = 158] and 69.2% [n = 209], respectively; P = .74), but females had a higher median (IQR) US Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE) Step 2 Clinical Knowledge (CK) score (248 [240-258] vs 245 [234-254]; P = .01). The pediatric ophthalmology subspecialty had the highest percentage of female matched applicants (67.5%; 27 of 40 matched applicants), while the retina subspecialty had the highest percentage of males (68.9%; 84 of 122 matched applicants). URiM applicants had lower match rates (55.0%, n = 33) than non-URiM applicants (72.2%, n = 293; P = .007). The URiM applicants had lower median (IQR) scores on the USMLE Step 1 (238 [227-247]) compared with Asian applicants (246 [235-254]) and White applicants (243 [231-252]; P = .04). Additionally, URIM applicants submitted fewer median (IQR) applications (10 [1-23]) than Asian (21 [8-37]) and White (17 [8-32]; P = .001) applicants and completed fewer interviews (median [IQR], 2 [0-11]) than Asian (median [IQR], 12 [3-18]) and White applicants (median [IQR], 8 [1-14]; P = .001). Among matched fellows in each subspecialty, URiM applicants comprised 13.9% (n = 11) in glaucoma, 10% (n = 4) in pediatric ophthalmology, 7.3% (n = 6) in cornea, and 6.6% (n = 8) in retina. Conclusions and Relevance Ophthalmology subspecialty fellowship match rates were lower for URiM vs non-URiM applicants in 2021. Underrepresentation of females exists in the retina subspecialty, while racial and ethnic differences exist in all ophthalmology subspecialty fellowships examined. Monitoring trends in fellowship diversity over time should help inform where targeted efforts could improve diversity. Introduction Health care disparities are the manifestations of racial and social injustices on the health and well-being of patients, and efforts are needed to address them. An important component of such initiatives is to improve diversity in the health care workforce. 1 A diverse workforce has an impact on patient outcomes, as physicians from minoritized populations are more likely to care for minoritized, medically indigent, and sicker patients. 2 , 3 Moreover, physician-patient concordance in sex, gender, race, ethnicity, language, and culture has been shown to be associated with increased patient satisfaction and adherence to medical management. 4 , 5 , 6 , 7 , 8 Currently, disparities exist in the prevalence and treatment of numerous ocular conditions including cataract, glaucoma, amblyopia, refractive error, age-related macular degeneration, diabetic retinopathy, uveitis, keratoconus, and ocular trauma. 9 The term underrepresented in medicine (URiM) is defined as individuals who identify as Black, Hispanic, Native Hawaiian, Native American, or multiracial. Among the medical disciplines, ophthalmology has the second lowest number of URiM residents, higher only than integrated thoracic surgery. 10 Non-URiM applicants include Asian or White race. Moreover, the percentage of female residents in ophthalmology has been decreasing since 2011. 11 Ophthalmology has the third-lowest proportion of URiM faculty at US medical schools. 12 These trends are clearly discordant with the increasing proportion of females and URiM students graduating from US medical schools and the changing racial and ethnic demographics of the US population. 13 , 14 While studies have examined diversity among ophthalmology residents and faculty, there is a paucity of knowledge regarding the state of diversity in ophthalmology fellowships in the US. 11 The San Francisco (SF) Match began collecting data on the race, ethnicity, and sex of ophthalmology fellowship applicants in 2021. This study investigated the sex and racial and ethnic diversity among ophthalmology subspecialty fellowship applicants, assessed match rates by sex and race and ethnicity, and identified factors associated with successful matching. Methods Data Source Deidentified ophthalmology clinical fellowship match data provided by the SF Match from 2021 were analyzed. Fellowship applicants to all subspecialties were included except oculoplastic surgery (data for oculoplastic surgery are not readily available through SF Match). The following data were collected: applicant sex, self-identified race and ethnicity, country of medical school, Alpha Omega Alpha membership, USMLE (US Medical Licensing Examination) Step 1 and Step 2 Clinical Knowledge (CK) scores, number of applications submitted, number of interviews completed by the applicant, and applicant’s final match status. The Institutional Review Board of the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine deemed this cohort study exempt from review since deidentified data were used. The study adhered to the tenets of the Declaration of Helsinki 15 and followed the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) reporting guideline. Statistical Analysis Statistical analysis was performed using SPSS Statistics, version 26 (IBM). Descriptive statistics were used to summarize applicant characteristics stratified by sex and race and ethnicity. The groups were subsequently compared using χ 2 and Mann-Whitney U or median tests for categorial and continuous variables, respectively. The percentages of female and URiM applicants who successfully matched into each specialty were also calculated. A multivariable logistic regression model was used to assess the association of applicant characteristics with their match outcomes. The variables included in the model were country of medical school, sex, race and ethnicity, number of applications submitted, number of interviews completed, and USMLE Step 1 and Step 2 CK scores. Two-sided P values were calculated and were not adjusted for multiple analyses (except for the multivariable model). P < .05 was considered statistically significant. Results In 2021, 537 candidates applied for an ophthalmology subspecialty fellowship; 224 (42.6%) were females, and 302 (57.4%) were males ( Table 1 ). Match rates for female applicants (70.5%; n = 158) and male applicants (69.2%; n = 209) were similar ( P = .74). Female applicants had higher median (IQR) USMLE Step 2 CK scores (248 [240-258]) than their male counterparts (245 [234-254]; P = .01). There was no difference in the distribution of the country of medical school, Alpha Omega Alpha membership, median USMLE Step 1 score, median number of applications submitted, and median interviews completed between the 2 groups based on sex. Table 1. Characteristics of Applicants to Ophthalmology Subspecialty Fellowships in 2021 Stratified by Sex. Characteristic Female sex, No. (%) Male sex, No. (%) P value a Total applicants 224 (42.6) 302 (57.4) NA Country of medical school US 142 (45.1) 173 (54.9) .26 Canada 6 (30.0) 14 (70.0) International Medical Graduate 76 (39.8) 115 (60.2) Alpha Omega Alpha membership Yes 49 (57.0) 37 (43.0) .01 No 70 (38.9) 110 (61.1) Unknown 105 (40.4) 155 (59.6) USMLE Step 1 score, median (IQR) 243 (233-252) 244 (231-252) .76 USMLE Step 2 CK score, median (IQR) 248 (240-258) 245 (234-254) .01 Applications submitted, median (IQR) 17 (8-32) 17 (8-36) .37 Interviews completed, median (IQR) 9 (1-16) 8 (1-15) .23 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CK, Clinical Knowledge; NA, not applicable; USMLE, US Medical Licensing Examination. a χ 2 Test for comparing categorical variables or Mann-Whitney U test for comparing 2 quantitative variable categories. Female applicants constituted the majority (67.5%; 27 of 40) of matched applicants in pediatric ophthalmology. Male applicants constituted the majority (68.9%; 84 of 122) of the matched applicants in the retina subspecialty ( Figure , A). Table 2 summarizes the subspecialty of applicants who matched into ophthalmology fellowships stratified by sex. Among the 158 matched female applicants, cornea was the most common subspecialty (n = 41 [25.9%]), followed by retina (n = 38 [24.1%]), glaucoma (n = 32 [20.3%]), and pediatric ophthalmology (n = 27 [17.1%]) ( P = .001). Among the 209 matched male applicants, retina (n = 84 [40.2%]) was the most common subspecialty, followed by glaucoma (n = 44 [21.1%]), cornea (n = 39 [18.7%]), and pediatric ophthalmology (n = 12 [5.7%]) ( P < .001). Figure. Distribution of Matched Fellowship Applicants in Ophthalmology Subspecialties by Sex and Race and Ethnicity. Open in a new tab Other subspecialty includes uveitis, neuro-ophthalmology, global ophthalmology, ocular oncology, refractive/anterior segment, and combined fellowships. Underrepresented in medicine (URiM) is defined as individuals who identify as Black, Hispanic, Native Hawaiian, Native American, or multiracial. Table 2. Applicants Who Matched Into Ophthalmology Subspecialty Fellowships in 2021 Stratified by Sex and Race and Ethnicity. Characteristic Total, No. Applicants matched to subspecialty fellowships, No. (%) P value Cornea Glaucoma Pediatric ophthalmology Retina Other a Total applicants 375 b 82 (21.9) 79 (21.1) 40 (10.7) 122 (32.5) 52 (13.9) NA Sex Female 158 41 (25.9) 32 (20.3) 27 (17.1) 38 (24.1) 20 (12.7) .001 Male 209 39 (18.7) 44 (21.1) 12 (5.7) 84 (40.2) 30 (14.4) <.001 Unknown 8 2 (25) 3 (37.5) 1 (12.5) 0 2 (25.0) .34 Race and ethnicity Asian 108 23 (21.3) 25 (23.1) 7 (6.5) 41 (38.0) 12 (11.1) .28 White 185 41 (22.2) 32 (17.3) 25 (13.5) 59 (38.0) 28 (15.1) .22 Unknown 49 12 (24.5) 11 (22.4) 4 (8.2) 14 (28.6) 8 (16.3) .90 URiM c 33 6 (18.2) 11 (33.3) 4 (12.1) 8 (24.2) 4 (12.1) .45 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: NA, not applicable; URiM, underrepresented in medicine. a Other supspecialties include uveitis, neuro-ophthalmology, global ophthalmology, ocular oncology, refractive/anterior segment, and combined fellowships. b Among 537 applicants, 440 applicants submitted a rank list and 375 matched. c Defined as individuals who identify as Black, Hispanic, Native Hawaiian, Native American, or multiracial. Table 3 summarizes the characteristics of fellowship applicants stratified by race and ethnicity. Overall, URiM applicants accounted for 12.9% (n = 60) of the total applicants, with 55.0% (n = 33) matching. URiM applicants had lower match rates than non-URiM applicants (72.2%, n = 293) ( P = .007). Compared with White and Asian applicants, URiM applicants had lower median (IQR) USMLE Step 1 scores (238 [227-247]; 243 [231-252] for White applicants; 246 [235-254] for Asian applicants; P = .04), applied to fewer programs (median [IQR], 10 [1-23] programs; White applicants: 17 [8-32]; Asian applicants: 21 [8-37]; P = .001), and completed fewer interviews (median [IQR], 2 [0-11]; 8.0 [1-14] for White applicants; 12 [3-18] for Asian applicants; P = .001). Table 3. Characteristics of Applicants Who Applied to Ophthalmology Subspecialty Fellowships in 2021 Stratified by Race and Ethnicity. Characteristic Applicants, No. (%) Total a URiM b Asian White P value c Total applicants 537 60 (12.9) 139 (29.8) 267 (57.3) .005 Country of medical school US 326 26 (9.4) 101 (36.3) 151 (54.3) Canada 20 1 (5.9) 4 (23.5) 12 (70.6) .001 International Medical Graduate 191 33 (19.3) 34 (19.9) 104 (60.8) Alpha Omega Alpha membership Yes 86 9 (11.4) 22 (27.8) 48 (60.8) .57 No 182 26 (16.4) 46 (28.9) 87 (54.7) Unknown 269 25 (11.0) 71 (31.1) 132 (57.9) USMLE Step 1 score, median (IQR) 244 (232-252) 238 (227-247) 246 (235-254) 243 (231-252) .04 USMLE Step 2 CK score, median (IQR) 246 (235-256) 247 (238-257) 245 (236-255) 246 (235-257) .92 Applications submitted, median (IQR) 17 (8-34) 10 (1-23) 21 (8-37) 17 (8-32) .001 Interviews completed, median (IQR) 9 (1-16) 2 (0-11) 12 (3-18) 8 (1-14) .001 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CK, Clinical Knowledge; URiM, underrepresented in medicine; USMLE, US Medical Licensing Examination. a Total includes applicants whose race and ethnicity were unknown. b Defined as individuals who identify as Black, Hispanic, Native Hawaiian, Native American, or multiracial. c χ 2 Test for comparing categorical variables or median test for comparing 3 quantitative variable categories. Among the matched applicants in each subspecialty, URiM applicants comprised 13.9% (11 of 79) of applicants for glaucoma fellowships, 10% (4 of 40) of applicants for pediatric ophthalmology fellowships, 7.3% (6 of 82) applicants for cornea fellowships, and 6.6% (8 of 122) applicants for retina fellowships ( Figure , B). Table 2 summarizes the subspecialty of applicants who matched into ophthalmology fellowships stratified by their race and ethnicity. Among the 33 matched URiM applicants, glaucoma (n = 11 [33.3%]) was the most common subspecialty, followed by retina (n = 8 [24.2%]), cornea (n = 6 [18.2%]) and pediatric ophthalmology (n = 4 [12.1%]) ( P = .45). On multivariable analysis, completing more interviews (adjusted odds ratio, 1.41; 95% CI, 1.28-1.55) and higher USMLE Step 2 CK scores (adjusted odds ratio, 1.03; 95% CI, 1.01-1.06) were associated with higher odds of matching ( Table 4 ). Country of medical school, sex, race, ethnicity, number of applications submitted, and USMLE Step 1 score had no association with matching into an ophthalmology fellowship. Table 4. Multivariable Logistic Regression Analysis for Matching in Ophthalmology Subspecialty Fellowships . Characteristic Adjusted odds ratio (95% CI) Country of medical school US 1 [Reference] Canada 0.97 (0.09-10.99) International Medical Graduate 0.46 (0.20-1.04) Sex Male 1 [Reference] Female 0.72 (0.34-1.52) Race and ethnicity White 1 [Reference] Asian 1.00 (0.41-2.45) URiM a 1.50 (0.42-5.32) Applications submitted 1.00 (0.98-1.03) Interviews completed 1.41 (1.28-1.55) USMLE Step 1 score 0.99 (0.96-1.02) USMLE Step 2 CK score 1.03 (1.01- 1.06) Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CK, Clinical Knowledge; URIM, underrepresented in medicine; USMLE, US Medical Licensing Examination. a Defined as individuals who identify as Black, Hispanic, Native Hawaiian, Native American, or multiracial. Discussion This cohort study analyzed diversity among ophthalmology subspecialty fellowship applicants and found that females and URiM applicants comprised two-fifths and one-tenth of the ophthalmology fellowship applicants, respectively, in 2021. Among the matched applicants, females were underrepresented in the retina fellowship. Racial and ethnic differences existed in fellowships of all ophthalmic subspecialties examined. Aguwa et al 11 reported that the proportions of female ophthalmology residents and faculty in the US are 43.8% and 42.5%, respectively, which is consistent with the proportion of matched female ophthalmology fellows noted in this study. A lower number of female fellows has been found in other surgery subspecialties. Using 2018 data to assess the proportion of female fellows in various surgical fellowships, Choinski et al 16 reported that females made up 30% of vascular surgery fellows, 26% of thoracic surgery fellows, 39% of plastic surgery fellows (2017 data), 54% of pediatric surgery fellows, and 44% of colorectal surgery fellows. 16 The differences between female and male ophthalmologists extends after training, as practicing female ophthalmologists have been found to have lower surgical volume and received less compensation from Medicare compared with their male counterparts. 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 Ophthalmology fellowship programs and professional societies can play an instrumental role by devising policies that encourage and support female applicants and fellows, especially in subspecialties where they are underrepresented. Fellows identifying as URiM comprised 8.8% of matched ophthalmology fellowship applicants, which is also consistent with data from other surgery subspecialties. Based on 2018 data on surgical fellowships, URiM applicants comprised 12% of vascular surgery fellows, 7% of thoracic surgery fellows, 12% of plastic surgery fellows (2017 data), 15% of pediatric surgery fellows, and 9% of colorectal surgery fellows. 16 Literature on diversity in the field of ophthalmology has focused on residents and faculty. 10 , 11 A number of successful pathway programs have been implemented to increase match rates for ophthalmology residency candidates with URiM status. The Minority Ophthalmology Mentoring program sponsored by the American Academy of Ophthalmology and the Association of University Professors of Ophthalmology and the National Medical Association’s Rabb-Venable Excellence in Ophthalmology Research Program provide a range of opportunities to medical students with URiM status to pursue an ophthalmology residency. 23 , 24 Ophthalmology departments have also established similar programs for medical students. 25 , 26 While there are pathway programs to encourage URiM medical students in applying for ophthalmology residencies, there has been a dearth of programs for ophthalmology residents, fellows, and early career faculty in academia. In the past year, there has been an expansion of these programs. Such programs for ophthalmology fellowships of which we are aware include the Program in Lasting Leadership and Academic Representation by the Byers Eye Institute at Stanford University and the National Medical Association’s Rabb-Venable Excellence in Ophthalmology Research Program that aim to increase URiM representation in academia. 27 Retina, glaucoma, and cornea societies have programs for their respective subspecialties as well. The Vit-Buckle Society Fostering Careers of Underrepresented Stars program, the American Society of Retina Specialists’ Underrepresented in Medicine Mentoring Program, and the Retina Society Underrepresented in Medicine research mentoring programs aim to diversify the subspecialty of retina by supporting medical students and residents with URiM status. 28 , 29 The Mentoring URiM Rising Stars in Cornea program that was introduced by the Cornea Society and Eye Bank Association of America in 2022 has a similar objective. There is a need for other subspecialty societies to initiate similar programs to diversify their fields. In addition to the overall differences across all subspecialties in terms of race and ethnicity of the applicant, retina fellowships had the lowest proportion of matched fellows who were either female or had URiM status. Ophthalmology subspecialty societies may use the findings of this study as a baseline as they establish pathway programs similar to those that exist for ophthalmology residencies. Moreover, longitudinal studies are needed to assess the effectiveness of the existing pathway programs available for ophthalmology fellowships. The results of such studies may help tailor these programs to suit the needs of fellows in their subspecialty as well as guide other subspecialty societies. We found that URiM applicants submitted fewer applications and completed fewer interviews compared with their non-URiM counterparts. Costs of applications and traveling for interviews may be a limiting factor for certain applicants. The SF Match program has successfully implemented virtual interviews during the last 3 residency Match cycles (2021-2023). Moreover, they have capped the maximum number of interviews at 15 per applicant to discourage interview hoarding. The fellowship match could use a similar approach to provide equitable opportunity to all applicants. Limitations This study has several limitations. The SF Match began collecting data on the sex and race and ethnicity of applicants in 2021, allowing an assessment of a single snapshot in time but preventing an observation of trends in diversity over time or the consequences of the COVID-19 pandemic. However, the composition of female and URiM applicants in the recently released limited data for the 2022 fellowship match are similar to 2021, which is reassuring in terms of data validity. 30 However, future research with more longitudinal data can build on the findings of the current study. Subspecialty information was limited to matched applicants only, and thus if an applicant did not match, we did not know what subspecialty they applied to. Therefore, we could not compare match rates for each subspecialty. Another limitation is that these data include only applicants who applied via the SF Match. Neuro-ophthalmology and uveitis programs that do not currently use the SF Match, and applicants who accept offers for prematch or unmatched vacancies after the Match, also are not captured in our data. Additionally, data for applicants who matched in oculoplastic surgery could not be included because these fellowships have an early match in which participants apply in the second year of their ophthalmology training instead of their final year. Finally, our assessment was limited to objective parameters collected by the SF Match. The definition for URiM may be limiting because certain Asian groups, including Filipino, Hmong, or Vietnamese; first-generation college students 31 ; and those from disadvantaged socioeconomic backgrounds are not included. The role of surgical volume during residency, letters of recommendations, and interview performance could not be assessed using the current data. Conclusions This cohort study evaluated the proportions of female and URiM applicants applying and matching into various ophthalmology subspecialty fellowships. These results may provide valuable information to fellowship programs and subspecialty societies that are striving to increase representation and diversity. Supplement. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.5KB, pdf) References 1. Aguwa UT, Srikumaran D, Brown N, Woreta F. Improving racial diversity in the ophthalmology workforce: a call to action for leaders in ophthalmology. Am J Ophthalmol. 2021;223:306-307. doi: 10.1016/j.ajo.2020.10.007 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Marrast LM, Zallman L, Woolhandler S, Bor DH, McCormick D. Minority physicians’ role in the care of underserved patients: diversifying the physician workforce may be key in addressing health disparities. JAMA Intern Med. 2014;174(2):289-291. doi: 10.1001/jamainternmed.2013.12756 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Moy E, Bartman BA. Physician race and care of minority and medically indigent patients. JAMA. 1995;273(19):1515-1520. doi: 10.1001/jama.1995.03520430051038 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Takeshita J, Wang S, Loren AW, et al. Association of racial/ethnic and gender concordance between patients and physicians with patient experience ratings. JAMA Netw Open. 2020;3(11):e2024583. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2020.24583 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Adamson AS, Glass DA II, Suarez EA. Patient-provider race and sex concordance and the risk for medication primary nonadherence. J Am Acad Dermatol. 2017;76(6):1193-1195. doi: 10.1016/j.jaad.2017.01.039 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Fernandez A, Schillinger D, Warton EM, et al. Language barriers, physician-patient language con