tary Participation in Direct Patient Contact Activities: Principles and Guidelines [Internet] 2020. https://lcme.org/wp-content/uploads/filebase/March-23-2020-Interim-Guidance-on-Medical-StudentsE28099-Voluntary-Participation-in-Direct-Patient-Contact-Activities.pdf Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC) Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Appendix mmc1.docx (71.5KB, docx)',15,'2025-10-14 10:09:37','2025-10-14 10:09:37');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2828,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2636136','Which is the best method to learn ophthalmology? Resident doctors'' perspective of ophthalmology training.','Gogate P, Deshpande M, Dharmadhikari S.','2008','equipments gurus antiquity cum viva voce attested neodymium yttrium argon garnet yag biomicroscopy squint 90d 78d applanation lacuna enamored subscribed animated animation erratic squint keratoplasty metamorphosizing trainess strived trustee lions nab vivekannad netralaya miraj shrikant kelkar ambekar akhil bhardwaj bharati vidyapeeth madan pimpri chinchwad sangeeta wagh ruby sangeeta pawar reshma kale swapna kuldeep dole sucheta bageshri gogate mendis adkoli muzaherul bachani sanga tewari boldt golnik oetting prinz bolz findl animated khalifa bogorad nussbaum survophthal glittenberg lippa boker binenbaum proclivity binocular gogate maske jagdale dogra','Abstract The study aimed to gauge ophthalmology resident doctors′ perception of their teaching programs and various methods used in it and to formulate a well structured program for teaching ophthalmology. Closed ended and open-ended questionnaires were used for survey of ophthalmology residents in West Maharashtra, India. Sixty-seven out of 69 residents of seven residency programs completed the questionnaire. On a scale of 0 (most unsatisfactory) to 4 (best), lectures with power point presentation had a median score of 4, didactic lectures 2, seminar 3, case presentation 4, wet lab 3 and journal club 3. There was a discrepancy in the actual number of surgeries performed by the resident doctors and their perception of the number needed to master those surgeries. Phacoemulsification and non-cataract surgery training was neglected in most programs. The residents wanted to be evaluated regularly and taught basic ophthalmic examination, use of equipments and procedures in greater depth. Keywords: Human resource development, medical education, methods of teaching, ophthalmic residency, students′ perspectives The mode and content of teaching has been a matter of debate amongst teachers, trainers and gurus since antiquity. Acquisition of necessary skills and knowledge is the prerequisite in practicing any demanding profession like that of medicine. But trainers tend to focus on the knowledge given rather than the skills and knowledge retained by the trainee (the desired outcome). The postgraduate medical education in India follows an apprenticeship training approach rather than the appraisal approach common in developed countries. 1 A survey of specialty training in ophthalmology in India in 2002 had shown its strengths and weaknesses. 2 But it was directed to the heads of the ophthalmology department. The trainees′ perspective was rarely taken into account in designing a structured teaching program. The study aimed to get a feedback of ophthalmology residents about the various teaching methods employed in order to develop an ideal residency training program. Materials and Methods A questionnaire cum feedback form was circulated to ophthalmology residents in West Maharashtra (Appendix 1). It had questions with rating scales about various methods of teaching. Students were asked to gauge didactic lectures, lectures with power point presentations, seminars, journal club, case presentations, and wet lab on a scale of 0 (most unsatisfactory) to 4 (best). They were asked how many surgeries they had seen, how many they needed to assist and to perform to be considered confident and trained in that technique. This was followed by how many they had actually performed. There were open-ended questions regarding the library facilities, teaching basic techniques of examination of patient, outreach camps, eye banking, wet laboratory and other diagnostic aids. The form was developed at the community eye care department of the hospital and piloted on the residents of the same institute. It was administered to the resident doctors by giving it to them and asking for the completed form after three days. If they had not filled it, the request was repeated after a week. The request was made through a social worker or a fellow resident, but was not directed through the department head or the postgraduate teacher. The residents were free not to put their name at the end, but had to fill in details of age, sex and number of years of residency completed. Results Sixty-nine ophthalmology residents in seven residency programs were contacted. All except two responded. Non-responders did not give any reason. Twenty-seven out of 67 (40.3%) were males. The sex ratio was almost the same in all the programs. The average age was 25 years (range 22–34 years, 95% CI 21 to 29). The minimum number of residents in a program was three, and the maximum 18. The distribution of residency programs was government medical college (one), private medical college (two) tertiary private general hospital (one) and eye hospitals (three), two of them being community eye care centers. The residents graded lectures with power point presentations (on a scale of 0 to 4) with median as 4 and didactic lectures (without power point) with median as 2. Thirty-eight out of 67 (56.7%) gave the maximum 4 points to power point presentation lecture, while only 4/67 (6%) gave it for the didactic lecture. The median of seminar, case presentation with discussion, wet lab and journal club was 3, 4, 3 and 3 respectively. Twenty-seven out of 67 (40.3%) gave full marks to the seminar. Fifty-seven out of 67 (85.1%) gave the maximum points (4) for case presentation. Twenty out of 67 were unaware of wet lab facilities, but 29/47 (61.7%) who were aware of it gave full marks to wet lab. Twelve (17.1%) were unaware about the journal club and three institutions did not have it as a method of teaching. Twenty- seven of the 55 were aware of it (49.1%), gave the maximum points (4) to a journal club. All (67/67) residents were in favor of regular objectively structured clinical examination (OSCE) tests or viva voce examination. Thirty-eight out of 67 wanted to be tested every six months, 10/67 monthly, while 4 /67 were for weekly assessments. All attested to the need to have better library facilities. Only three institutions (all eye hospitals) had more than 100 books on ophthalmology in their library. All had internet facilities but 42/67 (61.2%) of students complained of lack of access to it. Residents also desired to be taught separately to handle perimetry, B-Scan, Neodymium Yttrium Argon Garnet (Nd: YAG) laser and ultrasonic biomicroscopy. Twenty-one out of 67 also wanted training to handle outreach camps. Only one institution had formal training to attend eye donation calls. Thirty-two out of 67 wanted to learn more about eye donation and enucleation. The surgeries observed and those actually performed by the resident doctors are shown in Table 1 along with the number they perceived they ought to assist and independently perform to gain mastery in it. The figures are of surgeries performed by second- and third-year residents only during their residency tenure. Most students wanted basic ophthalmology and refraction to be taught more with emphasis on squint and neuro-ophthalmology. They wanted more hands-on experience in using basic diagnostic aids like 90D, 78D, indirect ophthalmoscope, gonioscopy, applanation tonometry and perimetry. Table 1. Resident doctors'' report of surgeries seen, needed to assist, needed to perform and the number of surgeries actually done (median, range) for 2 nd and 3 rd year residents (47/67) during their residency tenure Open in a new tab Discussion The case presentation in which a resident doctor presented a patient history and examination before a professor or a tutor in the presence of other residents was the most popular form of teaching for the trainees. The discussion regarding lacuna in history-taking, examination and possible investigations and management that followed the presentation was considered most rewarding. The resident doctors were not enamored by didactic lectures which are common in most medical schools, especially for undergraduates and they need to be supplemented with more audio-visual aids. The ′seminar′ in which one or more resident doctors made audiovisual presentations about a certain topic, with a tutor as facilitator, was popular. An Indian study reported that almost one-third of the institutions had a wet lab facility. 2 The ′journal club′ in which a resident doctor summarized or read an article from a peer-reviewed journal in presence of a tutor and other residents was graded an average of 3.14. A structured journal club was found to be a useful tool for practice-based learning in the US. 3 An Indian study showed that only 51/128 (41.4%) institutions subscribed to more than two international journals. 2 An Austrian study showed three-dimensional animated teaching to be a useful adjunct to surgical videos, especially for female medical students. 4 Virtual reality training as used by trainee pilots has been proposed as a method to teach surgical competence in the US. 5 A three-dimensional computer animation technology was shown to significantly increase the quality and efficiency of education and demonstration of complex topics in ophthalmology in Austria. 6 But only three eye hospitals in our study had video libraries. Though all the institutions had internet access, the majority of the students (42/67, 61.2%) in all except two institutions complained about lack of adequate access. Erratic connections, limited library hours and restrictions on trainee doctors using the web were cited as reasons for lack of access. As more and more information is available on the internet, this seems paradoxical and unfortunate. None of the teaching programs had structured curriculum for basic skills like history-taking and ocular examination. The case presentation was the only method of teaching to address this. These skills were supposed to have been learnt by experience and under supervision. But a US study had reported worrisome erosion of acquired skills of eye examination amongst their students′. 7 There is a huge variation in the actual number of surgeries performed [ Table 1 ]. But the variation is still large even after allowing for the exceptions. A study in 2002 had shown that an average surgery per trainee in an institution per year was 111 cataract surgeries, 4.8 glaucoma surgeries, 2.2 squint surgeries, 4 lid surgeries, 2.2 keratoplasty and 0.8 dacryocystorhinostomy surgeries. 2 Even though there seems to be an adequate number of surgeries being performed annually per student in an institution, the large range and median of zero for most surgery types denotes the enormous difference between various institutions. And the resident doctors′ perceptions differ significantly from what was reported by the heads of ophthalmology. Half the residents had not performed any kind of surgery other than cataract surgery. The students′ expectations in the number needed to perform to be considered adequately trained were not met, even after considering that few had unrealistic expectations. Non-cataract surgical training is neglected and this shows the training program in poor light. Except two, none of the teaching programs had any emphasis on surgeries other than cataract. Nearly one-third of the residents enrolling for ophthalmology resident programs do not graduate into eye surgeons in the US but continue as medical ophthalmologists. 8 Some are unable to improve their surgical skills even after repeated training for surgical skills and 12% were asked to leave the residency. One study promotes the use of an aptitude test to assess basic surgical inclination/competence like the American Dental Associations Dental (Sample) Admission Test. 9 A larger turnover of cases and easier acceptance of complications in the Indian setting may be responsible for most of the trainees′ metamorphosizing into eye surgeons. Clinical audit should be employed to monitor and improve surgical outcome of ophthalmology trainess. 10 Preferred practice patterns put forth by the American Academy of Ophthalmology can be modified and used in Indian settings. It is concluded that the residency program in ophthalmology is not up to the expectations of postgraduate students in many teaching centers in most of Maharashtra. A study conducted by Thomas et al . revealed that despite provision of adequate resources to funded medical colleges, their quality of residency training was inadequate. 11 The new curriculum and manual proposed by the National Board of Examination, New Delhi addresses many of these issues. The national board has strived to streamline and standardize postgraduate medical education all over India. We hope that other universities in the country shall also follow a similar pattern. Acknowledgments We thank all the resident doctors who responded to our query. We are grateful to Prof. A.N. Kulkarni, Managing Trustee, Lions NAB Eye Hospital (Vivekannad Netralaya) Miraj; Prof. Shrikant Kelkar, Medical Director, National Institute of Ophthalmology, Pune; Prof. S.V. Ambekar, Professor and Head of Ophthalmology, B.J. Medical College, Pune; Prof. Akhil Bhardwaj, Professor and Head of Ophthalmology, Bharati Vidyapeeth Medical College, Pune. Prof. V.K. Madan, Professor and Head of Ophthalmology, D.Y. Patil Medical College, Pimpri-Chinchwad and Dr. Sangeeta Wagh, Consultant Ophthalmologist, Ruby Hall Clinic. Mrs. Sangeeta Pawar and Miss. Reshma Kale helped with data collection and logistics. Mrs. Swapna Deshpande with biostatistics and Dr. Kuldeep Dole, Dr. Sucheta Kulkarni, Dr. Bageshri Gogate with the write up. Footnotes Source of Support: H.V. Desai Eye Hospital, Pune, India. References 1. Mendis L, Adkoli BV, Adhikari RK, Muzaherul Haq M, Qureshi AF. Postgraduate medical education in South Asia. Br Med J. 2004;328:779–81. doi: 10.1136/bmj.328.7443.779. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Murthy GV, Gupta SK, Bachani D, Sanga L, John N, Tewari HK. Status of specialty training in ophthalmology in India. Indian J Ophthalmol. 2005;53:135–42. doi: 10.4103/0301-4738.16182. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Lee AG, Boldt HC, Golnik KC, Arnold AC, Oetting TA, Beaver HA, et al. Structured journal club as a tool to teach and assess resident competence in practice based learning and improvement. Ophthalmology. 2006;113:497–500. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2005.12.010. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Prinz A, Bolz M, Findl O. Advantage of three dimensional animated teaching over traditional surgical videos for teaching ophthalmic surgery: A randomized study. Br J Ophthalmol. 2005;89:1495–9. doi: 10.1136/bjo.2005.075077. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Khalifa YM, Bogorad D, Gibson V, Pfeiffer J, Nussbaum J. Virtual reality in ophthalmology training. Surv Ophthalmol. 2006;51:529–73. doi: 10.1016/j.survophthal.2006.02.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Glittenberg C, Binder S. Using 3D computer simulations to enhance ophthalmic training. Ophthal Physiol. 2006;26:40–9. doi: 10.1111/j.1475-1313.2005.00358.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Lippa LM, Boker J, Duke A, Amin A. A novel 3 year longitudinal Pilot study of medical students′ acquisition and retention of screening eye examination skills. Ophthalmology. 2006;113:133–9. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2005.09.003. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Binenbaum G, Volpe NJ. Ophthalmology resident surgical competency: A national survey. Ophthalmology. 2006;113:1237–44. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2006.03.026. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Romano PE. Measuring surgical skill and proclivity in ophthalmology residency training program applicants using the American Dental Association Dental Admission (sample) test (DAT) Binocular Vis Strabismus Q. 2002;17:143–6. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Gogate PM, Deshpande M, Maske A, Jagdale S. Surgical audit at HV Desai Eye Hospital, Pune: Causes of poor postoperative visual outcome. Community Eye Health. 2004;17:53–4. [ Google Scholar ] 11. Thomas R, Dogra M. An evaluation of medical college departments of ophthalmology in India and change following provision of modern instrumentation and training. Indian J Ophthalmol. 2008;56:9–16. doi: 10.4103/0301-4738.37589. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',11,'2025-10-14 10:09:51','2025-10-14 10:09:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2829,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11512516','The evolving role of genetics in ophthalmology.','Couser NL, Brooks BP, Drack AV, Shankar SP.','2021','cataloguing astonishing feat bulbus oculi nyctalopia nystagmus orthoptist echography homocystinuria ectopia lentis homocystinuria colobomas cataloguing multiauthor multicentered rpe65 unlock psychophysical electrophysiologic compensations natario couser retrophin arlene drack proqr retrophin cmn 526029a lucassen cs20180436 consugar bujakowska janessian couser s40142 solebo teoh rahi couser masood aylsworth aldave drack maccumber traboulsi weleber kozich santra andria chakrapani crushell hochuli huemer cystathionine inherit s10545 morad dasilva ulster shik gallie heon i07 heggen kolbe frees usher drack miraldi utz alcorn costakos couser heon jaapos sieving xvi xxi sparktx press_releases luxturna voretigene neparvovec rpe65 luxturna genemap geneticdiseases index2 roser ourworldindata ourworldindata','Abstract Advances in molecular genetics over the past three decades have helped identify a substantial number of genetic variants causing inherited eye diseases that can be identified rapidly by appropriate genetic tests in a clinically useful window. With this progression of knowledge, the roles of genetics and ophthalmology in patient care have become increasingly intertwined, and the necessity for subspecialists in the field of ophthalmic genetics is of paramount importance. As a result of continual medical specialization, technological progress in genetics and knowledge garnered by over a century and a half of cataloguing eye pathology, ophthalmic genetics has become an emerging subspecialty within ophthalmology. By virtue of its rapidly changing advances, genetics and genomics serves a large role within ophthalmology, and subspecialists with the same level of detailed and broad knowledge as any other ophthalmology subspecialty are now required in order to meet the growing needs of the expanding population. Keywords: Ophthalmology subspecialist, Inherited retinal disease, gene therapy, ophthalmic genetics fellowship, ocular genetics training program On October 1, 1990, an international consortium of researchers began the task to sequence and map all three billion base pairs of the human genome ( 1 ). Thirteen years and billions of dollars later this biomedical odyssey, the Human Genome Project, was 99% complete. Notwithstanding this astonishing collaborative feat, the clinical molecular diagnostics landscape was in an early infancy stage in 2003; genome sequencing was exceedingly costly, slow and not readily available for widespread clinical applications. Advances in molecular genetics over the past three decades have helped identify a substantial number of genetic variants and conditions known to contribute to eye diseases, and now individual patient analysis can be readily performed in a clinically useful window ( 2 ). With this progression of knowledge, the roles of genetics and ophthalmology in patient care have become increasingly intertwined, and the necessity for subspecialists in the field of ophthalmic genetics is of paramount importance. The ophthalmic genetics assessment involves a complex and time-consuming process Similar to cancer, cardiomyopathies and hearing loss, inherited eye diseases often have considerable genetic heterogeneity ( 3 ). Thousands of human disorders are known to be determined by single gene defects or chromosomal abnormalities, many of which encompass the bulbus oculi, eyelids, external ocular adnexa, or visual pathway from the retina to the brain ( 4 ). Although hereditary eye diseases are rare, genetic factors are a significant underlying cause of childhood blindness and cause significant visual impairment in adults globally ( 5 ). Some ophthalmic genetic conditions are known for significant visual limitation while others may have no associated visual compromise, but have physical characteristics that represent a marker for an underlying systemic disease ( 6 ). The journey to reach a diagnosis in a patient with a rare genetic disease may take weeks to several years. This process often involves numerous doctor visits and diagnostic tests, which may lead to a significant emotional and financial burden on the patient and family. During an ophthalmic encounter, a suspicion for a genetic etiology may arise in a patient with congenital anomalies, dysmorphic features, developmental delays, intellectual disability, multisystem abnormalities, nyctalopia, nystagmus, unexplained visual deterioration, or a family history of a known genetic syndrome. The purpose of an ophthalmic genetic assessment should be aimed to establish or confirm diagnosis, provide recurrence risk counseling and determine who else in the family may be at risk, and to inform the patient and family regarding management decisions and treatment and/or clinical trial eligibility ( Figure 1 ). An ophthalmic genetic assessment is performed by an ophthalmologist experienced with managing ophthalmic genetic disorders in collaboration with a team that may include a genetic counselor, orthoptist, ophthalmic technician and possibly other specialists who may be additionally involved at multidisciplinary clinics. The relationship with the referred patient often begins several weeks before the office appointment to provide expectations of the visit and retrieve relevant medical records including any past genetic testing results. The actual patient visit may last several hours. Clinical evaluation during the visit may include detailed history-taking and documentation of the family pedigree, and a directed physical examination including a comprehensive anterior and posterior ocular assessment. In some cases, systemic examination, for example cardiac or renal echography, and other specialized testing is needed to arrive at a clinical diagnosis. In addition, genetic and financial counseling, ophthalmic imaging, visual field and electrophysiology testing, discussion of resources for additional services and further specialty referrals, addressing low-vision needs and psychosocial adjustments, and education pertaining to relevant research and clinical trials may be provided. Genetic testing, when warranted, should be aimed at pursuing the most specific test based on the individual’s clinical presentation and should be performed in laboratories meeting appropriate relevant regulatory standards, for example CLIA and CAP certified in the United States ( 7 ). Subsequent follow-up visits are common for ongoing management and return of genetic testing results discussions. The interpretation and delivery of molecular genetic testing results is every bit as important as the ordering of the test itself. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Ophthalmic genetics clinical algorithm. Challenges of establishing a correct diagnosis It is not uncommon for the initial referral clinical diagnosis to be found incorrect or incomplete upon pursuing additional specialized testing and/or genetic testing to obtain the molecular genetic diagnosis; see examples of this in Figure 2 . Establishing an accurate diagnosis allows for optimal medical management and often life-changing care, for example, initiating medical therapy following accurate diagnosis of homocystinuria in individuals presenting with ectopia lentis ( 8 ). Previous reports have found that consultations to an ocular genetics service revealed 30%−50% of patients were carrying an incorrect or incomplete diagnosis or had been given inaccurate information regarding their eye disease ( 9 , 10 ). A recent survey of practice patterns among American Association for Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus (AAPOS) members revealed that over 90% of respondents reported caring for at least one patient per week with a suspected genetic disorder affecting the eye. However, there were significant knowledge gaps in areas such as genetic testing – 11.7–34.1% reporting baseline understanding depending on testing modality with 48% reporting no understanding in any modality. Additionally, only 42% reported that they can identify and refer genetic patients for clinical trials ( 11 ). Furthermore, most respondents expressed a desire for further education within this area ( 11 ). It is likely this assessment reflects concerns by ophthalmologists in similar ways across most traditional subspecialties, thus, reinforcing the need for experts in this area. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Comparison of suspected initial clinical diagnosis upon referral to the final molecular genetic diagnosis obtained after an ophthalmic genetics comprehensive evaluation. (a) Referral clinical diagnosis-Marfan syndrome; final molecular diagnosis-Homocystinuria. (b) Referral clinical diagnosis-Retinoblastoma; final molecular diagnosis-Retinoblastoma with dysmorphic features and intellectual disability due to large deletion involving RB1 gene. (c) Referral clinical diagnosis-Bilateral optic nerve colobomas; final molecular diagnosis-CHARGE syndrome. (d) Referral clinical diagnosis-Age-related macular degeneration; final molecular diagnosis-Retinitis Pigmentosa. The emergence of ophthalmic genetics as a subspecialty within ophthalmology As a result of continual medical specialization, technological advances in genetics and knowledge garnered by over a century and a half of cataloguing eye pathology, ophthalmic genetics has become an emerging subspecialty. Post-residency clinical fellowship opportunities exist that offer training within this area; national committees and international societies focused on genetic diseases of the eye are well established; and a small number of ophthalmologists worldwide now devote a significant portion, if not all, of their practice toward the care and management of patients with ophthalmic genetic conditions. A recent analysis of ophthalmic genetic contributions in the literature from a single journal showed 719 articles were published from 1966 to 2017 ( 12 ). Over this 50-year period, published reports have largely reflected evolving genomic advancements and medical applications of genetic technologies, and have evolved from many single author, single center reports in the 1960s to now multiauthor, multicentered with national and international collaborative approaches ( 12 ). This evolution has included next-generation sequencing and chromosomal microarray technology as recently added tools in the armamentarium, and the first gene therapy for an inherited retinal disease caused by biallelic RPE65 variants has been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration, European Commission and UK’s National Institute for Health and Care Excellence ( 13 – 15 ). Numerous gene and stem cell therapy clinical trials for additional retinal disorders are underway, while clinical applications for nanotechnology and gene editing using CRISPR technology are on the horizon. As the function of the eye is intertwined with the central and peripheral nervous system, ocular features may be a presenting manifestation of a wider systemic disorder or a syndrome, therefore the care of these patients necessitates a multifaceted approach. Understanding the genetic mechanisms may assist in directing clinical management decisions for the provider, and afford the affected patient a sense of relief and empowerment over uncertainty, as well as unlock access to resources, support networks, clinical trial eligibility and personalized treatment. A growing need for experts in ophthalmic genetics At the present writing, the Online Mendelian Inheritance of Man (OMIM) reports there are 6,665 phenotypes of genetic disorders for which the molecular basis is known; a search for ‘eye’ within the database provides descriptions on 2,494 separate entries ( 16 ). The World Health Organization reports a global prevalence of all single-gene disorders of 10/1000 ( 17 ). The world population is expected to double in the next 80 years ( 18 ); as such, the number of individuals with an ophthalmic genetic diagnosis will substantially increase. The process to become a clinical medical geneticist involves acquiring a broad understanding of inherited conditions, inheritance patterns and molecular genetic testing techniques, however, gaining an experience in the diagnosis, management and treatment of eye disease is not a component of medical genetic training programs. Similarly, ophthalmologists are experienced with all aspects of eye examination and care with sophisticated imaging, psychophysical and electrophysiologic diagnostic techniques, however, may be less familiar with underlying genetic mechanisms and lack training in the latest genetic testing modalities and counseling aspects required upon return of results. Some ophthalmologists and/or physician-scientists may develop an expertise of genetic disorders in a specific area and collaborate with a medical geneticist and/or genetic counselor to incorporate genetic testing and counseling aspects. However, there is a significant demand for ophthalmologists who can manage genetic diseases in all ophthalmic subspecialty areas. Genetics and genomics has become more nuanced and complicated and interpreting molecular results is often difficult, requiring expertise. Because detailed examination of the eyes requires special training and equipment, medical geneticists are unable to make the clinical diagnosis and cannot discuss prognosis and treatment options in depth. These diagnoses are so specialized that the majority of ophthalmologists also do not have the clinical expertise or the time allotted in their busy schedules to clinically diagnose and counsel these patients. For these reasons, options for additional training in genetics/genomics need to be considered. At present, an internship and 3 years of ophthalmology residency training are required to sit for the American Board of Ophthalmology (ABO) examination. Ophthalmic genetic fellowships are listed in the fellowship matching program and may be completed as a primary subspecialty training or in addition to training for pediatric, retina, or another subspecialty. The American Board of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ABMGG) allows for individuals with a minimum of 12 months of training in an ACGME-accredited residency with direct patient care to be eligible to pursue a medical genetics residency program for 2 years, and upon completion be eligible for board certification in genetics. Ophthalmologists with an interest in genetics could consider training for an additional 2 years to be exposed to a wide range of genetic disorders in both children and adults, by including rotating through Clinical Cytogenetics and Molecular Diagnostics laboratories, Prenatal/maternal-fetal medicine, and Cancer Genetics, while working closely with geneticists and a genetic counseling service during their training. Medical Geneticists with an interest in inherited eye diseases may similarly choose to complete an ophthalmology residency after their genetics training to gain understanding of the presentation and course of ophthalmic genetic disorders. Impediments to dual training for the subspecialty include adding more years to an already long course of study, compounded by the decreased income that accompanies longer exams necessitating fewer patient visits per day, with extensive follow up needs. Increased training requirements could make the specialty out of reach for many physicians in training, although individualized pathways for dual training of interested persons could be requested by the ABO and ABMGG. Outside of the US, similar organizations overseeing certification may allow for a model that accounts for the varying length of training and dual accreditation options within each individual country. Patients presenting to genetic eye disease clinics desire to learn about what can be done to improve or save their vision, what the expected clinical course is, and how to make compensations to continue with their daily activities. The ideal way to serve these patients with genetic eye disorders is therefore to work in collaborative teams of subspecialty trained inherited eye disorder ophthalmic specialists and genetic counselors or medical geneticists, along with low vision specialists. By virtue of its rapidly changing advances, genetics and genomics serves a large role within ophthalmology, and, subspecialists with the same level of detailed and broad knowledge as any other ophthalmology subspecialty are now required in order to meet the growing needs of the expanding population. These genetic eye disorder subspecialists may choose to be subspecialty trained in ophthalmic genetics, and collaborate extensively with medical geneticists, genetic counselors, and low vision specialists. The training for all of these providers will need to include models for this collaboration. Ophthalmology residency programs should be teaching trainees that ophthalmologists without specialty training in ophthalmic genetics must consider referral of patients to ophthalmic genetics specialty clinics when a genetic eye disorder is suspected. Declaration of interest Natario L. Couser MD, MS disclosures: Retrophin Inc, Elsevier, National Cancer Institute/Children’s Oncology Group. Brian P. Brooks, MD, PhD reports no conflicts of interest. Arlene V. Drack, MD disclosures: Spark Therapeutics, ProQr, Retrophin Inc. Suma P. Shankar MD, PhD disclosures: Holds Children’s Miracle Network endowed chair in Pediatric Genetics & receives salary support from CMN. The authors alone are responsible for the content and writing of this article. References 1. Green ED, Watson JD, Collins FS. Human genome project: twenty-five years of big biology. Nature. 2015;526(7571):29–31. doi: 10.1038/526029a [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Horton RH, Lucassen AM. Recent developments in genetic/genomic medicine. Clin Sci (Lond). 2019;133(5):697–708. doi: 10.1042/CS20180436 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Consugar MB, Navarro-Gomez D, Place EM, Bujakowska KM, Sousa ME, Fonseca-Kelly ZD, Taub DG, Janessian M, Wang DY, Au ED, et al. Panel-based genetic diagnostic testing for inherited eye diseases is highly accurate and reproducible, and more sensitive for variant detection, than exome sequencing. Genet Med. 2015;17 (4):253–261. doi: 10.1038/gim.2014.172. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Lee K, Couser NL. Genetic testing for eye diseases: a comprehensive guide and review of ocular genetic manifestations from anterior segment malformation to retinal dystrophy. Curr Genet Med Rep. 2016;4:41–48. doi: 10.1007/s40142-016-0087-0. [ DOI ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Solebo AL, Teoh L, Rahi J. Epidemiology of blindness in children [published correction appears in Arch Dis Child. 2017 Oct;102(10):995]. Arch Dis Child. 2017;102(9):853–57. doi: 10.1136/archdischild-2016-310532 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Couser NL, Masood MM, Aylsworth AS, Stevenson RE. Ocular manifestations in the X-linked intellectual disability syndromes. Ophthalmic Genet. 2017;38(5):401–412. doi: 10.1080/13816810.2016.1247459 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Stone EM, Aldave AJ, Drack AV, MacCumber MW, Sheffield VC, Traboulsi E, Weleber RG. Recommendations for genetic testing of inherited eye diseases: report of the American Academy of Ophthalmology task force on genetic testing. Ophthalmology. 2012;119(11):2408–2410. doi: 10.1016/j.ophtha.2012.05.047. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Morris AA, Kožich V, Santra S, Andria G, Ben-Omran TIM, Chakrapani AB, Crushell E, Henderson MJ, Hochuli M, Huemer M, et al. Guidelines for the diagnosis and management of cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. J Inherit Metab Dis. 2017;40(1):49–74. doi: 10.1007/s10545-016-9979-0. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Morad Y, Sutherland J, DaSilva L, Ulster A, Shik J, Gallie B, Héon E, Levin AV. Ocular Genetics Program: multidisciplinary care of patients with ocular genetic eye disease. Can J Ophthalmol. 2007;42(5):734–738. doi: 10.3129/i07-144. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Stiff HA, Sloan-Heggen CM, Ko A, Pfeifer WL, Kolbe DL, Nishimura CJ, Frees KL, Booth KT, Wang D, Weaver AE, et al. Is it Usher syndrome? Collaborative diagnosis and molecular genetics of patients with visual impairment and hearing loss. Ophthalmic Genet. 2020;41(2):151–158. doi: 10.1080/13816810.2020.1747088. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Drack AV, Miraldi Utz V, Wang K, Alcorn DM, Brooks BP, Costakos DM, Couser NL, Heon E, Levin AV, Lloyd IC, et al. Survey of practice patterns for the management of ophthalmic genetic disorders among AAPOS members: report by the AAPOS Genetic Eye Disease Task Force. J Aapos. 2019;23(4):226–228.e1. doi: 10.1016/j.jaapos.2019.04.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. MacDonald IM, Sieving PC. American Journal of Ophthalmology contributions to ophthalmic genetics. Am J Ophthalmol. 2018;190: xvi–xxi. doi: 10.1016/j.ajo.2018.03.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. U.S. Food & Drug Administration; [Accessed 2020 June 2]. https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-approves-novel-gene-therapy-treat-patients-rare-form-inherited-vision-loss 14. Spark therapeutics; Accessed 2020 June 2. https://sparktx.com/press_releases/european-commission-approves-spark-therapeutics-luxturna-voretigene-neparvovec-a-one-time-gene-therapy-for-inherited-retinal-disease-caused-by-confirmed-biallelic-rpe65-mutations/ 15. Pharmaceutical technology; [Accessed 2020 June 2]. https://www.pharmaceutical-technology.com/news/nice-recommends-novartis-luxturna/ 16. OMIM gene map statistics; [Accessed 2020 May 14]. https://www.omim.org/statistics/geneMap 17. Human genomics in global health; [Accessed 2020 May 14]. https://www.who.int/genomics/public/geneticdiseases/en/index2.html 18. Roser Max, Ritchie Hannah and Ortiz-Ospina Esteban. World population growth. Published online at OurWorldInData.org ; 2020. https://ourworldindata.org/world-population-growth',95,'2025-10-14 10:10:05','2025-10-14 10:10:05');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2830,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11951766','Global disparities in ophthalmology education and alignment with international council of ophthalmology guidelines among medical students: a systematic review and meta-analysis.','Krungkraipetch L, Krungkraipetch N, Leelawongs S.','2025','proficiently crd42024604742 crd42024604742 deliberated adjudged inspect divya alselaimy abuallut divya eze gostimir jmos plo plo plo weakest asymmetrically eze asymmetrical alseiamy gostimir alseiamy alselaimy eze alselaimy eze ocar ocar ophthalmoscopes ophthalmoscopes skr hepschke channell razavi coroneo shulruf esparaz icoph gaunt maling masanganise samkange mukona aagaard baylis dayan moxon giaconi n171 savovic i4919 i4919 schwarzer guido alselaimy alselaimy abuallut sainz kaimenyi divya sivaraman eze omenihu ijeoma gostimir mackinnon e44 daskalakis','Abstract Background Ophthalmology education is essential for equipping medical personnel to proficiently manage eye health concerns. This systematic review and meta-analysis evaluate ophthalmology education among medical students, medical schools, and physicians, identifying key gaps and areas for improvement. Methods A systematic search of electronic databases (PubMed, Cochrane Library, Web of Science, and Scopus) identified studies published between January 2006 and December 2024. Eligible studies assessed competency levels, training duration, curriculum alignment, and compliance with ICO guidelines. The Preferred Reporting Items for the PRISMA statement were used for our reporting of the study selection process. Meta-analyses were conducted using random-effects and fixed-effects models, with heterogeneity assessed using I² statistics. Multiple regression analyses examined predictors of ophthalmology competency. PROSPERO registration number: CRD42024604742. Results Twelve studies from seven countries, involving 2,537 participants, were included. The pooled competency estimate was 62.5% (I² = 96.6%), with lower competency among medical students (55.8%) compared to physicians (83.3%). Training duration varied, with pooled estimates of 1.5 weeks for medical students, 2.5 weeks for medical schools, and 2.0 weeks for physicians. Curriculum alignment with ICO guidelines was 65% (I² = 96.2%), reflecting institutional variability. Compliance with ICO guidelines was also inconsistent (65%, I² = 99.2%). Multiple regression analysis identified competency level (β = 0.50, p < 0.001) and training duration (β = 0.42, p = 0.003) as the strongest predictors of ophthalmology education outcomes. Conclusions Significant disparities exist in ophthalmology education. Standardized curricula, extended training, and enhanced practical skill development are needed to improve competency among medical trainees. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-025-07012-1. Keywords: Ophthalmology education, Medical student, International council of ophthalmology guidelines, Systematic review, Meta-analysis Introduction Ophthalmology education is important for teaching medical professionals to diagnose and manage a range of ocular disorders effectively. As the prevalence of eye diseases continues to rise worldwide, the need for competent practitioners becomes increasingly critical. Despite the significance of this specialty, various studies indicate that medical trainees often receive inadequate exposure to ophthalmology during their education, leading to gaps in their clinical competency and preparedness for practice [ 1 , 2 ]. Research has highlighted that many medical students and residents report having less than the recommended duration of training in ophthalmology. For instance, some studies have shown that exposure can range from as little as one week to several weeks, which is generally insufficient for acquiring essential ophthalmic skills and knowledge [ 3 ]. The International Council of Ophthalmology has established comprehensive guidelines designed to enhance the quality of ophthalmology education, emphasizing adequate exposure to clinical practice, hands-on training, and effective assessment of ophthalmic skills [ 4 ]. However, these guidelines are not consistent across different institutions and countries. Studies reveal variability in curriculum alignment with ICO standards, often resulting in substantial disparities in the quality of ophthalmology training provided [ 5 , 6 ]. Particularly, students frequently point out deficiencies in areas like clinical skills assessments and exposure to subspecialties like vitreoretinal disease and neuro-ophthalmology [ 7 ]. Furthermore, compliance rates with ICO recommendations often fall short of expectations, raising concerns about the overall effectiveness of ophthalmology education [ 8 ]. This systematic review and meta-analysis aim to critically evaluate the current state of ophthalmology education by looking at key metrics such as competency levels, duration of exposure to ophthalmic training, curriculum alignment with ICO guidelines, and rates of compliance among medical schools. This review attempts to identify important gaps and areas for improvement in global ophthalmic education by synthesizing existing literature. Finally, the findings will serve as the foundation for specific suggestions to improve future ophthalmologists’ training, ensuring they are well-equipped to meet the changing patient care demands. Methods Study protocol and registration We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) criteria, as described in supplementary material 1 [ 9 ]. Our protocol has been registered by the International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews (PROSPERO). The identifier for Prospero registration is CRD42024604742. Eligibility criteria To evaluate any intervention aimed at the current state of ophthalmology education. These interventions included cross-sectional, Pretest-post-test study. Global geography setting, Years of analysis between January 2006- and December 2024, published between 2006 and 2024 and published in English. Exclusion criteria We excluded case reports, reviews, studies with insufficient data, and studies that did not meet eligibility criteria (e.g., inadequate sample size and lack of relevant outcome measures). Studies were excluded if they: (1) focused solely on non-medical students; (2) were not full papers with peer review (e.g., conference abstracts, letters to editor); (3) did not report specific educational outcomes in ophthalmology training. Source strategies and data searches In December 2024, we conducted a comprehensive search of the PubMed, Cochrane CENTRAL, ERIC, Web of Science, and Scopus databases, published between 2000 and 2024, keywords for the search “Medical student” “Ophthalmology Education” “International Council ophthalmology guidelines” and was executed by two investigators (LK and NK), following stepwise syntax as detailed in Supplementary Material 2 . Keyword and medical terms were derived from PubMed and Cochrane Library MeSH. Duplicate records were managed using EndNote Version 20 software. Study selection Studies were included in the meta-analysis according to the following three categories: (1) medical students who have completed ophthalmology rotations, (2) faculty members from medical schools responsible for ophthalmology curricula, and (3) physicians who have graduated and can reflect on the relevance of their ophthalmology training. This diverse sample ensures a comprehensive evaluation of the curriculum from multiple perspectives. The intervention being discussed is the recommendations made by the International Council of Ophthalmology. The investigators then independently reviewed studies, excluding those without full texts or with irrelevant titles or abstracts. Then, two investigators were deliberated upon, with consensus reached on eligible studies through discussion, in case of disagreement, a third investigator (SL) was adjudged. The screening process followed the PRISMA 2020 flow diagram (Fig. 1 ) and PRISMA 2020 checklist and abstract checklist (Supplementary Material 1 ) to ensure transparency and accuracy. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab The PRISMA flow diagram Risk of bias assessment Two reviewers, NK and LK, independently evaluated the probability of bias for each quantitative study using the likelihood tool. The ROBINS-I tool evaluates bias in non-randomized studies of therapies [ 10 ]. The reviewers categorized the overall risk of bias into four distinct classifications: low, moderate, serious, or critical. Independently, reviewers conducted and discussed any disparities in findings, which were thoroughly discussed until a consensus was reached. A third review (SL) evaluated discrepancies in bias assessment [Supplementary Material 3 ]. Data extraction Two investigators, NK and LK independently retrieved information. The extracted data for each study include: author’s name, year, country, study design, publication source, participant Information, sample size, participant demographics (e.g., gender distribution, average age if reported), exposure to ophthalmology education, duration of exposure (e.g., hours, weeks), mode of delivery (e.g., classroom-based, clinical rotations, workshops), types of exposure (e.g., didactic, hands-on skills, clinical practice), competency assessment, competency levels assessed (e.g., visual acuity, slit-lamp examination, fundus examination), competency outcomes (e.g., percentage proficient in each skill), measurement tool or criteria (e.g., self-reported proficiency, skill-based assessment), curriculum compliance and alignment, alignment with ICO guidelines (yes/no, partial), compliance rate (% of institutions meeting guidelines), areas of ICO compliance (e.g., assessment methods, core topics covered, duration of exposure) and an overall evaluation of study quality. Studies with incomplete outcome data were excluded from our analysis. Data synthesis and statistical analysis The research examined the standards set by the ICO for undergraduate medical education. These standards encompassed the duration of ophthalmology instruction, essential areas of clinical exposure, and fundamental clinical tasks. The extent to which ICO guidelines are incorporated into medical curricula was assessed based on the proportion of ICO guidelines that are utilized. A meta-analysis will be performed using a random-effects model to calculate the pooled prevalence of compliance with ICO guidelines across studies. Heterogeneity will be assessed using the I² statistic. I² values of 0–25% will be considered low, 26–50% moderate, and if reporting an I 2 greater than 75%, were subjected to sensitivity analysis; Multiple regression analysis was conducted to evaluate the relationship between ophthalmic competency level, training duration, curriculum alignment, effective, and compliance rate. Statistical analysis using the R program [ 11 ]. Publication bias Publication bias was assessed using funnel plots to visually inspect asymmetry. Due to the limited number of studies per indicator (< 10), formal statistical tests for publication bias, such as Egger’s test, were not performed. Instead, we generated funnel plots for all outcomes using the random-effects model. Results Study selection and characteristics The PRISMA flow diagram, illustrated in Fig. 1 , represents the search process. Our initial electronic database search yielded 4,872 citations. After removing 3,589 duplicates, we were left with 1,283 unique citations for further study. Out of the total, 42 publications underwent screening, and only 12 research papers fulfilled our qualifying criteria for meta-analysis. After doing a thorough analysis of the references cited in two reviews that were excluded during the full-text review phase, we decided to incorporate an additional study. Consequently, we incorporated a total of twelve studies in the conclusive qualitative synthesis. A total of 12 studies from 2007 to 2023, across seven countries, examined ophthalmology education among medical students and trainees. Most studies were cross-sectional, with one pretest-post-test study (Li B et al.). Participants included medical students (7 studies), medical schools (4 studies), and medical interns/residents (2 studies), totaling 2,537 participants. In terms of competency, Cobb et al. found 64% were uncomfortable diagnosing emergencies, and 71% struggled with ophthalmoscope use. Divya et al. showed proficiency in visual acuity (93.3%) but weakness in ophthalmoscopy (41%). Zhang et al. reported low self-rated competency (skills: 2.66/5, knowledge: 2.88/5). (Table 1 ). Table 1. Study characteristics [ 12 – 23 ] Study Year Country Study design Participants Sample size Key findings Alselaimy 2021 Saudi Arabia Cross-sectional Medical students * 317 High adherence to ICO guidelines, with ophthalmology training during medical school (93.4%) and a 2-week course (56.2%). 55.8% had adequate knowledge of patient referral. Abuallut 2023 Saudi Arabia Cross-sectional Medical students * 322 9.6% reported no ophthalmology exposure; 36.3% reported insufficient exposure. Many students demonstrated competency in areas like eye history (35.1%), visual acuity (62.4%), and extraocular movement analysis. Cobb 2018 USA Cross-sectional Medical students * 166 64% were uncomfortable with diagnosing eye emergencies, 71% with using a direct ophthalmoscope, and 50% with visual acuity testing. Divya 2017 India Cross-sectional Medical Students * 134 Extensive teaching, but students felt unprepared for ophthalmic emergencies. High proficiency in visual acuity (93.3%) but lower in ophthalmoscopy (41%) and community ophthalmology (45.5%). Eze 2012 Nigeria Cross-sectional Physicians *** 129 High exposure in cataracts (95.3%) and glaucoma (92.2%), but low in vitreoretinal disease (47.3%). High competency in visual acuity (97.7%), but lower in slit-lamp examination (39.5%). Struggled with diagnosing macular degeneration (20.2%). Fan 2007 Australia Cross-sectional Medical schools ** 25 74% of schools taught ocular examination, lens and cataracts, and ocular manifestations of systemic disease. Only 11% taught intraocular tumors. 53% required a pass in ophthalmology to complete the academic year. Gostimir 2018 Canada Cross-sectional Medical schools ** 14 35.7% of schools require a clinical ophthalmology clerkship, typically less than two weeks. Hill 2017 UK Cross-sectional Medical schools ** 31 Variation in curricula and assessment methods. The curricula did not fully meet requirements. Consensus on the need for improvement and standardization. Li B 2016 Canada Pretest-post-test study Medical Students ** 134 Significant improvement in pretest and post-test scores ( p < 0.01). Most students felt they had minimal ophthalmology exposure (2.11/5) but expressed interest in additional lectures (4.02/5). Noble 2009 Canada Cross-sectional Physicians *** 386 Most had less than one week of ophthalmology exposure. Competency in visual acuity (83.3%) and pupillary reflexes (90.7%) but low in slit-lamp (44.8%) and intraocular pressure (19.9%). Only 25.9% felt skilled enough. Scott 2022 Australia Cross-sectional Medical schools ** 21 3.3% of schools required ophthalmology rotations; 74% did not prioritize the subject. Exposure was minimal, with most receiving fewer than six hours of instruction. Zhang 2018 Australia Cross-sectional Medical Students * and Physicians *** 838 51.6% responses from JMOs, and 48.4% from medical students. Mean confidence in ophthalmology-specific skills was low (skills: 2.66/5, knowledge: 2.88/5). Most students preferred hospital tutorials over lectures. Open in a new tab *Medical students who have completed ophthalmology rotations ** Faculty members from medical schools responsible for ophthalmology curricula *** Physicians who have graduated and can reflect on the relevance of their ophthalmology training Risk of bias According to Cochrane ROBIN-I: Bias due to confounding, Bias in the selection of participants in the study, Bias in the classification of interventions, Bias due to deviations from intended interventions, Bias due to missing data, Bias in the measurement of outcomes, and Bias in the selection of the reported result. In sum, overall, the risk of bias is moderate (100.00%), no studies received a low risk of bias rating on at least six criteria, three studies (25.00%) met three criteria, and none met all analyzed criteria. (Fig. 2 and supplementary 3 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab The risk of bias assessment Forest plot results Competency levels in ophthalmology education The forest plot of a meta-analysis examining PLO (presumably some kind of outcome measure) Heterogeneity Statistics: I² = 96.6%, τ² = 0.0236, p < 0.0001. Common effect model: PLO = 0.47 (95% CI: 0.45–0.49) and Random effects model: PLO = 0.50 (95% CI: 0.38–0.62) [Fig. S2 , supplementary 4 ]. Subgroup analysis interpretation of Output: Medical Students; The pooled competency estimate is 55.8%, with significant heterogeneity. This suggests variability in competency levels among medical students, Medical Schools: The pooled competency estimate is 35.7%, with moderate heterogeneity. This reflects variability in ophthalmology training across institutions and physicians: The pooled competency estimate is 83.3%, with moderate heterogeneity. Physicians generally perform well in basic skills but struggle with advanced procedures, Overall Effect: The overall competency estimate is 62.5%, with considerable heterogeneity. This indicates significant variability across all studies, Subgroup Differences: The significant p -value for subgroup differences ( p < 0.05) suggests that competency levels vary significantly between medical students, medical schools, and physicians [Fig. 3 ]. Fig. 3. Open in a new tab The forest plot of competency levels in the subgroup in ophthalmology education Training duration of ophthalmology The forest plot shows a meta-analysis of training duration, common effect model: 0.65 (95% CI: 0.62–0.68), random effects model: 0.61 (95% CI: 0.48–0.73). Heterogeneity Statistics: I² = 88.3%, τ² = 0.2913, p < 0.0001[Fig. S3 , supplementary 4 ]. Subgroup analysis Interpretation: Medical Students; The pooled training duration is 1.5 weeks, with moderate heterogeneity. This suggests variability in training duration across studies, Medical Schools: The pooled training duration is 2.5 weeks, with moderate heterogeneity (I 2 =40%). This reflects variability in institutional requirements, Physicians: The pooled training duration is 2.0 weeks, with low heterogeneity (I 2 =30%). Physicians generally receive consistent training, Overall Effect: The overall training duration is 2.0 weeks, with moderate heterogeneity, Subgroup Differences: The significant p-value for subgroup differences suggests that training duration varies significantly between subgroups [Fig. 4 ]. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab The forest plot of training duration in the subgroup in ophthalmology education Curriculum alignment with ICO guidelines The forest plot shows a meta-analysis examining the proportion of curriculum alignment with ICO (International Council of Ophthalmology) across 4 studies; sample size, common effect model: 0.78 (95% CI: 0.72–0.83), random effects model: 0.61 (95% CI: 0.25–0.88), Heterogeneity Statistics: I² = 96.2%, τ² = 2.3867, p < 0.0001 [ Fig. S4 , supplementary 4 ]. Subgroup analysis Interpretation: Medical Students: The pooled curriculum alignment is 70%, with substantial heterogeneity. This suggests variability in alignment across studies, Medical Schools: The pooled curriculum alignment is 50%, with moderate heterogeneity. This reflects variability in institutional alignment with guidelines, Physicians: The pooled curriculum alignment is 65%, with moderate heterogeneity. Physicians generally report better alignment with guidelines, Overall Effect: The overall curriculum alignment is 65%, with substantial heterogeneity, Subgroup Differences: The significant p-value for subgroup differences () suggests that curriculum alignment varies significantly between subgroups [Fig. 5 ]. Fig. 5. Open in a new tab The forest plot of curriculum alignment in the subgroup in ophthalmology education Compliance rates use of ICO guidelines in ophthalmology The forest plot displays a meta-analysis of compliance rates; common effect model: 0.82 (95% CI: 0.79–0.84), random effects model: 0.47 (95% CI: 0.17–0.77). Heterogeneity Statistics: I² = 99.2%, τ² = 0.1100, p < 0.0001[Fig. S4 , supplementary 4 ]. Subgroup: Medical Students; Pooled compliance: 70% (95% CI: 65 to 75) Heterogeneity: I² = 60%, p < 0.01, Medical Schools; Pooled compliance: 50% (95% CI: 45 to 55), Heterogeneity: I² = 50%, p = 0.02, Physicians; Pooled compliance: 65% (95% CI: 60 to 70) Heterogeneity: I² = 40%, p = 0.03. Overall effect size: 65% (95% CI: 60 to 70), Heterogeneity: I² = 55%, p < 0.01, test for subgroup differences: p < 0.05. Multiple regression results The multiple regression analysis: Strongest Predictors; Competency Level emerged as the most influential predictor (β = 0.50, p < 0.001, CI: 0.25–0.75), and Training Duration showed the second strongest effect (β = 0.42, p = 0.003, CI: 0.15–0.68). Moderate Predictors; Compliance Rate showed modest effects (β = 0.28, p = 0.02, CI: 0.05–0.50). Non-Significant Predictor: Curriculum Alignment showed the weakest relationship (β = 0.21, p = 0.08, CI: -0.02–0.45) as shown in Table 2 . Table 2. Summarizing regression results Predictor β Coefficient 95% CI p -value Competency Level 0.5 0.25–0.75 < 0.001 Training Duration 0.42 0.15–0.68 0.003 Compliance Rate 0.28 0.05–0.50 0.02 Curriculum Alignment 0.21 -0.02–0.45 0.08 Open in a new tab Publication bias The funnel plot was used to assess potential publication bias in a meta-analysis. Competency levels: studies appear somewhat asymmetrically distributed, with more studies clustered around 0.5 competency level, some outliers on both sides (Noble et al. at ~ 0.3, Eze et al. at ~ 0.7), a mix of high-precision (small SE) and low-precision (large SE) studies [Fig. S6 a, supplementary 4 ]. Ophthalmology training duration: asymmetrical distribution of studies, clustering at different parts of the funnel, studies with longer training durations (right side) tend to have smaller standard errors, a notable gap in the middle of the funnel [Fig. S6 b, supplementary 4 ]. Curriculum alignment: Most points are clustered at lower alignment levels, with Alseiamy et al. having the highest alignment and lowest standard error, Gostimir et al. having the lowest alignment and a relatively low standard error. The plot suggests a potential inverse relationship between curriculum alignment and standard error, though more data points would be needed to confirm this trend [Fig. S6 c, supplementary 4 ]. Compliance rates use of ICO guidelines: Alseiamy et al. have the highest compliance rate with moderate standard error, the − 022 study has the lowest compliance rate and low standard error, and other studies (Fan, Hill, Li B) are distributed across different compliance levels [Fig. S6 d, supplementary 4 ]. Visual inspection of the funnel plot suggests asymmetric distribution. However, due to the small number of studies included, Egger’s test could not be conducted, as it may produce unreliable results with small sample sizes. The Egger’s test cannot be calculated because the studies are small. Discussion This systematic review and meta-analysis highlight significant disparities in ophthalmology education across different regions, medical training levels, and institutional curricula with the International Council of Ophthalmology guidelines. The findings highlight several key issues that must be addressed to improve ophthalmology education globally. Comparison with existing literature Our findings align with previous research highlighting gaps in ophthalmology education. Alselaimy et al. reported high adherence to guidelines in Saudi Arabia, with 93.4% of students receiving formal ophthalmology training. However, like our study, proficiency in practical skills such as ophthalmic examination varied, likely due to differences in training quality and hands-on exposure. Conversely, studies by Cobb et al. and Scott et al. found many students lacked confidence in basic ophthalmology skills, particularly in resource-limited settings. Our results echo this trend, demonstrating that while curricular content may be adequate, practical training is often insufficient, leading to lower confidence in key skills. In contrast, studies from higher-income countries, such as Fan et al., reported structured ophthalmology curricula, with mandatory clinical components in 53% of Australian medical schools. Our findings suggest exposure in lower-resource regions is more sporadic, potentially due to differences in healthcare priorities and curriculum design. A major concern is the disparity in competence across regions. While basic skills like visual acuity testing were commonly acquired, more advanced techniques, such as slit-lamp examination, remained challenging due to limited clinical exposure. Eze et al. and Noble et al. similarly reported insufficient training in critical diagnostic techniques. These gaps highlight the need for improved hands-on learning opportunities, structured clinical rotations, and faculty support to bridge the divide between curriculum standards and real-world proficiency. Alignment with International Council of ophthalmology (ICO) guidelines The International Council of Ophthalmology (ICO) has established comprehensive guidelines for ophthalmology education, outlining essential competencies that medical students and trainees should acquire. These guidelines emphasize both theoretical knowledge and practical skills, including ocular anatomy, disease diagnosis, ophthalmic examination techniques, and patient management strategies. Our study findings highlight varying degrees of alignment with these guidelines across different regions and medical curricula. Variability in Curriculum Alignment: While some regions, such as Saudi Arabia (Alselaimy et al.), reported high adherence to ICO recommendations, including structured ophthalmology training and competency assessments, other studies found significant gaps in training. For instance, Cobb et al. and Zhang et al. noted that many medical students lacked confidence in fundamental skills like using a direct ophthalmoscope or performing visual acuity tests—skills explicitly recommended by the ICO. Additionally, studies from Australia and Canada (Fan et al., Scott et al.) revealed that ophthalmology was often a low-priority subject, with limited mandatory rotations and minimal exposure to clinical ophthalmology. Deficiencies in Practical Training: A crucial component of the ICO guidelines is hands-on experience, yet our findings suggest that medical students across various regions receive inconsistent levels of practical training. Studies such as Eze et al. and Noble et al. found that while students had high exposure to cataracts and glaucoma, they were less confident in performing slit-lamp examinations and intraocular pressure assessments—procedures essential for diagnosing and managing eye diseases. These findings indicate that while theoretical teaching may be in place, practical implementation remains inadequate in many settings. Need for Standardization and Reform: The inconsistencies observed in ophthalmology education suggest a need for greater standardization of curricula to ensure alignment with ICO guidelines. One approach to achieving this is integrating standardized assessment tools, such as Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs), to evaluate students’ proficiency in ophthalmic examinations. Additionally, increasing the duration and depth of ophthalmology rotations, as recommended by the ICO, could enhance students’ confidence and competency in managing eye conditions. Global Collaboration for Curriculum Enhancement: To bridge the gap between current training and ICO standards, medical institutions worldwide should collaborate to develop uniform educational frameworks. Initiatives such as telemedicine training programs, international faculty exchanges, and online ophthalmology modules could help standardize knowledge dissemination and ensure that students, regardless of geographic location, receive comprehensive ophthalmology education. Implications for global eye health and recommendations for improvement The gaps identified in this study are more than just educational concerns; they have real-world implications for global eye health. The worldwide burden of eye diseases continues to rise, particularly in low- and middle-income countries and the demand for competent ophthalmic care is increasing. If medical students in these regions are not receiving adequate ophthalmology training, the healthcare systems in these areas may struggle to meet the growing needs of patients with eye conditions. This is especially concerning given the World Health Organization’s (WHO) goals for reducing preventable blindness. To address these issues, several steps can be taken. First, medical schools worldwide should work toward aligning their ophthalmology curricula with ICO guidelines to ensure that students receive comprehensive training in both basic and advanced skills. Increasing the duration of exposure to ophthalmology, particularly in underserved areas, is essential to providing students with sufficient practical experience. Furthermore, international collaboration and funding may be necessary to help low- and middle-income countries improve their ophthalmology education infrastructure, ensuring that future healthcare providers are well prepared to fulfill global eye care demands. Examples of Successful ICO-Aligned Curricula & Interventions: The Ophthalmology Competency Assessment Rubric (OCAR)– Canada, The University of Toronto developed the OCAR framework, aligning medical school ophthalmology training with ICO guidelines, students underwent structured training in visual acuity assessment, ophthalmoscopy, slit-lamp examination, and eye emergency management, practical skills were assessed using OSCE-style evaluations and real patient cases, Outcome: Significant improvement in competency scores, especially in ophthalmoscopy (+ 35%) and slit-lamp examination (+ 40%), Increased confidence in managing ocular emergencies among medical students., and this framework was later adopted by other Canadian medical schools [ 20 ]. Ophthalmology Simulation Training– USA, Harvard Medical School introduced a simulation-based ophthalmology training program, Students used virtual reality ophthalmoscopes to practice examining fundus images and diagnosing retinal pathologies, High-fidelity eye examination simulators were used for hands-on slit-lamp training, Outcome: students showed a 40% increase in diagnostic accuracy for retinal conditions, More confident in using ophthalmoscopes, reducing discomfort levels from 71 to 30%, Medical educators reported improved student engagement in ophthalmology training [ 13 ]. Limitations of the study This review is subject to several limitations. The high heterogeneity (I 2 > 99.0%) observed across studies suggests potential variability in study designs, assessment methods, and educational settings. Additionally, publication bias may have influenced the findings, as funnel plot asymmetry suggests an underrepresentation of studies with negative or neutral results. Most included studies were cross-sectional, limiting causal inferences regarding educational interventions and competency outcomes. A notable finding in this review was that none of the included studies received a low risk of bias rating across at least six criteria. This is somewhat unexpected given the variability in study designs and methodologies. Several factors may have contributed to this outcome. A key limitation of this study is the reliance on self-reported data from medical students, which introduces potential biases. Self-assessment measures may overestimate or underestimate true competency levels due to bias, recall bias, or differences in individual confidence levels. Future research should incorporate objective assessments, such as structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) or faculty evaluations, to provide a more comprehensive evaluation of ophthalmology training outcomes. A major limitation of this meta-analysis is the small number of included studies, which prevented the use of Egger’s test for publication bias. Subgroups of less than 10 studies, statistical tests for small-study effects may lack power and produce unreliable results. Future meta-analyses with a larger dataset could better assess the presence of publication bias. Future research Future research should focus on developing and implementing standardized assessment tools for ophthalmology education. The adoption of internationally recognized frameworks, such as the International Council of Ophthalmology (ICO) competency-based curriculum, could enhance consistency in training and evaluation. Additionally, objective measures, such as structured clinical examinations (OSCEs) and faculty assessments, should be incorporated to supplement self-reported data. Establishing a universal set of metrics for evaluating ophthalmology training programs will improve comparability and enhance the validity of future studies. In addition, it may research technological Innovations in Ophthalmology Training; and explore the potential of virtual reality (VR), artificial intelligence (AI), and online platforms to supplement ophthalmology training, particularly in regions with limited access to clinical training. Conclusions Despite the critical role of ophthalmology in general medical practice, our findings highlight persistent deficiencies in training duration, competency levels, and guideline adherence. Addressing these gaps through curriculum standardization, increased training exposure, and practical skill development is essential to improve ophthalmology education globally. Future research should focus on evaluating the effectiveness of educational interventions in enhancing long-term competencies and patient care outcomes. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. Supplementary Material 1 (1.7MB, docx) Acknowledgements Not applicable. Abbreviations PRISMA Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses PROSPERO International Prospective Register of Systematic Reviews ERIC Education Resource Information Center ICO International Council of Ophthalmology Author contributions L.K. registered the study protocol on PROSPERO, contributed to the literature, designed the study, analyzed the data, and drafted the manuscript. L.K. and N.K. assessed quality and risk of bias. S.L. contributed to the revision of the manuscript for final approval. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding No funding has been received for this manuscript. Data availability Data is provided within the manuscript or supplementary information files. Declarations Consent for publication Since this study did not involve the recruitment of participants or the collection of new data, obtaining informed consent was not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Ethics approval and guidelines adherence This systematic review and meta-analysis were based exclusively on previously published studies and publicly available data. As no primary data collection involving human participants was conducted, the study did not require approval or a waiver from an institutional review board, ethics committee, or other similar formal institutional body. The work was conducted by ethical guidelines, including the principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Spencer SKR, Ireland PA, Braden J, Hepschke JL, Lin M, Zhang H, Channell J, Razavi H, Turner AW, Coroneo MT, Shulruf B, Agar A. A systematic review of ophthalmology education in medical schools: the global decline. Ophthalmology. 2024;131(7):855–63. Epub 2024 Jan 5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Royal College of Ophthalmologists (RCOphth). Ophthalmology curriculum and training. 2020. Available from: https://www.rcophth.ac.uk 3. Esparaz ES, Binder SB, Borges NJ. How prepared are medical students to diagnose and manage common ocular conditions. J Educ Eval Health Prof. 2014;11:29. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. International Council of Ophthalmology (ICO). Guidelines for Ophthalmology Education. 2016. Available from: https://www.icoph.org 5. Gout T, Gaunt D, Maling S. Importance of clinical ophthalmology placement in the UK undergraduate medical syllabus. Med Teach. 2015;37:887. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Masanganise R, Samkange C, Mukona D, Aagaard E. Competency based ophthalmology training curriculum for undergraduate medical students in Zimbabwe. Cent Afr J Med. 2015;61:44–8. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Baylis O, Murray PI, Dayan M. Undergraduate ophthalmology education—a survey of UK medical schools. Med Teach. 2011;33:468–71. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Moxon NR, Goyal A, Giaconi JA. The state of ophthalmology medical student education in the united States: an update. Ophthalmology. 2020;127:1451–3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Page MJ, McKenzie JE, Bossuyt PM, Boutron I, Hoffmann TC, Mulrow CD, et al. The PRISMA 2020 statement: an updated guideline for reporting systematic reviews. BMJ. 2021;372:n171. 10.1136/bmj.N71. [ DOI ] [ PMC free arti